4700:
** σποδός**, ἡ,
**wood-ashes, embers**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.375, “hymnus ad Mercurium” 238}}: generally, **ashes**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.140}}; ἐπ᾽ Ἰσμηνοῦ τε μαντεία σ., of **the ashes** of an altar, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 21, compare “Ant.” 1007, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.35; σ. ἱερὰ ἡ ἐκ τοῦ βωμοῦ “SIG” 1171.18 (from Lebena)}}; of the dead, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 435, 443 (both Lyric poetry), “Ch.” 687, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 758, etc.}}; ἀμφὶ σποδὸν κάρᾳ κεχύμεθα, in sign of mourning, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 827 (Lyric poetry), compare 1160; σποδὸς δὲ τἄλλα, Περικλέης, Κόδρος, Κίμων 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 25.12}}:—death by plunging into a room filled with ashes, αὐτήν μιν ῥῖψαι ἐς οἴκημα σποδοῦ πλέον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.100}}; as a Persian punishment (cf. Val. {{LXX+5th c.BC+|1st c.BC(?): Maximus Astrologus 9.2.6), εἰς τὴν σποδὸν ἐμβάλλεται 5th-6th c.BC: Ctesias Historicus “fragment” 29.48, compare 51, +others, LXX.2Mac.13.5f —There seems to be no difference in sense between σποδός and τέφρα: both occur in Trag., the latter alone in _Attic dialect_ Prose.}}
**II** **dust**, τῆς χαμᾶθεν σποδοῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.172}}; μετρεῖν τὴν σ., of labour in vain, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.26.17.}}
**III** **oxide** of certain metals, σ. Κυπρίη copper **oxide**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.104; σ. Ἰλλυριῶτις [prev. author] “Ulc.” 13; σ. χρυσῖτις [prev. author] “Mul.” 1.103, compare 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.75.}}
**IV** _metaphorically_, κυλίκων, πίθων σ., of a bibulous old woman, 'soaker', 'sponge', {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 6.291, 7.455 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus).}}
**V** **lava**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 6.2.3.}}

4701:
** σπορ-ά**, ἡ,
(σπείρω) **sowing** of seed, σπερμάτων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Amatores” 134e: hence _metaphorically_, μαθημάτων εἰς ψυχήν [prev. passage]}}
**b** of children, σπορᾶς γε μὴν ἐκ τῆσδε from this **origin**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 871}}; τοιοῦτος ὢν τοιῷδ᾽ ὀνειδίζεις σπορά; his **origin, birth.. **? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1298}}; **procreation**, παίδων, γένους, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 729c, 783a}}; τὴν Ῥωμύλου σ. **begetting**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.320b, compare 2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 103, 105.}}
**2** **seed-time, sowing-time**, ἀπὸ τῆς σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 8.2.6}}; δεκέτεσιν σποραῖσιν in the tenth **seed-time**, i.e. **year**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1152 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II** **seed**, {{NT|NT.1Pet.1.23, “PLeid.W.” 11.50}}; **field sown**, ξηρὰ σ. dry land, uncertain reading in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 637}}; σ. δράκοντος **ground sown** with the dragon's teeth, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1125 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II.b** of persons, **seed, offspring**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Tr.” 316, 420; γυναῖκα καὶ τέκνων.. σποράν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 598}}: _plural_, **young ones**, uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 56: generally, θῆλυς σ}}. the female **race**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hec.” 659}}; θήλεια σ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tr.” 503.}}

4702:
** σπόρῐμος**, ον
(ᾱ, ον {{Refs|“Hymnus ad Isim” 162}}; η, ον, see #1.3), (σπείρω) **sown, to be sown, fit for sowing**, αὖλαξ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.219}}; γῆ σ. **seed**-land, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.2.10, etc.}}; so ἡ σ (i.e. γῆ) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 6.5.4; σ. ἄρουραι “PGiss.” 28.4 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}; τὰ σ. the **corn-fields,** {{NT|NT.Matt.12.1, “Geoponica” 1.12.37}}; γένοιτο αὐτῷ τὰ σ. ἄσπορα Tab.Defix. in {{Refs 5th c.AD+|“BCH” 51.149 (Salamis 5th c.AD: Cypria)}}; πεδίων σπορίμαν βάσιν, i.e. solid corn-fields, {{Refs|“Hymnus ad Isim” [prev. cited]}}
**2** σ. σπέρμα **fit for sowing** or **bearing seed**, {{LXX|LXX.Gen.1.29.}}
**3** μὴν σ. a month **for sowing**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.378e: _metaphorically_, σπορίμη ἡμέρα}} day **of conception**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 50.31, 4th c.AD: Paulus Alexandrinus Astrologus “R.” 1; σπόριμος γένεσις “Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 8 (1).244.}}
**4** μέτρον σ. a measure **of seed-corn,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|Anthologia Graeca 6.95 (1st c.AD: Antiphilus Epigrammaticus).}}
**II** _active_, αἰδὼς σ.,= τὸ αἰδοῖον, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 3.396.}}

4703:
** σπόρος**, ὁ,
(σπείρω) **sowing**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.109, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 7.20, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 16.94, etc.}}; μετὰ τὸν σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 42d: _metaphorically_, ὁ γαμήλιος σ. καὶ ἄροτος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.144b: _plural_, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 7.5.5.}}
**2** **seed-time**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 17.4; ἀπὸ σπόρω 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 10.14.}}
**II** **seed**, λίνου σ. {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 7.65; σ. ἐν νειοῖσιν βάλλοντες 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.25, compare 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.413, NT.Mark.4.26, etc.}}
**II.2** **harvest, crop**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.53, “PGrenf.” 2.36.16 (1st c.BC), etc.}}; ὁ πρώϊμος σ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“OGI” 56.68 (Canopus, 3rd c.BC)}}; γᾶς σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 706 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II.3** **offspring**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 221,750.}}
**II.4** **semen genitale**, variant for{γονή} in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 2.54.}}

4704:
** σπουδάζω**,
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1143, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 471 (Lyric poetry)}}, etc.: _Attic dialectfuture_ **-άσομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 3e, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.213}}, later **-άσω** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.5.8, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.58, etc.}}: _aorist_ ἐσπούδασα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 507, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 114e}}: _perfect_ ἐσπούδακα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 694, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 236b, etc.}}:—_middle_, _future_ see above:—_passive_, _future_ σπουδασθήσομαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 4.13}}: _aorist_ ἐσπουδάσθην {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 17.3.15, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 24}}: _perfect_ ἐσπούδασμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 219e (see. below):}}
**I** _intransitive_,
**I** **to be busy, eager** to do a thing, with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1143, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 817, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 293a, etc.}}; σπούδασον ἐλθεῖν.. ταχέως **make haste.. **, {{NT|NT.2Tim.4.9}}; ὅτ᾽ ἐσπούδαζες ἄρχειν **wast eager** to rule, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 337 (troch.)}}: with _participle_, ἐσπ. διδάσκων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 9.1: frequently σ. περί τινος or τι, [prev. author] “Mem.” 1.3.8, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 330c, etc.; ὑπέρ τινος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.77; εἰς τὰ σά [prev. author] 21.195; πρός τι [prev. author] 22.76; ἐπί τισι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.3.11, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.2: with _dative_, σ. γάμῳ 5th c.AD: Aristaenetus Rhetor 2.3}}; σ. ὅπως.. **endeavour that.. **, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 43.12, “SIG” 312.10 (Samos, 4th c.BC): _absolutely_, ἐσπουδακυῖα}} **in haste, hurriedly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 572}}; ἐσπουδακώς **eagerly**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 562.}}
**I.b** with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, σπουδάσαντες τοῦτ᾽ αὐτοῖς παραγενέσθαι {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus2.141d, cf. NT.2Pet.1.15, “BGU” 1080.14 (3rd c.AD), etc.}}
**I.2** of persons, σ. πρός τινα **pay** him **serious attention**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 510c, etc.; εἴς τινα “Anthologia Graeca” 9.422 (1st c.AD: Apollonides Epigrammaticus)}}; σ. περί τινα **to be anxious** for his success, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.10, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.4.13, etc. (distinguished from πρός τινα by 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Sol.” 10); περί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 4.1; ὑπὲρ τῶν οἰκετῶν 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.17; ὑπέρ τινος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.213, etc.}}; σ. τινί **be a partisan** or **backer of**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Artoxerxes” 21, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.11.27, “PGiss.” 71.6 (2nd c.AD)}}; ἀπό τινος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 2.27.6.}}
**I.3** **to be serious** or **earnest**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 813}}; opposed to σκώπτειν καὶ κωμῳδεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pl.” 557; frequently in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus, σπουδάζει ταῦτα ἢ παίζε; “Grg.” 481b, etc.}}; ἐσπούδακας, ὅτι ἐπελαβόμην ἐρεσχηλῶν σ; **did you take it seriously**, that I..? {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Phdr.” 236b; σπουδάζοντα τοῖς πράγμασι τοῖς ὀνόμασι παίζειν 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Lysia” 14}}; ἐσπουδάκατον **they have worked hard**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 694}}; μάλα ἐσπουδακότι τῷ προσώπῳ with a very **grave** face, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 2.17.}}
**I.4** **study**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 1.7.2}}; **lecture, teach**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 1.21.5.}}
**II** _transitive_,
**II.1** with _accusative_ _of things_, **do** anything **hastily** or **earnestly, be earnest about**, τὸ αὑτοῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 507; τὰς περὶ τὸ μανθάνειν ἡδονάς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 114e, etc.}}; opposed to παρέργοις χρῆσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Euthd.” 273d, compare “Ti.” 21c; τὰ ἑαυτοῦ ἡδέα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 8.17; σ. τοῦτο, ὅπως.. [prev. author] “Eq.” 11.10:}} —_passive_, σπουδάζεταί τι **is zealously pursued**, πᾶν ὅ τι σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 761; σ. ἀγών 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 10.3}}; χρήματα μετὰ πολλῆς δαπάνης σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 485e}}; ἡ κωμῳδία διὰ τὸ μὴ σπουδάζεσθαι.. ἔλαθεν because it **was** not **taken up seriously**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1449b1}}; οὐ πάνυ σπουδάζεται ὑπ᾽ αὐτῶν **is** not much **valued**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Cont.” 11}}: especially frequently in _perfect_ _participle_, πᾶσα ἡ τοιαύτη σπουδὴ οὐκ ἐπὶ τούτοις ἐστὶν ἐσπουδασμένη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 219e}}; προοίμια θαυμαστῶς ἐσπουδασμένα elaborately **worked up**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 722e, compare 659e}}; so τὰ μάλιστα ἐσπ. σῖτα καὶ ποτά the **choicest**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.2.38}}; τὰ ἐσπ., of writing tablets, the **best quality**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.9.7 (also κλίνας καὶ δίφρους καὶ τὰ ἄλλα τὰ σπουδαζόμενα [prev. work] 5.3.2)}}; εἰ ταῦτ᾽ ἐσπουδασμένα ἐν γράμμασιν ἐτέθη if those pains were **seriously** bestowed on letters, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 344c; αἱ ἐσπουδασμέναι παιδιαί 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1371a3, compare “Pol.” 1336a34.}}
**II.2** _passive_, of persons, **to be treated with respect**, opposed to καταφρονεῖσθαι, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Rh.” 1380a26}}; **to be courted**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 17.3.15, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Themistocles” 5, 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 5.75}}; of women, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cimon” 4, “Art.” 26.}}
**II.2.b** in LXX, **trouble, disturb** any one, {{LXX|LXX.Job.22.10, 23.16.}}

4705:
** σπουδ-αῖος**, α, ον
(σπουδή) properly **in haste, quick**, σ. τοὺς πόδας {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 1.197, 3.149; τὸ σ. τῆς πορείας 2nd c.AD: Polyaenus Historicus 6.24}}:—but in ordinary use denoting **energy** or **earnestness**:
**I** of persons, **earnest, serious**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.16 (_superlative_),compare “Smp.” 8.3}}; **active, zealous**, in canvassing, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aemilius Paulus” 1.}}
**I.2** **good, excellent** in their several kinds, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.69}}; opposed to φαῦλος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 757a, 814e, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1448a2}}; **ἀκροαταὶ-ότεροι** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.271}}; σ. αὐλητής, opposed to ἄνθρωπος μοχθηρός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antisthenes Rhetor cited in 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 1; κιθαριστής 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1098a9; σκυτεύς [prev. author] “EE” 1219a22; ἀνδράποδον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.31; σ. τὴν τέχνην 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.2.2; περί τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 817a.}}
**I.3** in moral sense, **good**, opposed to πονηρός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.19}}; opposed to φαῦλος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 2.2.24, 4th-3rd c.BC: Zeno Citieus Stoicus 1.52; οἱ σ. Λακεδαιμονίων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.1.9; σ. τὰ ἤθη 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.4; τῷ ἀρετὴν ἔχειν σ. λέγεται 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Categoriae” 10b8, compare “EN” 1166a13, “Top.” 131b2}}; σπουδαῖον ={ἀγαθον}, {{Refs|[prev. author] “EN” 1136b8, 1137b4}}: generally, of all **virtuous** objects or qualities, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Metaphorical” 1021b24, 1051b24, “EN” 1151a27, +others}}
**II** of things, **worth serious attention, weighty**, χρῆμα, πρῆγμα, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 65, 70, 116, etc.}}; τὰ -έστερα (-έστατα) τῶν πρηγμάτων [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.8, 133 (variant{-ότερα, -ότατα}),cf. Iisoc. 2.50; ταῦθ᾽ ὑμῖν σπουδαιότατ᾽ ἐστίν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.4}}]; opposed to γελοῖος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 392 (Lyric poetry)}}; τί γελᾷς ἐπὶ σπουδαίοις πράγμασι; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 300e.}}
**II.2** **good** of its kind, **excellent**, σ. νομαί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.23}}; ἡ σπουδαιοτάτη [τῶν ταριχεύσεων] **the most elaborate, costliest**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.86, compare “PSI” 4.413.26 (3rd c.BC)}}; ἡ ἰσηγορίη χρῆμα σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.78}}; λόγος σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.132; μουσική 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 668b; τιμαί [prev. author] “R.” 519d; σπέρματα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.4.23}}; δῶρον οὐ σ. εἰς ὄψιν not **goodly** to look on, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 577}}; τραγῳδία σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1449b17; σ. ὑπόδημα [prev. author] “EE” 1219a22}}:— a play on senses {{Refs 4th c.BC+|11.1 and 11.2 in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1176b25, 1177a3}}; ironically, σ. χρῆμα a **fine** thing, {{Refs|“hymnus ad Mercurium” 332.}}
