4710:
** σπουδή**, ἡ,
(σπεύδω) **haste, speed**, σπουδὴν ἔχειν make **haste**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.89}}; σ. ἔσται τῆς ὁδοῦ **haste** on the journey, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.77; ὅκως ἂν αὐτὸν ὁρῶσι σπουδῆς ἔχοντα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.66}}; χωρίον.., οἷ σπουδὴν ἔχω whither **I am hastening**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 288; τοῖς μήτε σχολὴν μήτε σπουδὴν διαγινώσκουσιν 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 3.6}}; σπουδῇ **in haste**, see infr. IV; σὺν σπουδῇ ταχύς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1223}}; σὺν πάσῃ σ. with all **dispatch,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|POxy. 63.5 (2nd-3rd c.AD)}}; διὰ σπουδῆς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 212, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.2.28, etc.; ἐκ σπουδῆς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mirabilia” 837a15}}; μετὰ σ. {{NT+5th c.BC+|NT.Mark.6.25, compare 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 6.4.3, etc.; κατὰ σπουδήν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.93, 2.90, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.6.28, etc. (but this sense frequently runs into the next).}}
**II** **zeal, pains, trouble, effort**, ἄτερ σπουδῆς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.409}}; σῆς ὑπὸ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 585; σπουδῆς οὐκ ἀξία 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 778, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 604c, etc.}}; frequently in _dative_ σπουδῇ, **zealously**, see below{{Refs|IV. 3; so σὺν σπουδῇ [prev. author] “Lg.” 818c}}; σὺν πολλῇ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.8.4}}; ἐπὶ μεγάλης σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 192c}}; μετὰ πολλῆς σ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Chrm.” 175e}}; σπουδὴν ποιεῖσθαι **exert oneself, take pains, be eager**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.30}}; with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.4, 7.205; σ. πολλὴν ποιέεσθαι [prev. author] 6.107; πᾶσαν σ. ποιήσασθαι ὅπως.. “PHib.” 1.71.9 (3rd c.BC)}}; σ. ποιεῖσθαι περί τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 177c}}; περί τινα{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 179d; ἐπί τινι 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Salt.” 1: with _genitive_, σπουδήν τινος ποιήσασθαι}} make **much ado about.. **, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.4}}; σπουδαὶ λόγων κατατεινομένων **zeal for** the conflicting arguments, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 130 (_anapaest meter_); πρός τι 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.114; ἀμφὶ Κυράνας θέμεν σ. ἅπασαν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.276; ὅτου χάριν σ. ἔθου τήνδ᾽ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 13}}; σ. ἔχειν, with _infinitive_, **to be eager**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.120}}; with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 7.149; σ. ἔχειν τινός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 778, 1014; περί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Amatores” 136c; εἴς τι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 557; ὅπως τι γένηται 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 22; σ. γίγνεται περί τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 276e; σ. ἐστι περὶ πραγμάτων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.2; σπουδῆς καὶ βουλῆς τὰ πράγματα προσδεῖσθαι [prev. author] 9.46}}; ἡ σ. τῆς ἀπίξιος my **zeal** in coming, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.49, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 257}}; ὅπλων σπουδῇ **with great attention to** the arms, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.31, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 855d: _plural_, ἐπιμέλειαι καὶ σ. πλήθους γεννημάτων}} **eagerness for.. **, {{Refs|[prev. work] 740d}}; **zealous exertions**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1061 (Lyric poetry), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1370a12.}}
**II.b** in a religious sense, **zeal**, πρὸς τὴν θεάν {{NT+2nd c.BC+|“Inscription Magn.” 85.12 (2nd c.BC),cf. NT.Rom.12.11}}; ἐνδείκνυσθαι σ. {{NT|NT.Heb.6.11.}}
**II.2** **esteem, regard** for a person, διὰ τὴν ἐμὴν σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.41; πάνυ πολλῆς σ. ἄξιος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 1.6}}; **good will, good offices**, σ. ὑπέρ τινος {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.2Cor.8.16, compare “PTeb.” 314.9 (2nd c.AD)}}; **support** in political life, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Crassus” 7}}: _plural_, **party feelings** or **attachments, rivalries**, σ. ἰσχυραὶ φίλων περί τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.5; κατὰ σπουδάς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1370, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 3.8}}; σπουδαὶ ἐρώτων erotic **enthusiasms**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 632a.}}
**II.3** **disputation**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 4.27, 34 (in _plural_).}}
**III** **earnestness**, σ. ἔχειν, ποιεῖσθαι,= σπουδάζειν, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 901, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 522; σπουδῆς μὲν μεστοί, γέλωτος δὲ ἐνδεέστεροι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 1.13, cf. NT.2Cor.7.11, etc.: frequently with a _preposition_, in adverb sense, ἀπὸ σπουδῆς ἀγορεύεις}} **in earnest, seriously**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.359, 12.233}}; μετὰ σπουδῆς, opposed to ἐν παιδιαῖς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 1.1}}; μετά τε παιδιᾶς καὶ μετὰ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 887d; οὐ σπουδῆς χάριν ἀλλὰ παιδιᾶς ἕνεκα [prev. author] “Plt.” 288c, compare “Smp.” 197e; καὶ χωρὶς σπουδῆς καὶ μετὰ σπουδῆς ἐπαινεῖν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1366a29.}}
**III.2** **object of attention, serious engagement** or **pursuit**, σπουδὴν ἐπ᾽ ἄλλην Ἡρακλῆς ὁρμώμενος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1199: _plural_, ἔν τε παιδιαῖς καὶ ἐν σπουδαῖς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 647d, compare 732d, +others}}
**IV** σπουδῇ as _adverb_, **in haste, hastily**, προερέσσαμεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.279; ἀνάβαινε 15.209; στρατιὴν ἄγειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.1, compare 89}}; _Doric dialect_, σπουδᾷ ἐξελθοῦσα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 42(1).121.21 (Epid., 4th c.BC)}}; frequently in _Attic dialect_, σ. πάνυ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.89, etc.; σπουδῇ ποδός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 216.}}
**IV.2** **with great exertion and difficulty**, and so, **hardly, scarcely**, σπουδῇ ἕζετο λαός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.99, compare 5.893, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.297; σ. παρπεπιθόντες 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.37, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.119.}}
**IV.3** **earnestly, seriously, urgently**, τί με καλεῖς σπουδ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 849; σπουδῇ ἀκούειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 388d; σ. χαριεντίζεσθαι [prev. author] “Ap.” 24c}}; πάνυ σ. **attentively**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phd.” 98b}}; πολλῇ σ. very **busily**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.88, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 791, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.12, etc.; πάσῃ σ. μανθάνειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 952a, etc.}}

4711:
** σπῠρίς**, ίδος, ;
also **σφυρίς**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 78}} (variant{σπυρίς}), {{NT+3rd c.BC+|“IG” 11 (2). 287 A 43 (Delos, 3rd c.BC), “PCair.Zen.” 754.15 (3rd c.BC), “PTeb.” 796.7 (2nd c.BC), “IG” 12(5).663.15, +others (Syros, 2nd c.AD), “Sammelb.” 4425 iii 9 (2nd c.AD), variant in NT.Matt.15.37, +others}}:—**large basket, creel**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.16, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1005, “Frr.” 415,545, 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 34, NT.Matt.15.37, etc.; σ. σίτων “PTeb.” [same place]}}; used for transport of money, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 112v18 (2nd c.BC)}}; ταῖς φιάλαις {{Refs|“IG” 11(2) [prev. cited]}}
**2** used to translate the Latin _sportula_, σπυρίσι δειπνίσαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 4.10.21}}; τὸ ἀπὸ σπυρίδος δεῖπνον, **cena e sportula**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 8.365a; σφυρίδος δηνάρια πέντε “IG” 12(5) [same place]}}

4712:
** στάδιος** [ᾰ], α, ον,
(ἵστημι) **standing fast and firm**, σ. ὑσμίνη **close** fight, **fought hand to hand**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.314, 713, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.38}}; ἐνὶ σταδίῃ (i.e. ὑσμίνῃ) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.241, compare 13.514; ἡ σ. μάχη 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 6.273f}}, compare σταδαῖο; [πῖδαξ] σταδίη μένει, of a spring from which no water flows, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 4.326.}}
**2** **firm, fixed**, θάλαμοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 5.13: τὸ σ}}. **immobility**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 39.43.}}
**3** **standing upright** or **straight**, σ. χιτών,= ὀρθοστάδιον, an ungirt tunic **hanging in straight plaits**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 59}}, compare στατό; θώραξ σ. a **stiff** breastplate, plate-armour, opposed to στρεπτός or ἁλυσιδωτός, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.1226 (see. Scholia).}}
**II** (ἵστημι A.IV) **weighed**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 402 (στήδην conjecture Bentley).}}

4714:
** στάσις** [ᾰ], εως, ἡ,
(ἵστημι) **placing, setting**, (i.e. δικτύων) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 2.8, 9.16; τῶν κλιμάκων 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.60.7}}; **erection** of a statue, εἰκόνος {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 7.411.34 (Oropus, 2nd c.BC); στήλης [prev. work]22.654.59 (3rd c.BC), 11(4).1023 (Delos, 3rd c.BC).}}
**2** **standing stone, pillar**, {{LXX|LXX.Judg.9.6.}}
**3** **erection, building,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|PPetr. 3p.139 (3rd c.BC, _plural_)}}; ={ἐργαστήριον}, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus; so perhaps in “BGU” 1122.18, 21 (1st c.BC).}}
**II** (ἵστημι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus IV)}} **weighing**, αὕτη 'στὶ λοιπὴ σφῷν στάσις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1401; βολίμου “SIG” 241 A 28 (Delph., 4th c.BC)}}; στάσις μισθοῦ the **paying** of the doctor's fee, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “παραγγελίαι” 4; ἀπὸ τᾶν κοινᾶν ποθόδων.. ἐπιλυθῆμεν τοὺς ἐρρυτιασμένους στάσι “IG” 42(1).77.13 (Troezen, 2nd c.BC).}}
**B** (ἵσταμαι) **standing, stature**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 36 (marginalM βάσιν)}}; **standing still, stationariness**, defined as ἀπόφασις τοῦ ἰέναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 426d; opposed to φορά, κίνησις, [prev. work] 437a, 438c, “Sph.” 250a, 251d, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1025b21, +others}}; **rest**, as a category of the intelligible, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.2.8}}; opposed to ἠρεμία, {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.3.27}}; ὀμμάτων στάσιες **fixed stare**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Acut.” (“Sp.”)}}; σ. ὤτων **pricking** of the ears, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 5.61}}; σ. τῆς γαστρός **constipation**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus (?) 13.6}}; [τοῦ αἵματος] **sluggishness**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Acut.” (“Sp.”)}}; τοῦ ἀέρος,= νηνεμία, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Ventis” 18, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 9.908.}}
**B.2** **the place in which one stands** or **should stand, position, posture, station**, ἔχοντες σ. ταύτην ἐς τὴν ἔστημεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.21}}; λέβης.. φυλάσσων τὴν ὑπὲρ πυρὸς σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 1}}; ἰδέσθαι.., τίν᾽ ἔχει σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 308 (_anapaest meter_), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 954; τὴν Ἰνοῦς σ. ἑστάναι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 925}}; τῆς αὐτῆς ἠξιοῦτο σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.272}}; σ. ἵππων,= ἱππόστασις, σταθμός, **stable, stall**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 442; ὄνων ἵππων τε στάσεις 4th c.BC: Ephippus Comicus 18; τῆς σ. παρασύρων.. τὰς δρῦς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 527}}; κατὰ τὴν σ. δὴ στάντες standing each in his **place**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antidotus Comicus 2}}; of military formation, κατάπυκνος σ. close **order**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 5.1}}; **row**, ἀμπέλων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Tab.Heracl.” 2.77, +others, compare “BGU” 1122.18, 21 (1st c.BC, unless in signification 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 1.3).}}
**B.2.b** **position in relation to the compass**, ἡ σ. ἤλλακτο τῶν ὡρέων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.26; ἡ σ. τοῦ νότου καὶ τῆς μεσαμβρίης [prev. passage]}} **setting** of a wind from a quarter, τῶν ἐτησίων ἤδη στάσιν ἐχόντων having **set in**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.5.3}}; γίνεταί τις ἀνέμου σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.48.2, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 362b33, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Signis Tempestatum” 35 (_plural_); see below 111.4.}}
**B.2.c** of planetary connexion, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 38.17.}}
**B.2.d** _metaphorically_, from a boxer's **position**, ὥσπερ.. ὁρᾶτε τοὺς πύκτας περὶ τῆς σ. ἀλλήλοις διαγωνιζομένους, οὕτω καὶ ὑμεῖς.. ὑπὲρ τῆς πόλεως περὶ τῆς σ. (τάξεως codices, but{{Refs 4th c.BC+|compare 1st c.AD: Quintilianus Rhetor Latinus “Inst.” 3.6.3) αὐτῷ μάχεσθε 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.206}}: hence, **position** taken up by a litigant (especially defendant), {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Topica” 25.93; ἐπ᾽ ἀδίκου σ. ἱστάμενος “PRein.” 18.16 (2nd c.BC)}}; **issue**, σ. ὁρική, νομική, λογική, etc., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ τῶν στάσεων” 2, compare 5th c.AD: Syrianus Philosophus et Rhetor “in Hermogenem commentaria” 2.55 R.}}
**B.2.e** **position, opinion** of a philosopher, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 4, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 2.48, 3.33, 37, +others, 2nd c.AD(?): Marcellinus Medicus “de Pulsibus - Festschrift zur 49. Versammlung deutscher Philologen und Schulmänner” 234.}}
**B.3** **position, state, condition** of a person, ἐν τῇ καλλίονι στάσει εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 253d}}; especially of moral, social, political **position**, μειρακιώδης {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.33.6; ἰδιώτου 1st-2nd c.AD: Epictetus Philosophus “Enchiridion” 48; φιλοσόφου 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.15.13; σ. ἔχειν ἐν τῷ βίῳ [prev. work] 1.21.1}}; **state of affairs,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|Ostr. 1151.3 (3rd c.AD)}}; ἡ σ. τῆς νόσου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ κρισίμων ἡμερέων” 10, compare “Mochl.” 21 (_plural_).}}
**B.4** στάσις μελῶν, explained by [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia as ={στάσιμον} (which see), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1281.}}]
**B.II** **party, company, band**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1117 (Lyric poetry), “Ch.” 114, 458 (Lyric poetry), “Eu.” 311 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**B.III** especially **party formed for seditious purposes, faction**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 51, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.59, 60; ἐπεκράτησε τῇ στάσι [prev. work] 173}}; αἱ τῶν Μεγαρέων σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.71.}}
**B.III.2** **faction, sedition, discord**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 781, 6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 4.19, 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 245, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.65; οἴκων 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 9.13, +others, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.28, +others; σ. ἀντιάνειρα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 12.16}}; σκεπτομένων πόθεν ἡ σ. how the **row** began, {{Refs|Batrachomyomachia 135}}; στάσις ἐν ἀλλήλοισιν ὠροθύνετο a **contest**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 202; ὅστις.. στάσιν ποιέοι περὶ γαδαισίας “Berl.Sitzb.” 1927.8 (_Locrian dialect_, 5th c.BC)}}; εἰς λόγου στάσιν ἐπελθών {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1180; σ. γλώσσης [prev. author] “OT” 634; στάσει νοσοῦσα πόλις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 34; τὰς σ. ἐποιοῦντο πρὸς ἀλλήλους 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.79; στάσεις παύω 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.6.14; καταλύειν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 359; πόλιν εἰς στάσιν ἐμβάλλειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.4.11; τὴν πόλιν εἰς στάσεις κατέστησαν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 25.26; κατὰ στάσιν ἀποκτείνειν [prev. author] 30.13}}; opposed to πόλεμος, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 470b, compare “Phd.” 66c, 6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus [same place]; στάσεις καὶ διαστάσεις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1296a8.}}
**B.III.3** **division, dissent**, στάσιν ἐνέσεσθαι τῇ γνώμῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.20}}; οὐδ᾽ ἔνι σ. there's no **disputing** it, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 738 (troch.).}}
**B.III.4** _metaphorically_, τὰν ἀνέμων σ. {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 18 (unless in signification 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 1.2b); ἀνέμων πνεύματα.. στάσιν ἀντίπνουν ἀποδεικνύμενα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1087 (_anapaest meter_); σ. κυμάτων Ach. Tat. 3.2.}}
**B.IV** στάσεις,= τὰ πεφυκότα σπέρματα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 859.}}
**B.V** **statute, decree**, {{LXX|LXX.Dan.6.7(8), LXX.1Mac.7.18.}}

4715:
** στᾰτήρ**, ῆρος, ὁ,
(ἵστημι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus IV, compare “Etymologicum Magnum” 725.11) a weight,= λίτρα, κρόκης πέντε σ. 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 252, compare “IG” 12.314.42, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 4.173, “BGU” 953.2 (3rd-4th c.AD), 9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc.}}; σ. Αἰγιναῖος as a weight, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.78.}}
**II** **standard coin**, struck in various materials, whether gold, electrum, or silver:
**II.1** gold, σ. χρυσοῖ, σ. χρυσοῦ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 816, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 299e}}; of various standards, e.g. σ. Δαρεικός (compare Δαρεικός) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.28, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.28; Δαρεικοῦ χρυσίου στατῆρες “IG” 12.310.103; σ. Κροίσειος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.823a, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 3.87, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus; χρυσοῦ στατῆρες Λαμψακηνοί “IG” 12.339.32, +others, compare 7.2425, +others (Thebes, 4th c.BC)}}; σ. Φιλίππειος, Ἀλεξάνδρειος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 9.59, compare “SIG” 285.12 (Erythrae, 4th c.BC)}}; Πτολεμαϊκὸς σ. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Inscription Délos” 442 B 190 (2nd c.BC).}}
**II.2** electrum {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (χρυσός 1.1a), struck at Cyzicus, χρυσίου Κυζικηνοῦ σ. “IG” 12.302.12, +others, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 32.6; Κύζικος πλέα στατήρων 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 233}}; at Phocaea, σ. Φωκαϊται, Φωκαιῆς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.52, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 40.36; Φωκαϊκὼ στατῆρε “IG” 22.1388.42.}}
**II.3** silver, σ. Αἰγιναῖοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work]12.310.111, 22.1126.17, 1388.70, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.22; σ. Κορκυραῖοι “IG” 12.310 add.; σ. Κορίνθιος “SIG” 421.39 (Aetolia, 3rd c.BC), 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 4.175}}; in Sicily called δεκάλιτρος σ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 10; σ. πάτριος “SIG” 976.8 (Samos, 2nd c.BC)}}; later applied to the Attic τετράδραχμον, {{Refs 9th c.AD+|9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc., 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}}; also to the Ptolemaic τ., {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 567.4, 734.3, “PRev.Laws” 58.7 (all 3rd c.BC), “BGU” 1846.8 (1st c.BC), 2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus}} “*Geometry texts” 23.55,56; also of the Jewish **shekel,** {{NT|NT.Matt.17.27.}}
**III** **one who owes money, debtor**, πολλοὶ σ., ἀποδοτῆρες οὐδ᾽ ἂν εἷς (οὐδαμεῖ conjecture Kaibel) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 116.}}

4716:
** σταυρός**, ὁ,
**upright pale** or **stake**, σταυροὺς ἐκτὸς ἔλασσε διαμπερὲς ἔνθα καὶ ἔνθα πυκνοὺς καὶ θαμέας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.11, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.453, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.90, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.2.21}}; of **piles** driven in to serve as a foundation, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.16, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.25.}}
**II** **cross**, as the instrument of crucifixion, {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 2.18, NT.Matt.27.40, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.554a; ἐπὶ τὸν σ. ἀπάγεσθαι 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Peregr.” 34}}; σ. λαμβάνειν, ἆραι, βαστάζειν, _metaphorically_ of voluntary suffering, {{NT|NT.Matt.10.38, NT.Luke.9.23, 14.27}}: its form was represented by the Greek letter T, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Jud.Voc.” 12.}}
**II.b** **pale for impaling** a corpse, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Artoxerxes” 17.}}

4717:
** σταυρ-όω**,
(σταυρός) **fence with pales**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.25; σ. τὰ βάθη ξύλοις 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 24.1}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.100.}}
**II** **crucify**, {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.86.4, NT.Matt.20.19, Critodem. in “Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 8(4).200: _metaphorically_, σ. τὴν σάρκα}} **crucify** it, **destroy** its power, {{NT|NT.Gal.5.24, compare 6.14: ἧλος ἐσταυρωμένος}} nail **from a cross**, as amulet, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Asclepiades Junior Medicus cited in 6th c.AD: Alexander Trallianus Medicus 1.15.}}

4718:
** στᾰφῠλ-ή**, ἡ,
**bunch of grapes**, σταφυλῇσι μέγα βρίθουσαν ἀλωήν {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.561; ἡμερὶς ἡβώωσα τεθήλει δὲ σταφυλῇσι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.69, compare 7.121; σταφυλαὶ παντοῖαι 24.343, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 844e, NT.Rev.14.18}}; Πυρναίαις σ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.46}}; of ripe, fresh grapes, opposed to ὄμφαξ on the one hand, and σταφίς on the other, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 5.303: collectively in _sing._, “PPetr.” 3p.60 (3rd c.BC), “PCair.Zen.” 300.14 (3rd c.BC), “POxy.” 116.18 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}
**2** σ. ἀγρία,= μήλωθρον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.18.11, 1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Historia Naturalis” 23.21.}}
**II** **uvula when swollen at the end so as to resemble a grape** on the stalk, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προγνωστικόν” 23, 5th-6th c.BC: Nicopho Comicus 28, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 493a3}}; of the **uvula** generally, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Archigenes Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.969,974}}; **inflammation of the uvula,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Inscriptiones Graecae 42(1).126.30 (Epid, 2nd c.AD), 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 7.731 (_plural_).}}
**III** _paroxytone_ σταφύλη, **plummet of a level**, ἵπποι.. σταφύλῃ ἐπὶ νῶτον ἔϊσαι horses equal in height even **by the level**, matched to a nicety, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.765, compare 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 159, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus, “Etymologicum Magnum” 742.44.}}

4719:
** στάχυς** [στᾰχῡς
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 5, but στᾰχῠν 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Dianam” 130, 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.688]}}, υος, ὁ: _plural_ στάχυες {{Refs|“IG” 12.280.78,22}}.1424a333, etc.; _Epic dialect_ _dative_ σταχύεσσιν {{LXX+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.598; _accusative_ στάχῡς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 393, “OGI” 56.68 (Canopus, 3rd c.BC), but στάχυας LXX.Gen.41.7, etc:}}—**ear of corn**, in _plural_, Il.[same place], {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 473, NT.Matt.12.1, etc.}}; τοὺς ὑπερέχοντας τῶν σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1284a30: in _sing._, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 761, “fragment” 304.7, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 395, and freq in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus, “Hec.” 593, +others: _metaphorically_, σ. ἄτης 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 821}}; ἐκ καλάμης.. στάχυες, of Bacchylides' poems, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 4.1.34 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus):—of the Theban Σπαρτοί, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 939, “HF” 5, “Ba.” 264}}; of **the crop reaped** by Cleon in capturing the Spartans at Sphacteria, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 393}}; βίον θερίζειν ὥστε κάρπιμον σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 757.6.}}
**2** generally, **scion, progeny**, σ. ἄρσην {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] “fragment” 360.22, compare 3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 214}}; δισσὸν Βορέον σ. {{Refs 4th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Argonautica” 218; τέκνων 4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 6.304; Ἰνδῶν 4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 18.267}}; Ἑλλάδος ἀμώων ἄγαμον σ. {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.362.25.}}
**3** name of the chief star in the constellation Virgo, **Spica Virginis**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 97, 1st c.BC: Ptolemaeus Epigrammaticus “Alm.” 7.5: in _plural_, 4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 2.134.}}
**II** **lower part of the abdomen**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Heliodorus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 50.26 at Title Page, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.168, compare 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 194.4, 410.17.}}
**III** **base horehound, Stachys germanica**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.106, 1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Historia Naturalis” 24.136.}}
**IV** νάρδου στάχυς,= ναρδόσταχυς, {{Refs|“Geoponica” 7.13.1}}; ὁ τῆς νάρδου σ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.267.}}
**V** **surgical bandage, 'spica' bandage**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Heliodorus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 48.46 at Title Page, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(1).814.}}
**VI** παρὰ τοῖς ναυπηγοῖς τὸ ἐπὶ τῆς φάλαγγος μεριζόμενον, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**VII** = Heb. **shibboleth**, {{LXX|LXX.Judg.12.6.}}

4721:
** στέγ-η**,
_Doric dialect_and _Aeolic dialect_ **στέγα**, ἡ, **roof**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 897, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.27, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.8.9, NT.Mark.2.4, etc.}}; παρέχειν τινὶ σ. give one **shelter**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 631; στέγῃ δέχεσθαί τινας “OGI” 665.25 (Egypt, 1st c.AD).}}
**2** **ceiling**, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 15, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Iambi” 1.115 (“Hermes” 69.169).}}
**II** **roofed place, chamber, room**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.2, 148, 175, 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 347, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 8.13, etc.}}; **covered vestibule,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 22.1046.13}}; ἕρκειος σ., of **a tent**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 108}}; a hare's **seat** or **form**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “fragment” 174}}; ἐκ κατώρυχος σ., of the grave, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 1100, compare 888.}}
**II.2** **storey** of a house, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PStrassb.” 110.6 (3rd c.BC), “PCair.Zen.” 766.4 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}; ἡ ἀνωτάτη σ. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 15.3.7}}; αἱ στέγαι the upper **storeys,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|PPetr. 2p.28 (3rd c.BC),compare “SIG” 344.16 (Teos, 4th c.BC), “IG” 42(1).102.293 (Epid., 4th c.BC), “PLond.” 3.1164f28 (3rd c.AD).}}
**II.3** frequently in _plural_, **house, dwelling**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 3, 518, +others}}; κατὰ στέγας at **home**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 637, +others}}; ἐπελεῦσαι τῷ ἀνδρὶ ἐπὶ στέγαν to the man's **house,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Leges Gortynensium 3.46, compare “Schwyzer” 177.3 (Crete, 5th c.BC).}}
**III** **deck of a ship**, in Latin _stega_, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Plautus Comicus Latinus “Bacch.” 278, “Stich.” 413.}}

4722:
** στέγω**,
used by early writers mainly in _present_ and _imperfect_: _future_ στέξω uncertain conjecture in {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 11.29}}: _aorist_ ἔστεξα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.12.5, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 35, etc.:}} —_middle_, _aorist_ ἐστέξατο conjecture for ἐδέξατο in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 13.27 (3rd c.BC: Phalaecus Epigrammaticus)}}:— _passive_, _aorist_ ἐστέχθην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|6th c.AD: Simplicius Philosophus “in Epictetum commentaria” p.117 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}:—**cover closely, so as to keep** a fluid either **out** or **in**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 78a (of fire):}}
**A** **keep out** water, δόμος ἅλα στέγων a house **that keeps out** the sea, i.e. a good ship, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 135 (Lyric poetry): _absolutely_, νῆες οὐδὲν στέγουσαι}} not **water-tight**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.94; εὐνὰς τοιαύτας οἵας.. στέγειν.. ἱκανὰς εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 415e, compare “Ti.” 45c, “Cra.” 412d}}; τῇ.. στεγούσῃ γῇ in the **impervious** earth, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Criti.” 111d}}; συμμύει καὶ στέγει, of timber, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 5.7.4, compare 5.4.5; οἰκία στέγουσα “IG” 22.2498.23, compare 12(5).568.12 (Ceos, 5th-4th c.BC):}} —so in _middle_, στέγετο.. ὄμβρους **kept off** the rain **from himself**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.81}}; νεῦς οὐκ ἐστέξατο κῦμα {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” [prev. cited] (see. above); ταῦτα δὲ παρέξοντι οἰκοδομημένα καὶ στεγόμενα καὶ τεθυρωμένα “Tab.Heracl.” 1.142.}}
**2** of other things, **fend off, repel**, οὔτε οἱ πῖλοι ἔστεγον τὰ τοξεύματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.34; δόρυ πολέμιον στέγειν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 216}}; στέγων γὰρ ἐχθροὺς θάνατον εἵλετ᾽{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1014; σ. τὰς πληγάς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1295; στέγει ἡ σὰρξ τὸ προσπῖπτον θερμόν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 889a11.}}
**3** later, **bear up, sustain, support**, ἡ θάλαττα.. σ. τὰ βάρη {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] “fragment” 217; σ. τὸν ὄροφον 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 5.8.12; τοῦ κρυστάλλου στέγοντος τὰς διαβάσεις στρατοπέδων 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.34}}; **bear up against, endure, resist**, τὴν ἐπιφοράν, ἔφοδον, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.53.2, 18.25.4, compare “SIG” 700.23 (Lete, 2nd c.BC)}}; σ. νόσον {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.340 (6th c.AD: Palladas Epigrammaticus); τὸ δυσῶδες 1st c.AD: Memnon Historicus 2.4; τὰς ἐνδείας 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.526}}; ἡ ἀγάπη.. πάντα σ. {{NT|NT.1Cor.13.7, compare 9.12}}: _absolutely_, **contain oneself, hold out**, στέγειν, καρτερεῖν [{{NT+5th c.BC+|“Lyrica Alexandrina Adespota” 1.30, cf. NT.1Thes.3.1, 5; ἔστεξα ἕως ἔλθῃς “POxy.” 1775.10 (4th c.AD) (in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 11 στέξαντες is _falsa lectio_ for{στέρξαντες}).}}]
**B** **keep in, hold** water, etc., δάκρυον ὄμματ᾽ οὐκέτι στέγει probably _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 888 (troch.)}}; οὐκ ἂν δυναίμην μὴ στέγοντα πιμπλάναι I could not fill **leaky vessels**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “fragment” 899}}; ὕδωρ σ., of a vessel, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 621a: _metaphorically_, τὴν ψυχὴν κοσκίνῳ ἀπῄκασε.. τετρημένην, ἅτε οὐ δυναμένην στέγειν δι᾽ ἀπιστίαν καὶ λήθην 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 493c; [ψυχὴν] στέγουσαν οὐδέν [prev. author] “Lg.” 714a; in [prev. author] “R.” 586b, τὸ στέγον ἑαυτῶν}} probably means the **continent part** of each man,{{Refs|compare στεγανός 11.4.}}
**B.II** generally, **contain, hold**, ἄγγος σῶμα τοὐκείνου σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1118, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1412; ὄχλον σ. δῶμα [prev. author] “Hipp.” 843.}}
**B.III** **shelter, protect**, πύργοι πόλιν στέγουσιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 15 codices, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 797: _metaphorically_, ὅρκος σ. τὴν ὁμόνοιαν αὐτῶν 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 11.29 (conjecture)}}; τὸ ξύλον ἔστεξεν ἡ γῆ **retained and cherished** it, so that it struck root, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Romulus” 20, compare “Alex.” 35.}}
**B.III.2** **conceal, keep hidden**, κακόν τι κεύθεις καὶ στέγεις ὑπὸ σκότῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1214; ἥξει.., κἂν ἐγὼ σιγῇ στέγω 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 341}}; τί χρὴ στέγειν ἢ τί λέγει; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 136 (Lyric poetry); τὸ γὰρ γυναιξὶν αἰσχρὸν σὺν γυναῖκα χρὴ στέγειν [prev. author] “fragment” 679; σ. τἀμὰ καὶ σ᾽ ἔπη 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 273; στέξαι τὸ κριθέν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.8.2}}:—_passive_, **to be kept secret**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.72}}; παρ᾽ ὑμῶν εὖ στεγοίμεθ᾽ **let my counsel be kept secret** by you, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 596.}}
**B.IV** **close up**, in _passive_, τὰ τῶν ἀγγείων στόματα στεγόμενα {{Refs 7th c.AD+|7th c.AD: Paulus Aegineta Medicus 6.7}}. (Cf. Sanskrit _sthagati_ 'cover, hide', Latin _tego_, Engl. **thatch.**)

4723:
** στεῖρα**, ἡ,
a cow or other animal
**B** **that has not brought forth young**, στεῖραν βοῦν.. ῥέξειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.522; αἶγα στεῖραν “PCair.Zen.” 429.15 (3rd c.BC).}}
**B.II** of women, **barren**, {{LXX+NT|Diocl.fragment 29, LXX.Gen.11.30, 25.21, NT.Luke.1.7, 36}}; Μοῖρα σ. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 7.468 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus); στείρῃσι γυναιξί 5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Lithica” 459}}; of women past childbearing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 2.127: _metaphorically_, στεῖρα διάνοια, φύσις, ψυχή, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.441, 636, 478.}}
**B.II.2** **not having had offspring, virgin**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 670, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 17}}. (Cf. Sanskrit _star[imacracute]s_ 'barren cow, heifer', Latin _sterilis._)

4724:
** στέλλω**,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.294, etc.}}: _future_ στελῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 640}}, _Epic dialect_ στελέω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.287}}: _aorist_ ἔστειλα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.20}}, _Epic dialect_ στεῖλα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.248}}: _perfect_ ἔσταλκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 2.11.9, (ἀπ-, ἐπ-) 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.2, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 863}}: _pluperfect_ ἐστάλκει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 3.16.6, (ἐπ-) 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.37}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.285, etc.}}: _future_ στελοῦμαι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 604}}: _aorist_ ἐστειλάμην, _Epic dialect_ στειλ-, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.433, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 434}}, etc.:—_passive_, _future_ στᾰλήσομαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|(ἀπο-) 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.114 (variant), 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.93; simple σταλήσομαι 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 2.4.2:}}_aorist_ ἐστάλθην (in _compound_ ἀποσταλθέντες) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“GDI” 5186.4 (from Crete), compareScholia 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.21}}; more frequently ἐστάλην [ᾰ], {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.49, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.159, (ἐπ-) 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.91, etc.}}: _perfect_ ἔσταλμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.62, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 833d, etc.}}: _pluperfect_ ἐστάλμην {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 3.25}}, _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐστάλατο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 288}}; ἐσταλάδατο and ἐστελάδατο uncertain _lines_in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.89 (to be read ἐστάλατο)}}:—**make ready**, οὓς ἑτάρους στέλλοντα καὶ ὀτρύνοντα μάχεσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.294; οὔτε κέ σε στέλλοιμι μάχην ἐς κυδιάνειραν 12.325}}; νῆα σ. **rig** or **fit** her **out**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.287, compare 14.247; πλοῖον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.52; ναῦς τριάκοντα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.20; τὰ ἐκ νεώς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1077: also στρατιήν, στόλον, στρατόν}}, **fit out** an armament, **get** it **ready**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.141, 5.64, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 177, etc.; ᾧ δὴ τόνδε πλοῦν ἐστείλαμεν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1045: also στέλλειν τινὰ ἐσθῆτι}} **furnish with, array in**, a garment, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.14; χιτῶνι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 612: with double _accusative_, στολὴν σ. τινά 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 827 {στέλμα}; σ. τινὰς ὡς δεσποίνας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.4.5}}]; σ. ἕλκος **dress** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἐν κεφαλη—ͅ τρωμάτων” 14}}; **bury**, ἐνὶ γαίῃ σ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.205:}} —_middle_, στεῖλαί νυν ἀμφὶ χρωτὶ.. πέπλους **put on** robes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 821: with _dative_, ἐσθῆτι στειλάμενοι}} **having dressed themselves in.. **, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Philops.” 32: _metaphorically_, σ. κιθάρην 4th-3rd c.BC: Hermesianax Elegiacus 7.2}}:—_passive_, **fit oneself out, get ready**, ἄλλοι δὲ στέλλεσθε do you others **prepare** (to compete in the games), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.285; στρατὸν κάλλιστα ἐσταλμένον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.26, compare 3.14, 7.93: with _accusative_ _cognate_, τὴν αὐτὴν ταύτην ἐστ. [prev. work] 62: with _dative_, πρεπούσῃ στολῇ ἐστ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 833d}}: followed by a _preposition_, ἐσταλμένος ἐπὶ πόλεμον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.7}}; ἐς ἄγραν, ἐπ᾽ ἄγρην, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 604, “Anthologia Graeca” 7.535 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus); περὶ ὄργια 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1000 (Lyric poetry): with}}_infinitive_, ἐστέλλετο ἀπιέναι he **prepared** to go, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.124; κινεῖν κώπας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 181 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II** **dispatch, send**, ἐς οἶκον πάλιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 389, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 119 (Lyric poetry), etc.; ἐξ ἑνὸς στείλαντος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 737}}:—_middle_ and _passive_, **set out**, or (especially in _aorist 2_ _passive_) **journey**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.165, 3.53, 4.159, 5.92.β}}: with _accusative_ _cognate_, ὁδὸν στέλλεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1416 (_anapaest meter_), compare 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.296; πρὸς θάλασσαν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1527; ἐπὶ τὸν χρυσόν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.102; ἐπὶ πλοῖα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.1.5; τούτων γὰρ οὕνεκ᾽ ἐστάλην 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 328; ἴδιος ἐν κοινῷ σταλείς 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.49; οἷπερ ἐστάλην ὁδοῦ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 404; οἴκαδε Τροίας ἄπο 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1264}}; κατὰ γῆν (variant{γῆς}) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.6.5: _absolutely_, στέλλου, κομίζου}}**begone!** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 394: with _accusative_ loci, ὀμφαλὸν γῆς σ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 668; μέλαθρα [prev. author] “HF” 109 (Lyric poetry)}}; of things, **to be sent**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 776: _metaphorically_ of speech, ἀέρα ἐκπέμπειν στελλόμενον ὑφ᾽ ἑκάστων τῶν παθῶν 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.27U.}}
**II.2** _active_ _intransitive_ in sense of the _passive_ (in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus and Trag.)}}, **prepare to go, start, set forth**, ἔστελλε ἐς ἀποικίην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.147, compare 148, 5.125, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 571, 640: with _accusative_ _cognate_, κέλευθον τήνδε.. ἔστειλα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 609.}}
**II.3** _middle_, **set out upon** a task, στέλλεσθαι πρός τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 50e; ἐπί τι [prev. author] “Sph.” 230b}}; ἐπ᾽ αὐτὸ δὴ τοῦτο στελλώμεθ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Lg.” 892e; ἐπὶ θήρας πόθον ἐστέλλου 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 234 (_anapaest meter_); ἐπὶ τυραννίδ᾽ ἐστάλης 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 487.}}
**III** **summon, fetch, bring** a person to a place, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 860, compare “OC” 298, “Ph.” 623, 983; ὑμᾶς ἔστειλ᾽ ἱκέσθαι [prev. author] “Ant.” 165, compare “Ph.” 60, 495}}; [ἐμπορίαν] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 313e:}} —_middle_, σ᾽ ἂν οἴκους τοὺς ἐμοὺς ἐστειλάμην I would have **sent for** thee.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “OT” 434}}:—_passive_, {{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 550 (conjecture).}}
**III.b** ἡ ὁδὸς εἰς Κόρινθον στέλλει **leads** to Corinth, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 27.}}
**IV** **gather up, make compact**, especially as a nautical term, **furl, take in**, ἱστία.. στεῖλαν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.11, 16.353; στείλασα λαῖφος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 723}}:—_middle_, ἱστία μὲν στείλαντο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.433, compare 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Delum” 320, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mechanica” 851b8: _absolutely_, στέλλεσθαι (i.e. ἱστία) 3rd c.BC: Teles Philosophus p.10 H., 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.44.6}}; so ἐπιστολάδην δὲ χιτῶνας ἐστάλατο they **girded up, tucked up** their clothes to work, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 288, compare 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.45: _absolutely_, στειλάμενος σιγᾷς “Anthologia Graeca” 11.149.}}
**IV.2** **check**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.7U.}}; **repress**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.274, etc.}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.20.4}}; λόγον στειλώμεθα **draw in, shorten our** words, i.e. not speak out the whole truth, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 669}}; σ. τὸ συμβεβηκός **hush** it **up**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.85.7}}; πρόσωπον στέλλεσθαι **draw up** one's face, look rueful, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista “Praeparatio Sophistica” p.107 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}
**IV.3** in Medicine texts, **bind, make costive**, τὰ στέλλοντα **astringents**, opposed to τὰ καθαίροντα, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 1.221, compare 3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “Pr.Praef.”:}} —_passive_, φλέβες στέλλονται **shrink up**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 193.}}
**IV.4** _middle_, **restrict one's diet**, οὔτ᾽ ἂν ἀπόσχοιντο ὧν ἐπιθυμέουσιν, οὔτε στείλαιντο (variant{ὑποστ-}) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 5}}; στελλόμενοι τοῦτο **avoiding** this, {{NT|NT.2Cor.8.20; στέλλεο Περσεφόνας ζᾶλον “Supp.epigram” 2.615 (from Teos). (Cf. εὔσπολον, κασπολέω, σπολάς, σπόλος, σπελλάμεναι}}; probably I.-{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **sq[uglide]el-.** but not found in _cognate_ languages; I.-{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **st(h)el-** is probably found in OSlavonic _st[icaron]lati_ 'spread out', Latin _lātus_ (from ***stlātus**) 'broad', with which στέλλω may be _cognate_)

4725:
** στέμμα**, ατος, τό,
(στέφω) mostly in _plural_ (_singular_ in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.28, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 948)}}, **wreath, garland, chaplet**, especially of the priest's laurel-wreath, wound round a staff, στέμματ᾽ ἔχων ἐν χερσὶ.. χρυσέῳ ἀνὰ σκήπτρῳ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.14, 373}}; σκῆπτρον καὶ σ. θεοῖο{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 28, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 894}}; sometimes worn on the head, σ. ἐπὶ τῶν κεφαλῶν ἐχούσας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 617c}}; Φοῖβος ἔλακεν ἐκ τῶν σ. from **shrine with chaplcts decked**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 39, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1310, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.133}}; used in sacrificial ceremony, στέμμασι πυκασθείς (of victim) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.197, compare “SIG” 1025.31 (Cos, 4th-3rd c.BC)}}; σ. πάλας, as a prize, {{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 247 (from Mysia); στέμματ᾽ Ὀλυμπιάδων [prev. work]881 (from Cyzicus), etc.}}; ὁ ἐπὶ τῶν στεμμάτων an official connected with the **crowns of office** of magistrates (compare στεπτικός, στέφανος), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PFay.” 87i10 (2nd c.AD), “POxy.” 2130.7 (3rd c.AD),compare “PRyl.” 77.28 (2nd c.AD).}}
**2** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 3 says the στέμματα}} were **wreaths of wool** wound round the olive-branch; hence στέμματα ξήνασ᾽ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 12.}}
**II** in _plural_, στέμματα **pedigrees, family trees**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Numa” 1}}; Latin _stemmata quid faciunt?_ {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Juvenalis, D. Junius Poeta Latinus 8.1, compare 1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Historia Naturalis” 35.6.}}
**II.2** **guild**, {{Refs|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 3995b (from Iconium);= φυλή, [prev. work]9897 (Smyrna, Jewish); ὑπὲρ φιλοκυνηγῶν τοῦ σ}}. **guild** of huntsmen, {{Refs|“Supp.epigram” 3.499 (from Philippi).}}

4727:
** στενάζω**,
{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 696, etc.}}: _future_ **-άξω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.259, 3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 973, (ἀνα-) 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 656 (Lyric poetry)}}: _aorist_ ἐστέναξα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 33.6, etc.}}:—_passive_, _perfect_ ἐστέναγμαι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 412:}} —properly _frequentative verb_ of στένω, **sigh deeply**: generally, **sigh, groan**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus [same place], “Pers.” 1046 (Lyric poetry), “Eu.” 788 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 917; ἐπ᾽ ἄτῃ [prev. author] “El.” 1299; σ. κακοῖς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 199}}; οἴκοις **in** the house, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 1035 (Lyric poetry)}}; frequently with a _neuter_ _adjective_, οἰκτρόν, δεινὸν σ., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Supp.” 104, “Med.” 1184}}; μέγα σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “IT” 957, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.69}}; τί ἐστέναξας τοῦτ; why **utteredst thou** this **moan?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 550: with _accusative_ _cognate_, παιᾶνα σ. [prev. author] “Tr.” 578 (Lyric poetry), compare “HF” 753 (Lyric poetry); ἀρὰς τέκνοις [prev. author] “Ph.” 334 (Lyric poetry)}}; πηλίκον τί ποτ᾽ ἂν στενάξεια; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.210.}}
**2** _transitive_, **bemoan, bewail**, πότμον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 882 (Lyric poetry), compare “OC” 1672 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}; τινα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1640, “Ba.” 1027.}}

4728:
** στενός**,
_Ionic dialect_ **στεινός**, ή, όν, _Aeolic dialect_ **στένος** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 34 (uncertain reading)}}:— **narrow**, opposed to εὐρύς, πλατύς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.8 (_superlative_), 4.195, +others}}; ψαλίς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 367; δίαυλος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 435; ἐσβολή 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.175 (_comparative_)}}; πόρος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 176; ἡ ἔσοδος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.51; οὔτ᾽ εὐρεῖα οὔτε στενὴ διαφυγή 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 737a}}; ἐν στενῷ, _Ionic dialect_ στεινῷ, in **a narrow space**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 413, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.60}}. ; ποιεῖν τὸν δῆμον εὐρὺν καὶ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 720; σ. ποδεών 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.31; ἔντερον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 161}}; πόροι, φλέβες, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 101a, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 66a}}; κεφαλή, πόδες, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 5.30.}}
**2** _substantive_, τὰ σ. the **narrows, straits**, of a pass, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.223}}; of a sea, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.86, etc.}}; of the **straits** of Gibraltar, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 3.5.5}}; so τὸ σ. the **strait** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(from Hellespont), 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMar.” 9.1; ἐπὶ σ. τῆς ὁδοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.1.29}}; also ἡ στενή **a narrow strip** of land, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.99}}; τὰ σ. **passes, defiles**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.334 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **close, confined**, ἀπειληθέντες ἐς στεινόν driven into **a corner**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.34; σ. ζῶμεν χρόνον 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 410; εἰς στενὸν κομιδῇ τὰ τῆς τροφῆς τινι καταστήσεται 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.22; εἰς σ. τοῦ καιροῦ φθείρεσθαι 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 1.24.}}
**II.2** **scanty, petty**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 497c; ὑποθέσεις 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 7.7.6; ἐλπίδες 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.52; ἐρωτήσεις 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 2.30}}; **small-minded, narrow-minded**, in _adverb_ _comparative_, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PGiss.” 40 ii7 (3rd c.AD).}}
**II.3** of sound and style, **thin, meagre**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Audibilibus” 803b24, “Rh.” 1413b15}}; **hard** to pronounce, συλλαβὴ σ. καὶ δύστομος [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 2.15.— 4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus “Scholia in Theodosii Canones” 2.76 H., “Etymologicum Magnum” 275.50 say that στενός, like{κενός}, forms the _comparative_ and _superlative_ στενότερος, στενότατος, and these forms are explainable from *στενϝότερος, *στενϝότατος, which are implied by the Ionic forms στεινότερος, -ότατος (στεινότερος occurs in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.181, 7.175}}], **στενότερος** in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 7.3073.109 (Lebad., 2nd c.BC), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 111d, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.3 with variant)}}; and στενοτάτου is required by the metre in {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Scymnus Geographus 922}}; the form στενώτερος is however found in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 22, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 675a35, +others}}
**III** _adverb_, στενῶς διακεῖσθαι to be **in difficulties,** {{LXX+3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Zenon Papyri du Caire 498 (3rd c.BC), “PTeb.” 760.19 (3rd c.BC), 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 8.86, cf. LXX.1Ki.13.6.}}

4729:
** στενοχωρ-έω**,
**to be straitened, confined**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Macho Comicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 13.582b}}: _metaphorically_, **to be anxious, in difficulty**, ἐπί τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “παραγγελίαι” 8; εὶς τὴν ἀκρίβειαν τῆς ἑρμηνείας στενοχωρεῖ ὁ λόγος “IPE” 12.39.18 (Olbia, not before 2nd c.AD).}}
**II** _transitive_, **crowd, straiten**, τοὺς ἀπαντῶντας {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nigr.” 13}}; τὰς πύλας, τὰς ὁδούς, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Charito Scriptor Eroticus 5.3, 4.7; ταλάντοις τοὺς θησαυρούς 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 59.15}}:—_passive_, with _future_ _middle_ {{Refs 4th c.AD+|(4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 25.310d)}}, **to be crowded together**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 20.29, 2nd c.AD(?): Charito Scriptor Eroticus 3.2}}; ἐν ταὐτῷ σ. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tox.” 29; χῶραι -οῦνται ποσὶ μιαιφόνων 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 4.3.10; ἀγορὰ -ουμένη ὄχλῳ 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.67}}; of stricture, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Heliodorus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 50.9.1}}; **to be cramped** or **confined**, ὁ Εὐφράτης -ούμενος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Isidorus Characenus Geographus 1, cf. Scholia 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” “Oxy.” 221 xi 8, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “Sententiae ad intelligibilia ducentes” 27}}; of a picture, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista [prev. cited]}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_, **press closely**, **tina** {{LXX|LXX.Judg.16.16}}:—_passive_, **to be straitened, cramped**,{{LXX+5th c.AD+|LXX.Isa.28.19(20), 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 98}}; **ἐν** τοῖς σπλάγχνοις {{NT|NT.2Cor.6.12}}; ὑπὸ τῶν κακῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 57.}}

4730:
** στενοχωρ-ία**,
_Ionic dialect_ -ίη, ἡ, **narrowness of space, a confined space**, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 14 (codices, but see at {στενυγροχωρίη}) σ. τῇ φάρυγγι παρέχειν [prev. work] 41}}]; **want of room**, by sea or land, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.89, 4.26, 30, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 708b; ὑπὸ στενοχωρίας [prev. author] “Tht.” 195a}}; opposed to εὐρυχωρία and ἄνεσις, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.679f}}, compare 182b; σ. βίου **the short space** of life **remaining**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 2.41; τὸν λόγον ἀπὸ τῆς ἐκεῖ στενοχωρίας εἰς πεδίον ἐξαγάγῃ 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 64.10.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **straits, difficulty**, ἡ σ. τοῦ ποταμοῦ **difficulty of passing** the river, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.3.7, cf. LXX.Deu.28.53, +others}}; **distress**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“OGI” 339.103 (Sestos, 2nd c.BC)}}; ἡ τῆς πόλεως {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.67.1, etc.; ἡ τοῦ καιροῦ 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 39.34: _plural_ in NT.2Cor.6.4, “PLond.” 5.1677.11 (6th c.AD)}}; also, **narrow limits**, probably in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.220 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}

4731:
** στερεός**, ά, όν,
also **στερρός** (which see), **firm, solid**, σ. λίθος ἠὲ σίδηρος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.494; βοέαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.493}}; αἰχμὴ σ. πᾶσα χρυσέη all of **solid** gold, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.52, compare 183; ἕρμα σ. γῆς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 854, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 9.16; γῆ σ. καὶ ἀδιάλυτος 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 14.2}}; τὰ -ώτερα τῶν ὀστέων, opposed to τὰ ἀραιότερα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 33}}; τὸ σ., opposed to κενόν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus cited in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 188a22, “Metaphorical” 985b7}}; opposed to μαλθακός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 239c}}; κυσὶ σ. καὶ ἰσχνοῖς, opposed to προβάτοις πίοσι καὶ ἁπαλοῖς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “R.” 422d; ἀθλητής 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 2.132; βραχίονες 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.48; δέρματα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 321a; νῆμα [prev. author] “Plt.” 282e}}; σ. κέρας **solid**, opposed to κοῖλον, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 500a6; σ. κάλαμος 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.11.10}}; στερεὰ τροφή **solid** food, {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 2.4, NT.Heb.5.12, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.16.39 (_comparative_)}}; τὸ σ. σῶμα, opposed to ὁ χυλός, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.463}}; σ. κοιλίη **costive**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Acut.” (“Sp.”)}}. _adverb_ -ρεῶς **firmly, fast**, κατέδησαν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.346; ἐντέτατο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.263; νῶτα.. ἑλκόμενα σ., of wrestlers, 23.715.}}
**b** of money, **standard, of full value**, ἀργυρίου στερεὰ τάλαντα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 826 D 20 (Delph., 2nd c.BC)}}; so perhaps of sums due in kind, πυροῦ στερεοῦ {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PRein.” 8.5 (2nd c.BC), +others}}; and of linear and square measures, τῆς προσούσης αὐλῆς πηχῶν σ. ὀκτὼ τὸ ἐπιβάλλον αὐτῷ μέρος ἥμισυ πήχεις σ. τέσσερας eight (four) **standard** cubits, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PStrassb.” 87 (2nd c.BC),compare “PLond.” 3.1024.19 (2nd c.BC)}}; πόδες σ. **standard** feet, {{Refs|“Milet.” 7p.59 (from Didyma)}}; μέτρημα σ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Supp.epigram” 4.446.11 (ibid, 3rd-2nd c.BC).}}
**c** ὠρύγη ποταμὸς ἐπὶ τὰ τρία σ. the ditch was restored by digging to its three **normal dimensions**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“OGI” 672 (Canopus, 1st c.AD)}}, compare 673, where the Latin version has **at tria soldu** (**m**).
**2** _metaphorically_, **stiff, stubborn**, στερεοῖς ἐπέεσσι, opposed to μειλιχίοις, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.267; κραδίη -ωτέρη ἐστὶ λίθοιο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.103}}. _adverb_ -ρεῶς, ἀποειπεῖν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.510, compare 23.42.}}
**3** later, **hard, stubborn, cruel**, πῦρ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).36; ὀδύναι [prev. author] “P.” 4.221; ἀπειλαί 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 174 (_anapaest meter_); ἁμαρτήματα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1262 (Lyric poetry); ἦθος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 309b; οὕτω σ. [τι] πρᾶγμα θερμόν ἐσθ᾽ ὕδωρ 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 245; σ. φωνή 3rd-4th c.AD: Tryphiodorus Epicus 490}}; τοῦτο ἤδη -ώτερον **harder, more difficult**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 348e.}}
**4** of language, τὸ εὔτονον καὶ σ. **solidity**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Dinarcho” 8; ποιήματα 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 5.5, compare 4 (_superlative_).}}
**5** σ. ζῴδια, i.e. productive of **settled** conditions, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Serapio Astrologus in “Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 1.100.17, 2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 32, “PMag.Lond.” 46.47.}}
**II** of bodies and quantities, **solid, cubic**, opposed to ἐπίπεδος (plane), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 51c}}; σ. γωνία a **solid** angle, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ti.” 54ef, compare 3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 11 “Def.” 11; σ. πῆχυς “POxy.” 669.7 (3rd c.AD)}}; σ. ἀριθμός a **cubic** number, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1316a8}}; τὰ σ. **cubic numbers, representing bodies of three dimensions**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 148b}}: _dative singular_ στερεῷ **in the third power,** {{Refs|Theol.Ar. 4}}. (Cf. Sanskrit _sthirás_ 'firm, hard, solid', OHG. **star** 'rigid', O{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **starian** 'stare fixedly'.)

4732:
** στερεό-ω**,
**make firm** or **solid**, τοὺς πόδας {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 4.3, compare 5; τὴν γῆν ἐπὶ τῶν ὑδάτων LXX.Psa.135(136).6}}; τὸν οὐρανόν{{LXX|LXX.Isa.45.12}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 1.9, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 735b2.}}
**2** **strengthen**, τοῦτον {{NT|NT.Act.3.16}}; **confirm**, κρίσιν {{LXX|LXX.Sir.3.2}}:—_passive_, **to be made strong**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.8.8, NT.Act.3.7}}: _metaphorically_, **to be firmly established, confirmed**, {{LXX+1st c.BC+|LXX.1Ki.2.1, +others, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 15.57.}}

4733:
** στερέ-ωμα**, ατος, τό,
**solid body**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ φυσῶν” 8, 5th c.BC: Anaxagoras Philosophus cited in “Placita Philosophorum” 2.25.9.}}
**b** ἄϋλα σ. immaterial **solids**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 425, compare 205.}}
**2** **foundation** or **framework**, e.g. the skeleton, on which the body is, as it were, built, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 655a22}}; στερεώματος ἕνεκα τοῦ περιτρήτου to **strengthen** it, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Belopoeica” 95.8}}: _metaphorically_, **solid part, strength** of an army, {{LXX|LXX.1Mac.9.14}}; also, **ratification**, ἐπιστολῆς{{LXX|LXX.Est.9.29}}; **steadfastness**, τῆς πίστεως {{NT|NT.Col.2.5.}}
**3** ={στεῖρα} (of a ship), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 5.7.3.}}
**4** **firmament**, i.e. the sky, the heaven above, {{LXX+3rd c.AD+|LXX.Gen.1.6, LXX.Eze.1.22, +others; τὸν τῶν οὐρανίων σ. δεσπότην “Tab.Defix.Aud.” 242.8 (Carthage, 3rd c.AD).}}

4735:
** στέφᾰνος**, ὁ,
(στέφω) **that which surrounds** or **encompasses**, πάντῃ γάρ σε περὶ σ. πολέμοιο δέδηεν the **circling** fight, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.736}}; of the **wall round** a town, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.32; σ. πόλεος 6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 72, compare 5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Argonautica” 761, 897; compare στεφάνη 1.2}}; καλλίπαις σ. **circle** of fair children, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 839.}}
**II** **crown, wreath, chaplet,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Hymni Homerici 7.42; χρύσεος [prev. work]32.6; στεφάνους.. ἄνθεα ποίης 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 576, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.240; κισσίνους σ. δρυός τε 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 703}}; δάφνας σ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Isyllus Lyricus 19}}; ῥόδινος, ῥοδόεις, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 83, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.64; ἀνθεμεῦντες 6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 62; πλεκτὸν σ. ἐκ λειμῶνος φέρω 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 73, compare 6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 1.2; μύρτων 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 330 (Lyric poetry); κιττοῦ καὶ ἴων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 212e; φιλύρας 4th c.BC: Xenarchus Comicus 13, etc.; στεφάνωσεν δρακόντων στεφάνοις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 102 (Lyric poetry)}}; σ. εἴρειν, πλέκειν, ἀνείρειν, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.77, “I.” 8(7).74, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1006; ὑφαίνειν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.646e; πέρθεσθαι φόβαισι 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 78, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 984 (Lyric poetry)}}; θεῖσα ἀμφὶ βοστρύχοις{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1160; σ. ἐλαίας ἀμφέθηκά σοι [prev. author] “Ion” 1433; περιθεῖναι σ. τινί 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1227; χρυσῷ σ. ἀναδῆσαί τινα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.121; χρυσῷ στεφάνῳ στεφανωθῆναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Ion” 530d}}; μύρα, στεφάνους ἑτοίμασον for a feast, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 273, compare 4th c.BC: Amphis Comicus 9.4, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 250, etc.}}; hung at the door on festive occasions, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Ephippus Comicus 3.2.}}
**II.b** in _plural_, οἱ σ. the **garland-market**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 83.}}
**II.2** **crown of victory** at the public games, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.76}}; τῆς ἐλαίης τὸν διδόμενον σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.26; νικᾶν παγκρατίου στέφανον 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 5.5, compare “I.” 1.21; σ. ἔλαχεν [prev. author] “O.” 10(11).61}}; ὁ ἐπὶ τοῦ σ., title of an officer who had charge of these matters in Roman times, {{Refs|“IGRom.” 4.1435.15 (from Smyrna).}}
**II.2.b** **honorary wreath** or **crown**, frequently worked in gold, awarded for public services in war or peace, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.110.10, 22.212.24, 338.19, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 943c, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.46, “OGI” 49.7 (Egypt, 3rd c.BC), +others, 3rd c.BC(?): Callixinus Historicus 2}}: such crowns were frequently dedicated in temples, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 22.1386.33, compare 11(2).199 B 60, +others (Delos, 3rd c.BC)}}; περὶ τοῦ σ., title of {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18, compare 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.187, +others}}: _metaphorically_, **prize, reward**, αὑτῷ μὲν σ. περιθείς, Σαμίοισι δὲ κῦδος epigram cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.88; τοὺς παῖδας.. δημοσίᾳ ἡ πόλις θρέψει, ὠφέλιμον σ... τῶν.. ἀγώνων προτιθεῖσα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.46}}; τοῦδε γὰρ ὁ σ. his is the **prize** (or perhaps for (bringing) him the **prize** is offered), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 841 (hexameters).}}
**II.2.c** σ. πυργωτὸς καὶ οὐαλλάριος,= Latin _corona muralis et vallaris_, a Roman military decoration, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“OGI” 560.10 (Tlos, 1st c.AD); σ. τειχικός [prev. work]540.19 (Pessinus, 1st c.AD).}}
**II.3** **crown of glory, honour**, οὐκ ἂν αἰσχυνθείην εἰπὼν στέφανον τῆς πατρίδος εἶναι τὰς ἐκείνων ψυχάς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 50}}; ἐλευθερίας ἀμφέθετο σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 98; σ. εὐκλείας μέγας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 465, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1. 100, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 315; μέγας γάρ σοι ὁ σ. ἐστιν ὑπὸ πάντων εὐλογεῖσθαι “PSI” 4.405.4 (3rd c.BC)}}; σ. ζωῆς, δόξης, {{NT|NT.Jam.1.12, NT.1Pet.5.4}}; ὁ τῆς δικαιοσύνης σ. {{NT+8th c.BC+|NT.2Tim.4.8. ἀνδρὸς στέφανος παῖδες 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus “Epigrammata” 13, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 194 (Lyric poetry), NT.1Thes.2.19.}}
**II.4** **crown as a badge of office**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.32; πέπαυνται ἄρχοντες καὶ τοὺς σ. περιῄρηνται [prev. author] 26.5; ὁ βασιλεὺς ὅταν δικάζῃ περιαιρεῖται τὸν σ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 57.4}}; ὁ σ. οὗτος, of the office of ἀρχιερεὺς Ἀσίας, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 1.21.2, compare “OGI” 470.21 (Asia Minor), 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 53.4}}; ἔχειν τὸν σ. **to be in office**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 1007.22 (Pergam., 2nd c.BC); ἡ ἀπόθεσις τῶν σ. [prev. work]900.16 (Panamara, 4th c.AD)}}; ἀναδήσασθαι τὸν σ. τοῦτον {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1252v. 20 (3rd c.AD)}}: see at {στεφανηφόρος} {{Refs|11, στεφανόω 11.5.}}
**II.5** in Egypt, **money gift to the sovereign, levied by the state,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|PTeb. 746.24 (3rd c.BC), “PSI” 4.388.5 (3rd c.BC), “PCair.Zen.” 36.27 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}; like wise in Syria, {{LXX|LXX.1Mac.10.20}}; also of similar gifts to a court favourite, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PFay.” 14 (2nd c.BC),compare “Ostr.Bodl.” i202 (2nd c.BC).}}
**II.6** τὰς εἰς τὸν σ. ἐπαγγελίας οὐκ ἔλαβον,= **aurum coronarium non accepi, Mon.Anc.**{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|11.22,compare “PFay.” 20.7 (3rd-4th c.AD).}}
**II.7** **donation**, euphemism for a bribe, διεθέντος μου ὑπάρξει σοι εἰς σ. τάλαντα δεκαπέντε {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PGrenf.” 1.41.2 (2nd c.BC),compare “OGI” 221.6 (Ilium, 3rd c.BC), “PGoodsp.Cair.” 5.5 (2nd c.BC)}}; **gratuity, bonus,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|PFlor. 74.14 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II.8** the constellation {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“Corona”, 6th c.BC: Epimenides Philosophus 25, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 362b10, 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 71, “PHib.” 1.27.58, 187 (3rd c.BC)}}; σ. τόν τε κλείουσ᾽ Ἀριάδνης {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.1003.}}
**II.9** name of a πηγή in the Chaldaean system, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 96.}}
**II.10**= δάφνη Ἀλεξανδρεία, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.145}}; Ἡλίου σ.,= ἅλιμος, Ps.-{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.91.}}

4737:
** στεφᾰν-όω**,
_middle_, Syracusan _2nd pers._ _singular_ _imperative_ στεφάνουσο {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|Scholia 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 11.42}}:—_passive_, _future_ **-ώσομαι**{{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 1.496 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}; **-ωθήσομαι** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.20, +others, “PCair.Zen.” 60.7 (3rd c.BC):}}
**I** used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus and 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus}} only in _passive_, **to be put round in a circle** or **as a rim** or **border**, and hence **to be put round**, ἣν περὶ μὲν πάντῃ Φόβος ἐστεφάνωται **round about** the aegis **is** Terror **wreathed**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.739; τῇ δ᾽ ἐπὶ μὲν Γοργὼ ἐστεφάνωτο 11.36}}; ἀμφὶ δέ μιν θυόεν νέφος ἐστεφάνωτο **all round about** him **was** a cloud, {{Refs|15.153}}; νῆσον, τὴν πέρι πόντος ἐστεφάνωται the sea **lies round about** the island, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.195: rarely with _accusative_, τείρεα, τά τ᾽ οὐρανὸς ἐστεφάνωται}} constellations which heaven **has all round it**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.485, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 382, “IG” 42(1).129.9 (Epid.)}}; of a crowd of spectators **surrounding** a dancing-floor, ἀμφὶ δ᾽ ὅμιλος ἀπείριτος ἐστεφάνωτο {{Refs|“hymnus ad Venerem” 120}}; περὶ δ᾽ ὄλβος ἀπείριτος ἐστεφάνωτο around **were.. **riches **in a circle placed**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 204}}: so in later _Epic dialect_, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.1214, 4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 5.99, 5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Argonautica” 45, etc.: also in}}_active_, περίτροχον ἐστεφάνωσαν αἱμασιήν **made** a fence **round**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 4.90.}}
**I.2** **to be surrounded**, ἐστεφανωμένος τιάραν μυρσίνῃ **having his** tiara **wreathed** with myrtle, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.132}}; πεδία ἐστεφάνωται ὄρεσιν **are surrounded by.. **, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 19; ὅπλοισιν πόλις epigram cited in 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 9.15.6; χθὼν ἅτε νῆσος -ωται 2nd c.AD: Dionysius Periegeta 4}}: so in _active_, [Βαβυλῶνα] τείχεσιν ἐστεφάνωσεν {{Refs|[prev. author] 1006.}}
**II** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} in _active_, **crown, wreathe**, χαίταν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 14.24}}; Ὀρέστην σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 924; κρᾶτα κισσίνοις βλαστήμασιν [prev. author] “Ba.” 177; στεφάνοις [prev. work] 101 (Lyric poetry)}}; with _genitive_, πίτυος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD(?): Longus Scriptor Eroticus 2.31; σ. τινὰ ὡς σωτῆρα 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.45; τὸν νικῶντα θαλλῷ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 946b; νῖκαι σ. τινά 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 11.21}}; of crowning a corpse, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 538; a tomb, “IG” 12.1037, “Sammelb.” 7457.10 (3rd-2nd c.BC), 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Cont.” 22, “PLips.” 30.2 (3rd c.AD)}}; ships, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.981e}}; of the nuptial crown, {{LXX|LXX.Cant.3.11}}; κατηρῶντο τοῖς ἐστεφανωμένοις **newly wedded couples**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 33.29}}; στεφανοῦν εὐαγγέλια **crown** one **for** good tidings, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 647}}; στεφανοῦσα, title of a statue by Praxiteles (see. στέφω {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|111), compare 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 12.534d}}:—_passive_, **to be crowned** or **rewarded with a crown**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.55, 8.59, “PCair.Zen.” [same place], NT.2Tim.2.5; ἐλαίᾳ 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 4.13; ποίᾳ [prev. author] “P.” 8.19; φυτὸν στεφανούμενος 3rd c.AD(?): Achilles Tatius Astronomus 1.5; σ. καὶ ἀνακηρύττεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.18}}:—_middle_, **crown oneself**, στεφανωσαμένη δρυῒ καὶ.. σπείραισι δρακόντων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 535 (_anapaest meter_); στεφανοῦσθε κισσῷ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 106 (Lyric poetry); στεφανωσάμενος καλάμῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1006}}; στεφανωσάμενος αὐτόν (i.e. τὸν στέφανον) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Phalaris Epistolographus “Epistulae - Epistolographi” 40; στεφανοῦνται τῶν ἀνθέων 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Heroicus” 12a. 2; τῆς πίτυος 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 9.10}}: also _absolutely_, of one going to sacrifice, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.80; τῷ θεῷ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.3.21}}; at a festival, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1145, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 518.15, etc.}}; **win a crown**, of the victor at the games, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.15, 81, 12.17, “N.” 6.19}}:—_passive_, with _double_ _accusative_, ἐστεφανώθη Ἐλεύθερος.. Ἁδριάνεια πάλην {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“IG” 22.2087.64 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II.2** **crown** as an honour or reward {{Refs 4th c.BC+|compare (στέφανος 11.2b), 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.193, 4th c.BC: Theopompus Historicus 239, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 84, “IG” 22.212.30 (4th c.BC), etc.}}; **reward** by a gift of money, etc. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|compare (στέφανος 11.5), Καλλισθένην ἑκατὸν μναῖς 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator “fragment” 19, compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 14.53, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Timoleon” 16; σ. τινὰ πεντακοσίοις ἀργυρίου ταλάντοις, χιλίοις δὲ λιβανωτοῦ 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 13.9.5: also ἐστεφανωκότος.. τὰς δυνάμεις χρυσῶν μυριάδων τριάκοντα}} Gauthier et Sottas **Décret trilingue en l' honneur de Ptolémée IV** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|p.67 (3rd c.BC).}}
**II.3** _metaphorically_, **confer glory upon, decorate, honour**, τινὰ μολπᾷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.100; τοὺς πρεσβυτέρους ἤθεσι χρηστοῖς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 959}}; ἀπὸ τῶν ὑπαρχόντων τὴν πόλιν (by a victory in the games) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.26; Ἑλλάδα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1030, compare 5th c.BC: Critias Philosophus, Tragicus, Elegiacus 4 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator; ἔργοις γένος “TAM” 1.44 (from Xanthus)}}; [τὸ ῥόδον] ἐγκωμίῳ {{Refs 1st c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Epistulae” 51; ἀριστείοις 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.32; πανοπλίᾳ [prev. author] 20.84}}:—_passive_, σοφίας ἀριστεῖα ἐστεφανοῦτο {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Heroicus” 10.4.}}
**II.4** **crown** or **honour** with libations, σ. τύμβον αἵματι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 126 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**II.5** **crown** with the badge of office, especially of persons sacrificing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 26.8}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.1.40}}; of magistrates in office, ὁ ἐστεφανωμένος ἄρχων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.17; βούλεται -ωθῆναι ἐξηγητείαν “PRyl.” 77.37 (2nd c.AD).}}

4738:
** στῆθος**, εος, τό,
**breast**, of both sexes, being the front part of the θώραξ, divided into two μαστοί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|(4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 493a12, “PA” 688a13, +others), 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus and later (compare στέρνον), especially in Prose, rare (and usually _metaphorically_)}} in post-Homeric verse; found once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus, twice in 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus, twice in 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus, never in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus or 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus (see. below 1, 11); βάλε σ. παρὰ μαζόν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.480; ἔβαλε σ. μεταμάζιον 5.19; κληῒς ἀποέργει αὐχένα τε στῆθός τε 8.326, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 69e, 79c (_plural_), “Prt.” 352a (_plural_): in _plural_, διὰ στήθεσφιν (_Epic dialect_ _genitive_) ἔλασσε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.41; στήθεά τ᾽ ἠδ᾽ ἁπαλὴν δειρήν (of Briseis) 19.285}}; of animals, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|11.282, 16.163, +others,compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 4.1, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 496a9, 15, +others, “PCair.Zen.” 532.7, 18 (3rd c.BC), “BGU” 469.7 (2nd c.AD)}}; σ. φάσσης ἑψημένης {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.41, compare 1.51}}: as the seat of the voice and breath, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.221, 9.610, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 5.15, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 563 (Lyric poetry), 865 (_anapaest meter_)}}; more frequently as the seat of the heart, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.189, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.341, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 2.6, etc.}}; **chest**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 1.70, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1012, 1017 (both _anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.49 (_plural_), Diocl.fragment 142, “IG” 42(1).121.100 (_plural_, Epid., 4th c.BC), frequently in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus (see. above), “PEnteux.” 79.7 (3rd c.BC), “PTeb.” 316.19 (1st c.AD), 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.70b, +others}}; τὰ σ. **breasts** of a woman, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 2.133.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **the breast as the seat of feeling and thought**, as we use **heart**, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, but always in _plural_, θυμὸν ἐνὶ στήθεσσιν ὄρινε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.142, +others; θάρσος ἐνὶ σ. ἐνῆκεν 17.570}}; ἔχει κότον.. ἐν σ. ἑοῖσι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|1.83; ἐν γάρ τοι σ. μένος πατρώϊον ἧκα 5.125; νόον καὶ θυμὸν ἐνὶ σ. ἔχοντες 4.309; μῆτιν ἐνὶ σ. κέκευθε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.18, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 218, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 10.54: in Prose, εἰπεῖν ἃ ἔφησθα ἐν τῷ σ. ἔχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 236c; πλῆρες τὸ σ. ἔχειν [prev. work] 235c.}}
**III** ={στέρνον} {{Refs|111}}**breastbone**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 14.}}
**III.2** **ball of the foot**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 55, 58,compare “Epid.” 4.1, 2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus “περὶ ὀνομασίας” 125; τὸ σαρκῶδες [τοῦ ποδὸς] κάτωθεν στῆθος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 494a13}}; **ball of the hand** (below the thumb), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus “περὶ ὀνομασίας” 86; (below the fingers), 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 14.704}}; **palm**, uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ὀστέων φύσιος” 9: compare προστηθίς.}}
**III.3** **swelling, tumour**, ἐν τῷ ἥπατι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “ὀξέων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 2.6 (_plural_).}}
**IV** **breastshaped hill** or **bank**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.41.3, “PMasp.” 169b47 (6th c.AD),compare 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus (στῆθος}} has pan-Hellenic η, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus [same places], “IG” 42(1) [same place], 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Lavacrum Palladis” 88, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.79, 15.108, 135.)}}

4740:
** στηριγ-μός**, ὁ,
**being fixed, standing still**, τῶν πλανήτων ἀστέρων {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.81, compare 1st c.BC: Geminus Astronomicus 1.20, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.76d, Theo Sm.p.148 H., 2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 22, 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 34.28, +others, “PPar.” 19}} **bis**{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|11, 13 (3rd c.AD), 4th c.AD: Paulus Alexandrinus Astrologus “G.” 1, “Theologumena Arithmeticae” 15, “Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 8(4).241.}}
**2** **fixedness**, of steady light, opposed to flashing, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 395b7.}}
**3** _metaphorically_, ἐκπεσεῖν τοῦ ἰδίου σ. from your proper **firmness**, {{NT|NT.2Pet.3.17.}}
**4** Rhet., **sustaining** of the voice on certain words or syllables, so as to give them force, αἱ μακραὶ συλλαβαί, στηριγμούς τινας ἔχουσαι καὶ ἐγκαθίσματα {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 20}}; στηριγμοὺς ἔχειν πρὸς ἄλληλα τὰ ὀνόματα mutual **support, buttressing**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 40.4}}; compare ἀντιστηριγμός.

4741:
** στηρίζω**,
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1207, etc.}}: _future_ **-ίξω**{{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 4.52 (variant), NT.1Pet.5.10}}, **-ίσω** {{LXX|LXX.Sir.38.34, LXX.Jer.17.5, -ιῶLXX.Sir.6.37, LXX.Jer.24.6}}: _aorist_ ἐστήριξα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.443}}, _Epic dialect_ στήριξα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 498}}; _infinitive_ στηρίξαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.434, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 19.192, “PSI” 5.452.3 (4th c.AD)}}; _participle_ στηρίξας {{LXX+5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.57; _optative_ στηρίξειεν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.49; ἐστήρισα LXX.Gen.27.37, 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 1.98; _imperative_ στηρισάτω “Anthologia Graeca” 14.72}}:—_middle_, _aorist_ ἐστηριξάμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.242, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 11, etc. (see. below)}} later **-ισάμην** {{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.Isa.59.16, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Eumenes” 11}}: _future_ στηρίξομαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 5.35}}:—_passive_, _future_ στηριχθήσομαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 9.16}}: _aorist_ ἐστηρίχθην {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Tyrtaeus Elegiacus 11.22, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἐν κεφαλη—ͅ τρωμάτων” 3, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.126}}: _perfect_ ἐστήριγμαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 779, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 3.3, etc.}}; _infinitive_ ἐστηρίσθαι {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.26.19}}: _pluperfect_ ἐστήρικτο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.111, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 218, etc. (Cf. στῆριγξ, σκηρίπτομαι)}}:—**make fast, prop, fix**, [ἴριδας] ἐν νέφεϊ στήριξε **sets** rainbows in the cloud, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.28; οὐρανῷ ἐστήριξε κάρη, of Eris, 4.443}}; στηρίζειν αὐτὸ αὑτό φησι τὸ ἄπειρον (i.e. Anaxagoras) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 205b2, compare 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.61; σ. σήματ᾽ ἐν οὐρανῷ 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 10}}; so probably, [λίθον] Ζεὺς στήριξε κατὰ χθονός he **set** the stone **fast** in the ground, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 498; βάσιν ἐστήριξαν 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “fragment” 74.49; λίθον διορίζοντα ὅρους.. στηριχθῆναι ἐκέλευσαν “OGI” 769 (Palestine, 3rd-4th c.AD).}}
**2** **support**, σίτῳ τινά {{LXX|LXX.Gen.27.37}}; **feed up** a patient, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 19.192; σ. τὴν δύναμιν εὐστομάχοις τροφαῖς [prev. author] 18(2).34, compare 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “ὀξέων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 1.1}}: _metaphorically_, **confirm, establish**, τὴν ἀρχήν {{NT+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 1.98; τοὺς ἀδελφούς NT.Luke.22.32, cf. NT.2Thes.2.17, NT.1Pet.5.10}}; **corroborate**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.57.}}
**3** _middle_, **ground, establish for oneself**, κόσμον ἑαῖς στηρίξατο βουλαῖς {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Fragmenta” 299; πόδα ἐπὶ γαίης “Anthologia Graeca” 14.72}}; πόντος στηρίξατο κῦμα νήνεμον **settled its** wave into a calm, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. work]9.271 (1st c.AD: Apollonides Epigrammaticus).}}
**B** _passive_ and _middle_, **to be firmly set** or **fixed, stand fast**, οὐδὲ πόδεσσιν εἶχε στηρίξασθαι he could not **get a firm footing**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.242, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Eumenes” 11; οὐδαμῇ ἐστήρικτο 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 218}}; [δώματα] κίοσιν ἀργυρέοισι πρὸς οὐρανὸν ἐστήρικται the house **is lifted up** to heaven on pillars, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Th.” 779; ὀρθὴ δ᾽ ἐς ὀρθὸν αἰθέρ᾽ ἐστηρίζετο 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1073; στηριχθεὶς ἐπὶ γῆς 7th c.BC: Tyrtaeus Elegiacus 11.22; πρὸς τῇ γῇ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 376b23 (assuming variant)}}; ὅσοι ἐστηρίξαντο τῇ πτέρνῃ ἰσχυρῶς πηδήσαντες **light heavily on** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 11, compare “Art.” 86}}; ὕβον, ἐφ᾽ οὗ ἐστήρικται τὸ ἄλλο σῶμα **is steadied**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 499a17}}; ἐστηριγμένα [ἔχειν] τὰ σπλάγχνα **supported**, opposed to κρεμάμενα, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.570}}; ἄμπελος κάμακι σ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 7.731 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus); Ἀσκληπιὸν -ιζόμενον βάκτρῳ “IG” 42(1).88.9 (Epid., 2nd c.AD)}}; of the fixed stars, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 230, 274, etc.}}; opposed to ἀκοντίζεσθαι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 395b4; λίθος ἐστήρικται 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Apollinem” 23}}; χάσμα μέγα ἐστ. {{NT|NT.Luke.16.26}}; of places, merely **to be situated**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Dionysius Periegeta 204.}}
**B.2** _metaphorically_, κακὸν κακῷ ἐστήρικτο evil **was set upon** evil, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.111}}; τί τοι χόλος ἐστήρικτα; {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.816}}; δέκατος μεὶς οὐρανῷ ἐστήρικτο the tenth month **was set** in heaven, {{Refs|“hymnus ad Mercurium” 11}}; of a person, ὅπου.. στηρίζει ποτέ wheresoever **thou art tarrying, art settled**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 194 (Lyric poetry)}}; ὅροι ἐστηριγμένοι **fixed** principles, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus}} “*Geometry texts” 3.25; ἀνάγκη στηριχθῆναι τὸ ν ¯ must **be firmly pronounced**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 22.}}
**B.3** of diseases,{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|= below 11.2, μέχρις ἂν [οἱ νοσοποιοὶ χυμοὶ] ἔν τινι τῶν ἀσθενεστέρων στηριχθῶσιν 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.126, compare 789,855, 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων ὀξέων παθῶν” 1.5.}}
**B.II** _active_ _intransitive_ in same sense, οὐδέ πῃ εἶχον.. στηρίξαι ποσὶν ἔμπεδον.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.434}}; κῦμ᾽ οὐρανῷ στηρίζον a wave **rising up** to heaven, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1207: _metaphorically_, οὐρανῷ στηρίζον.. κλέος [prev. author] “Ba.” 972}}; πρὸς οὐρανὸν καὶ γαῖαν ἐστήριξε φῶς{{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. work] 1083, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sulla” 6.}}
**B.II.2** of diseases, **fix, settle, determine** to a particular part, ὁπότε εἰς τὴν καρδίαν στηρίξειεν (i.e. ἡ νόσος) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.49; ἐνταῦθα σ. ἡ νοῦσος 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 4.33; εἰ.. ἐς τὸ ὀστέον στηρίξειε τὸ βέλος [prev. author] “VC” 12; compare στήριξις 2.}}
**B.II.3** of planetary phases, **pause, stand still**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Geminus Astronomicus 12.23, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.76d, Theo Sm.p.147 H., 2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 75, 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 183.1, 4th c.AD: Paulus Alexandrinus Astrologus “G.” 2.}}
**B.II.4** _metaphorically_, ἐπὶ δόγματος σ. **hold fast** to an opinion, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 2.136.}}

4742:
** στῐγ-μα**, ατος, τό,
**tattoo-mark**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.35, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 585b33, “GA” 721b32, “IG” 42(1).121.48, +others (Epid., 4th c.BC), 2nd c.AD: Polyaenus Historicus 1.24}}; σ. ἱρά, showing that the persons so marked were devoted to the service of the temple, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.113}}; especially of a slave, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 187, 1st c.AD(?): Pseudo-Phocylidea 225, “Codex Theodosianus [5th c.AD]” 10. 22.4; or a soldier, [prev. passage], 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 8.12; στίγματα ἐξαίρει βατράχειον καταπλασθέν 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus “Eup.” 1.110: so _metaphorically_, σ. Ἰησοῦ NT.Gal.6.17 (_plural_)}}; ἀνωφελῆ σ., of inscribed laws, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 80.5.}}
**2** generally, **mark, spot**, as on the dragon's skin, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 166, compare 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 8.2.7, 8.4.7.}}
**3** **stud**, {{LXX|LXX.Cant.1.11.}}
**4** σ. χρυσοῦν **colour** of gold, Ps.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus cited in Zos.Alch. p.119 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus, comparep.126 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}
**5**= **cicatricis signum,** {{Refs|“Corpus Glossariorum Latinorum”}}

4743:
** στιγ-μή**, ἡ,
**spot** on a bird's plumage, Alex.Mynd. cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 9.398d (_plural_)}}; **brand-mark**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 34 / 5.2.1 (_plural_).}}
**2** **mathematical point**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 108b26, “EN” 1174b12, “de An.” 427a10, +others, 2nd c.BC: Apollodorus Seleuciensis Stoicus 3.259}}; ὅσον σ. αἱματίνη 'a **speck** of blood', {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 561a11.}}
**3** _metaphorically_ of anything very small, **jot, tittle**, εἴ γ᾽ εἶχε στιγμὴν ἢ σκιὰν τούτων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.115, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 1067}}; of time, {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 196, LXX.Isa.29.5}}; ἐν σ. χρόνου in a **moment,** {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Luke.4.5; σ. χρόνου ὁ βίος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.13a, compare “Anthologia Graeca” 7.472 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus)}}; ἐν σ. without χρόνου, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 131.4}}; στιγμῇ καιρο,= **puncto temporis,** {{Refs|“Corpus Glossariorum Latinorum”}}
**II** Grammars, ς. or τελεία σ. **full stop, period**, μέση σ. **colon**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 630.6, compare ὑποστιγμή: Nicanor made 8 στιγμαί, Scholia ib [prev. author] p.24 H., compare 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}} see at {Νικάνω; σ. πᾶσα σημεῖον αὐτοτελείας} {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 182.17, compare “Pron.” 53.16, +others}}

4744:
** στίλβ-ω**,
chiefly _present_ and _imperfect_: _aorist_ ἔστιλψα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Charito Scriptor Eroticus 2.2, 5th c.AD: Aristaenetus Rhetor 1.25}}:—**glitter, gleam**, of polished or bright surfaces, χιτῶνας.. ἦκα στίλβοντας ἐλαίῳ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.596; κάλλεΐ τε στίλβων καὶ εἵμασιν 3.392; κάλλεϊ καὶ χάρισι στίλβων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.237}}; λαμπραὶ δ᾽ ἀκτῖνες ἀπ᾽ αὐτοῦ αἰγλῆεν στίλβουσι **beam** from him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Hymni Homerici” 31.11; ὀμμάτων στίλβειν ἄτο.. φλόγα 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 17.55}}; σ. ὅπλοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1146}}; ἰδὼν στίλβοντα τὰ λάβδα, i.e. the λ upon the Spartan shields, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 359; σ. νῶτον πτερύγοιν χρυσαῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 697; σ. ἄνθει.. ἐπωμίδας 5th c.BC: Achaeus Tragicus 4.3; σ. ἐν χρωμάτων ποικιλίᾳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 110d, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Sensu” 77; ἱμάτια στίλβοντα NT.Mark.9.3: _absolutely_, of gold, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 59b}}; of sleek horses, σ. ὥστε κύκνου πτερόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 618}}; of brilliant complexion, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.79, etc.}}; of water in motion, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 370a18}}; of the white **gleam** on the eye, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “HA” 561a32, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.610; ὁρᾶν τῷ στίλβοντι 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Sensu” 26}}; of fixed stars, opposed to planets (exc. Mercury, see at {στίλβων}), **twinkle**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Posteriora” 78a30, “Cael.” 290a18: c._accusative_ _cognate_, σ. ἀστραπάς}} **flash** lightning, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 480: _metaphorically_, σ. ὁμηλικίην ἐρατεινήν 5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Argonautica” 1115.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **shine, be bright**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 194 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**II** _transitive_,= στιλπνόω, στίλβει πρόσωπον [{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.84 (variant for{στιλβοῖ}); στίλψασα τὰς παρειὰς ἐντρίμματι 5th c.AD: Aristaenetus Rhetor [same place]}}]

4745:
** στοά**, ᾶς, ἡ,
{{Refs 6th c.BC+|“SIG” 29 (Attic, found at Delphi, 6th-5th c.BC), +others, “IG” 12.115.7, +others}}; also **στοιά** (in _anapaestic_ verse) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 676, 684, 686}}, and in some dialect Inscrr., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 12(3).170.22 (from Astypalaea), 42(1).115.20 (probably, Epid., 4th-3rd c.BC), Ρχ. Ἐφ.1913.227 (from Lesbos)}}; **στωϊά** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Inscription Magn.” 67.6 (Cnossian decree, 3rd-2nd c.BC), “IG” 12(2).14.2, +others (Mytil.)}}:— **roofed colonnade, cloister**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.52, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.90, 8.90, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.29, 7.4.31, NT.John.10.23, “CPHerm.” 94.3 (3rd c.AD), etc.}}
**II** at Athens,
**II.1** **storehouse, magazine**, especially for corn, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 548; στοιὰ ἀλφιτόπωλις [prev. author] “Ec.” 686, compare 14.}}
**II.2** ἡ στοιὰ ἡ βασίλειος the **court** where the βασιλεύς sat, {{Refs|[prev. work] 684}}; ἡ τοῦ βασιλέως σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 210d, compare “Euthphr.” 2a, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 1.3.1, 1.14.6, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 8.86}}; σ. βασιλική at Thera, {{Refs|“IG” 12(3).326.19.}}
**II.3** ἡ στοὰ ἡ ποικίλη, see at {ποικίλος} 11.3: also ἡ σ. alone, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.85}}; so οἱ ἀπὸ τῆς σ., of the Stoics (since Zeno taught there), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Placita Philosophorum” 1.5.1, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 9.11, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 10.15, etc.}}; οἱ ἐκ τῆς σ. {{Refs|[prev. author] 18(1).259: also ἡ Σ}}. alone, the **Stoic school**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.68S.}}
**II.4** of other porticoes, ἡ σ. ἡ τῶν Ἑρμῶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.183}}; ἡ μακρὰ σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 34.37}}; ἡ τοῦ Διὸς τοῦ Ἐλευθερίου σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theages” 121a.}}
**III** **long roof** or **shed** used in sieges, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 569.36 (Halasarna, 3rd c.BC), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.48.2}}:—**gallery, communication trench**, whether above ground or excavated, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 83.32, 85.10, 91.31.}}

4746:
** στῐβάς**, άδος, ἡ,
(στείβω) **bed of straw, rushes**, or **leaves**, whether strewn loose {{NT|compare (NT.Mark.11.8)}}, or stuffed into a mattress, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 798; χαμαιπετής [prev. author] “Tr.” 507; σχοίνων 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 541; ἐπὶ στιβάδων ἐστρωμένων μίλακι καὶ μυρρίναις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 372b; χἁ σ. ἐσσεῖται πεπυκασμένα.. κνύζᾳ τ᾽ ἀσφοδέλῳ τε 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.67, compare 13.34.}}
**b** **straw strewn at a sacrifice**, hence as name of the ceremony, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“IG” 22.1368.48, +others (2nd c.AD).}}
**2** **mattress**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.71, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 663; ἐπὶ στιβάδος κατακείμενος 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 207}}; especially one used by soldiers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 254, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 348, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.1.16, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.48.4.}}
**3** generally, **bed**, {{Refs|Theopomp. Hist. 166.}}
**4** **nest** or **lair** of mice, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 1140}}; of the fish φυκίς, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 607b21.}}
**5** **grave**, {{Refs|“BCH” 13.37 (from Iasus), 22.373 (from Caria), “Ath.Mitt.” 15.277 ([prev. passage]).}}

4747:
** στοιχεῖον**, τό:
**I** in a form of sun-dial, **the shadow of the gnomon, the length of which in feet indicated the time of day**, ὅταν ᾖ δεκάπουν τὸ σ. when the **shadow** is ten feet long, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 652}}, see {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Scholia; ὁπηνίκ᾽ ἂν εἴκοσι ποδῶν.. τὸ σ. ᾖ 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 119.7, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 83.}}
**II** **element**,
**II.1** a **simple sound** of speech, as the **first component** of the syllable, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 424d}}; τὸ ῥῶ τὸ σ{{Refs 4th c.BC+|. [prev. work] 426d; γραμμάτων σ. καὶ συλλαβάς [prev. author] “Tht.” 202e; σ. ἐστι φωνὴ ἀδιαίρετος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1456b22; φωνῆς σ. καὶ ἀρχαὶ δοκοῦσιν εἶναι ταῦτ᾽ ἐξ ὧν σύγκεινται αἱ φωναὶ πρώτων [prev. author] “Metaphorical” 998a23, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.6:}} —στοιχεῖα therefore, strictly, were different from **letters** (γράμματα), {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Diogenes Babylonius Stoicus 3.213, Scholia 2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus p.32, +others}}, but are frequently not clearly distinguished from them, as by {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Tht.” [same place], “Cra.” 426d; τὰ σ. τῶν γραμμάτων τὰ τέτταρα καὶ εἴκοσι 4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 31.21}}; σ. ε ¯ **letter** ε (in a filing-system), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 959.2 (2nd c.AD)}}; ἀκουόμενα σ. **letters** which are pronounced, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 165.17}}; γράμματα and ς. are expressly identified by {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 630.32}}; the ς. and its name are confused by {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 29.1, but distinguished by Hdn.Gr. cited in 4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus “Scholia in Theodosii Canones” 1.340, Scholia 2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus [same place]:}} — κατὰ στοιχεῖον **in the order of the letters, alphabetically,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|Anthologia Graeca 11.15 (2nd c.AD: Ammianus Epigrammaticus); uncertain meaning in 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.422e.}}
**II.2** in Physics, στοιχεῖα were the **components into which matter is ultimately divisible, elements**, reduced to four by Empedocles, who called them ῥιζὤματα, the word στοιχεῖα being first used (_accusative_ to {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eudemus Philosophus cited in 6th c.AD: Simplicius Philosophus “in Aristotelis Physica commentaria - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 7.13) by 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus}}, τὰ πρῶτα οἱονπερεὶ σ, ἐξ ὧν ἡμεῖς τε συγκείμεθα καὶ τἄλλα {{Refs|“Tht.” 201e}}; τὰ τῶν πάντων σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Plt.” 278d; αὐτὰ τιθέμενοι σ. τοῦ παντός “Ti.” 48b, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione et Corruptione” 314a29, “Metaphorical” 998a28, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Sensu” 3, +others, 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 3.24; σ. σωματικά 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 338a22, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 46}}; ἄτομα σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2p.36U.}}; equivalent to ἀρχαί, Thales cited in {{Refs 6th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.875c, 6th c.BC: Anaximander Philosophus cited in 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 2.1, Anonymus cited in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 188b28, “Metaphorical” 1059b23, +others; but 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus}} also distinguishes ς. from ἀρχή as less comprehensive, {{Refs|[prev. work]1070b23}}; τὰ σ. ὕλη τῆς οὐσίας {{Refs|[prev. work]1088b27}}; τρία τὰ σ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 189b16}}; distinguished from ἀρχή on other grounds by {{Refs|“Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 2.111}}; ς. used in three senses by Chrysipp., {{Refs|[prev. work]136, cf. Zeno [prev. work]1.24, +others}}; in Medicine, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.3, 420, +others, 15.7, +others; Αἰθέρ, κόσμου σ. ἄριστον 5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Hymni” 5.4}}; ἀνηλεὲς σ., of the sea, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 71.4}}; τὸ σ., of the sea, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Polemo Sophista “Cynegirus” 44}}; ἄμφω τὰ σ., i.e. land and sea,{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 11, compare 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 3.1.5, 4th c.AD: Himerius Sophista “Eclogae” 2.18.}}
**II.3** the **elements of proof**, e.g. in general reasoning the πρῶτοι συλλογισμοί, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1014b1}}; in Geometry, the propositions whose proof is involved in the proof of other propositions,{{Refs|[prev. work] 998a26, 1014a36}}; title of geometrical works by Hippocrates of Chios, Leon, Theudios, and Euclid, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in primum Euclidis librum commentarius” pp.66,67, 68F.}}: hence applied to whatever is one, small, and capable of many uses, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1014b3}}; to whatever is most universal, e.g. the unit and the point,{{Refs|[prev. work] 6}}; the line and the circle, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Top.” 158b35; the τόπος (argument applicable to a variety of subjects), [prev. work] 120b13, +others, “Rh.” 1358a35, +others; στοιχεῖα τὰ γένη λέγουσί τινες [prev. author] “Metaphorical” 1014b10}}; τὸ νόμισμα σ. καὶ πέρας τῆς ἀλλαγῆς coin is the **unit.. **of exchange, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pol.” 1257b23}}; in Grammar, σ. τῆς λέξεως **parts** of speech, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 2}}; but also, the **letters** composing a word, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 313.7}}; **letters** of the alphabet, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Diogenes Cynicus Philosophus Bab. “Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 3.213}}; σ. τοῦ λόγου the **elements** of speech, _i.e. _ **words**, or the **kinds of words, parts** of speech, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus cited in 6th c.AD: Simplicius Philosophus “in Aristotelis Categorias commentarium - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 10.24, 3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.45, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 7.1, 313.6.}}
**II.4** generally, **elementary** or **fundamental principle**, ἀρξάμενοι ἀπὸ τῶν σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.1; σ. χρηστῆς πολιτείας 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.16}}; τὸ πολλάκις εἰρημένον μέγιστον σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1309b16; σ. τῆς ὅλης τέχνης 4th c.BC(?): Nicolaus Comicus 1.30, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.10U., “Ep.” 3p.59U., 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.127S., 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.306.}}
**II.5** ἄστρων στοιχεῖα the **stars**, {{NT+4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 4.624; σ. καυσούμενα λυθήσεται NT.2Pet.3.10, compare 12}}; especially **planets**, στοιχείῳ Διός {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PLond.” 1.130.60 (1st-2nd c.AD)}}; so perhaps in {{NT|NT.Gal.4.3, NT.Col.2.8}}; especially a **sign of the Zodiac**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 6.102}}; of the Great Bear, {{Refs|“PMag.Par.” 1.1303.}}
**II.6** σ. ={ἀριθμός}, as _etymology_ of Στοιχαδεύς, Scholia 2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus p.192 H.

4748:
** στοιχ-έω**,
**to be drawn up in a line** or **row**, οὐδ᾽ ἐγκαταλείψω τὸν παραστάτην, ὅτῳ ἂν στοιχήσω **beside** whom **I stand** in battle,—from the oath of Athenian citizens, cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.1.48, compare 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 8.105}}; **move in line**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.3.34, “Eq.Mag.” 5.7}}; **to be in rows**, of leaves or joints, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.18.5, 3.5.3}}; κατὰ τὸ στοιχοῦν **in sequence**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Interpretatione” 19b24.}}
**2** **correspond**, ὅπως ἀεὶ ἡ ἡμέρα στοιχῇ καθ᾽ ἑκάστην πόλιν {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“OGI” 458.52 (1st c.BC).}}
**3** **to be satisfactory** to one, στοιχεῖ μοι πάντα τὰ προγεγραμμένα {{Refs 5th c.AD+|“BGU” 317.14 (6th c.AD),compare “Sammelb.” 6258 (5th-6th c.AD), etc.}}
**II** with _dative_, **fit**, [καταστρωτῆρα] στοιχοῦντα τοῖς κειμένοις {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IG” 7.3073.153 (Lebad., 2nd c.BC)}}: _metaphorically_, **to be in line with, walk by, agree with, submit to**, τῇ τῆς συγκλήτου προθέσει {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 28.5.6; διὰ τῶν ἔργων στοιχεῖν αὐτοσαυτῷ “SIG” 734.6 (Delph., 1st c.BC)}}; τῇ πρός τινα εὐνοίᾳ {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BCH” 55.44 (Odessus, 1st c.BC)}}; ταῖς πλείοσι γνώμαις {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.65; τῷ νομίσματι 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 1.178}}; τοῖς προειρημένοις φιλοσόφοις{{NT|[prev. work] 11.59; Πνεύματι NT.Gal.5.25, cf. NT.Phil.3.16; τοῖς ἴχνεσι τῆς πίστεως NT.Rom.4.12}}; στοίχεις (_Aeolic dialect_ _present_ _participle_) τοῖς προϋπαργμένοισι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“IGRom.” 4.1302 (Cyme, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD)}}; ἠθέλησεν στοιχοῦσαν τοῖς προπεπραγμένοις παρέχεσθαι τοῖς πολίταις τὴν αὑτοῦ διάληψιν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“OGI” 764.45 (Pergam., 2nd c.BC)}}; μιᾷ σ. **to be contented** with one wife, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 773; στοιχῶν πᾶσιν ὑπέγραψα “CPR” 30 ii 41 (6th c.AD): _absolutely_, στοιχεῖν βουλόμενος καὶ τοῖς ἐκείνων ἴχνεσιν ἐπιβαίνειν “SIG” 708.5 (Istropolis, 2nd c.BC)}}; στοιχεῖς τὸν νόμον φυλάσσων observest it **regularly,** {{NT|NT.Acts.21.24.}}

4749:
** στολ-ή**, ἡ,
_Aeolic dialect_ **σπολά** (which see): (στέλλω):— **equipment, fitting out**, στρατοῦ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 764.}}
**2** **armament**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pers.” 1018 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II** **equipment in clothes, raiment**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 192}}; σχῆμα Ἑλλάδος σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 224, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 130; ἱππάδα στολὴν ἐνεσταλμένοι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.80; σ. ἱππική 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 846; Σκυθική 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.78; Θρῃκία 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 313; Μηδική 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.1.40; γυναικεία 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 851, compare 92; τοξική 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 833b; στολὴν ἔχειν ἢν ἂμ βούληται “SIG” 1003.14 (Priene, 2nd c.BC)}}: _metaphorically_ of birds, σ. πτερῶν {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Achilles Tatius Astronomus 1.15.}}
**II.2** **garment, robe**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1357, 1597, “PCair.Zen.” 54.32 (_plural_), 263.4, 8 (3rd c.BC), “BGU” 1860.4 (1st c.BC), etc.}}; σ. θηρός, of the lion's skin which Heracles wore, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 465}}; ἐν σ. περιπατεῖν in **full dress**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 1.7}} (variant{-λίῳ} cited in {{Refs|10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus), cf. NT.Mark.12.38.}}
**II.3** **act of dressing**, μετὰ τὴν σ. {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus “Synopsis” 5.21.}}
**III** (στέλλω IV) **check to motion, pressure**, τοῦ ἀέρος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 11.11, compare 14.4.}}
**III.2** **reduction, diminution**, τῶν σιτίων {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Herodotus Medicus cited in 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 5.129.}}

4750:
** στόμα**, τό,
_Aeolic dialect_ **στύμα** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 29.25}}; _genitive_ στόματος, but στομάτοιο {{Refs|“Hymni Magici” 2(2).10,28}}:—**mouth**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.467, etc.; σύν τε στόμ᾽ ἐρεῖσαι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.426}}; ἱμείρων γλυκεροῦ σ. {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 25}}; of animals, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 146, 389, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1156 (Lyric poetry), etc.:}} —_plural_ is sometimes used for _sing._, ἀμφιπίπτων στόμασιν, of kissing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Tr.” 938, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 403 (Lyric poetry)}}, and frequently in later Poets, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.1607, 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 210, 240, etc.: _metaphorically_, πτολέμοιο, ὑσμίνης στόμα}}, **the very jaws** of the battle, as of a devouring monster, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.8, 20.359 (but compare below 111.1).}}
**2** especially **the mouth as the organ of speech**, δέκα μὲν γλῶσσαι, δέκα δὲ στόματ᾽ {{Refs 6th c.BC+|2.489, compare 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 18; βραχύ μοι σ. πάντ᾽ ἀνᾱγήσασθαι 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.19}}; frequently in Trag., σ. τὸ Δῖον the **mouth** of Zeus, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1032}}; τὸ Φοίβου θεῖον ἀψευδὲς σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “fragment” 350.5, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 603; τοῦ στόματος τὸ στρογγύλον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 471}}; Μοισᾶν καπυρὸν σ. their **mouthpiece, organ**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.37, compare 2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus 3.72}}; Πιερίδων τὸ σοφὸν σ., of Homer, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 7.4 (Paul. Sil.), compare 7.6 (Antip. Sid.), 7.75 (Antip.), 9.184; τὸ μισόχρηστον σ. τῆς κωμῳδίας 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Pietate - Herkulanische Studien” p.93G.}}; **speech, utterance**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 426, 706, “OC” 132 (Lyric poetry), etc.; εἰς τόδ᾽ ἐξελθόντος ἀνόσιον σ. [prev. work] 981}}; κἂν καλὸν φορῇ σ. {{NT|[prev. author] “fragment” 930; τὸ σὸν.. σ. ἐλεινόν [prev. author] “OT” 671; διδόναι σ. καὶ σοφίαν NT.Luke.21.15}}: in _plural_ of a single speaker, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1220 (Lyric poetry):—special phrases: οἴγειν σ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 611}}; τοὐμὸν οὐ λύω σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1060, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.96}}; διᾶραι τὸ σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.112}}; κοίμησον σ. keep silence, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1247}}; δάκνειν σ., i.e. to keep a stern silence (compare ὀδάξ), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “fragment” 397; ἴσχε δακὼν σ. σόν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 977 (_anapaest meter_)}}; ὀδόντι πρῖε τὸ σ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “fragment” 897}}; so κλῄσας σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 865}}; οὐκ ἐφέξετε σ.; {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hec.” 1283}}; σῖγ᾽ ἕξομεν σ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hipp.” 660}}; εὖ ἔχειν σ.,= εὐφημεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 381}}; συγκλῄειν σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 40 (_anapaest meter_):—of style, τὸ Λυσιακὸν σ. 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Lysia” 12.}}
**3** with Preps.,
**3.a** ἀνὰ στόμα ἔχειν have always in **one's mouth**, whether for good or ill, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 80; ἀνὰ σ. καὶ διὰ γλώσσης ἔχειν [prev. author] “Andr.” 95.}}
**3.b** ἀπὸ στόματος εἰπεῖν speak from memory (compare ἀπὸ γλώσσης), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 142d, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.6.9, 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 48, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Solon” 8, etc.}}
**3.c** διὰ στόμα λέγειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 579, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 103 (so κατὰ τὸ σ. ᾄδειν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 158); διὰ στόμα ἔχειν [prev. author] “Lys.” 855; οἶκτος οὔτις ἦν διὰ στόμα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 51}}; πᾶσι διὰ στόματος 'tis the common **talk**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 12.21.}}
**3.d** ἐν στόμασι εἶχον {{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.157, 6.136; πολλῶν κείμενος ἐν στόμασιν 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 240; ἐν τῷ σ. λέγειν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 198.}}
**3.e** ἐξ ἑνὸς σ. with one **voice**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Eq.” 670. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 364a, “PGiss.” 36.13 (2nd c.BC), 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.763}}; so ὡς ἀφ᾽ ἑνὸς σ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.159 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus).}}
**3.f** ἐπὶ στόμα **on one's face, face-foremost**, ἐξεκυλίσθη πρηνὴς.. ἐπὶ σ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.43, compare 16.410; ὡς κύων ἐπὶ σ. κείμενος 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus “Supp.” 2.9}}; ὗς ἔκειτ᾽ ἐπὶ σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 21}}; ἐπὶ σ. κεῖται lies **prone**, of the right ventricle, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ καρδίης” 4}}; ἐπὶ σ.,= **pronus,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Corpus Glossariorum Latinorum”; ἐπὶ σ. πεσόντα 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Artoxerxes” 29; ἐπὶ σ. φερόμενον ἐν πᾶσι 4th-3rd c.BC: Timaeus Historicus cited in 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 12.8.4}}; also ὅ τι νῦν ἦλθ᾽ ἐπὶ σ. whatever came uppermost, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 351}}; ἐπὶ στόματος Φαραώ **by the command** of P., {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.23.35.}}
**3.g** κατὰ στόμα **face to face**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.11, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 801, “Rh.” 409, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.2.26}}; οἱ κατὰ σ. θεοί (compare ἀντήλιοι) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 781.33}}; κατὰ σ. τινός **confronted** with him, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 855d; στόμα κατὰ στόμα λαλήσω αὐτῷ LXX.Num.12.8; στόμα πρὸς στόμα NT.2John.12, NT.3John.14, “PMag.Berol.” 1.39.}}
**II** **mouth** of a river, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.24, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.441, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 847, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.17, etc.}}; so ἠϊόνος σ. μακρόν the wide **mouth** of the bay, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.36, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.90; σ. τοῦ Πόντου 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.75}}; κόλπου{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 49; τὸ σ. τῆς ἐσβολῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 1107}}; τὸ ἄνω σ. [τῆς διώρυχος] the **width** of the trench at top, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.23 (but τὰ σ. τ. δ}}. **mouths**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 37).}}
**II.2** **any outlet** or **entrance**, ἀργαλέον σ. λαύρης {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.137; σ. τῆς ἀγυιᾶς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.4; σ. φρέατος [prev. author] “An.” 4.5.25; καδίσκου 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 581, compare “Anthologia Graeca” 6.251 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus)}}; χθόνιον Ἄιδα σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.44}}; τὰ τῶν διεξόδων σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 251d}}; ἑπτάπυλον σ. the seven gates of Thebes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 119 (Lyric poetry): in Medicine texts, τῶν μητρέων, τῶν ὑστερέων}},= **os uteri** (not distinguished from the **cervix**), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.36, “Aph.” 5.46; τῆς κοιλίας 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Posteriora” 94b15, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.50; γαστρός 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 20, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 5.274}}; [ἕλκους] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 863a11.}}
**III** **foremost part, face, front**:
**III.1** of weapons, **point**, κατὰ στόμα εἱμένα χαλκῷ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.389; [ὁ κριὸς] ἔχει σ. σιδηροῦν 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus Mech. 24.2; τὸ σ. τῆς αἰχμῆς 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Heroicus” 19.4}}; **edge** of a sword, μαχαίρας {{NT+5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 3.5, NT.Luke.21.24, etc.: _metaphorically_, ἐθηλύνθην σ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 651.}}
**III.1.b** the **front ranks** of the battle, the **front**, ἀπὸ στόματος (opposed to ἀπὸ τῆς οὐρᾶς) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.4.42, compare “HG” 4.3.4; τὸ σ. τοῦ πλαισίου [prev. author] “An.” 3.4.43, compare 5.4.22, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.12.7 (so perhaps σ. πολέμοιο, ὑσμίνης in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, see above 1.1).}}
**III.1.c** τὸ τῶν λοχαγῶν τάγμα σ. καλεῖται {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 2.5.}}
**III.2** ἄκρον σ. πύργων the **edge** or **top** of the towers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1166}}; πρὸς τῷ σ. τοῦ βίου at the very **verge** of life, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 11.15.}}
**III.3** ={ὀμφαλός}{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|111.3, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Tactica - Griechische Kriegsschriftsteller” 7.3}}. (Cf. Avest. **staman-**, m. 'mouth (of dog)', Welsh **safn** 'mouth'.)

4751:
** στόμᾰχ-ος**, ὁ,
(στόμα) **throat, gullet**, ἀπὸ στομάχους ἀρνῶν τάμε νηλέϊ χαλκῷ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.292, compare 19.266; κατὰ στομάχοιο θέμεθλα νύξε 17.47}}; ={οἰσοφάγος}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 495b19f, 493a8, 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 22.}}
**2** **neck of the bladder**, τῆς κύστιος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus}} “Aër.” 9; or **of the uterus**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Mul.” 1.18, “Steril.” 217; τοῦ αἰδοίου [prev. author] “Mul.” 1.36.}}
**3** later, **orifice of the stomach**,= στόμα τῆς γαστρός, τῆς κοιλίας, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.687d, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.431, 7.127}}; the **stomach** itself, {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.1Tim.5.23, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.6, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.698b, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.15, +others, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.227, 15.460, 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 10.31, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.79f}}; ἀμφοτέρας (i.e. τὰς χεῖρας) ἐπὶ τοῦ σ. {{Refs|“PMag.Leid.W.” 18.36}}; cf. Latin _stomachus._
**4** **anger**, γέγονε σ. πρὸς δουλικὸν πρόσωπον {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 216.3; ἵνα μὴ ἔχωμεν στομάχους μηδὲ φθόνον “POxy.” 533.14 (2nd-3rd c.AD).}}

4752:
** στρᾰτεία**,
_Ionic dialect_ -ηΐη, ἡ, (στρατεύω) **expedition, campaign**, στρατηΐην ποιεῖσθαι ἐς.., ἐπὶ.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.71, 171, etc.; πολλὰς σ. ἐποιήσαντο 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.11; σ. ἐστράτευσ᾽ ὀλεθρίαν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 116; σ. ἡμῖν εἰς Ποτείδαιαν ἐγένετο 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 219e, compare “IG” 12(2).645.15 (from Nesus), etc.}}; ἀπὸ στρατείας coming from **war**, after **service done**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 603, “Eu.” 631}}; κατὰ τὴν Σιτάλκου σ. about the time of his **expedition**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.101; εἰς δὲ σ. πάντας Ἀργείους ἄγων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 229}}; ἐπὶ στρατείας εἶναι to be on **foreign service**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 220c (codices, στρατιᾶς Cobet, Burnet)}}; so ἐν στρατείᾳ ὄντας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.2.19}}; ἐν τῇ σ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PEnteux.” 48.3 (3rd c.BC)}}; παραγγέλλειν τινὶ σ. κατὰ γῆν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.1.13; ἐκδήμους σ. οὐκ ἐξῇσαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.15}}; στρατείαν ξυνεξελθεῖν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 3; σ. δ᾽ οὐ φέρει περιουσίαν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 382, compare “OGI” 5.44 (Scepsis, 4th c.BC)}}; τῆς σ. γιγνομένης ἐκ καταλόγου {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 26.1}}; frequently in _plural_, **military service, warfare**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 404a}}; πρὸς ταῖς αὑτοῦ σ. in addition to the **campaigns** which he is bound to serve, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Lg.” 878d; ἐν ταῖς σ. μισθοφορεῖν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 27.2; ἀπὸ σ. ἱππικῶν “IGRom.” 3.58 (from Bithynia)}}; στρατείας στρατεύεσθαι {{Refs|“IG” 22.505.54}}; ἀφειμένος στρατείας, = Latin _exauctoratus_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.274a.}}
**2** σ. ἐν τοῖς ἐπωνύμοις **levy of those liable to serve** in the year of such and such archons, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Harpocratio Grammaticus see entry , compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 53.7.}}
**3** σ. ἡ ἐν τοῖς μέρεσιν **expedition** for special service, to train the young soldiers next after serving as περίπολοι, [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.168, compare 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus see at {τερθρεία}.}}]
**4** **military discipline**, ἡ ἀκριβὴς σ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 78.36.}}
**5** **military appointment**, ἐπώλησε στρατείας {{Refs|[prev. author] 72.12.}} —στρατιά is a constant variant, and is sometimes undoubtedly used= στρατεία (**campaign**), see at {στρατιά} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|11 and cf. Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 835 (= 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 369)}}; but στρατεία= **army, expeditionary force** is very rare, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 495 (restored in “Rh.” 263 (Lyric poetry)): in Inscrr. στρατεία}} never = **army**, but both -εία {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|(“IG” 22.1132.14, “SIG” 398.2 (Cos, 3rd c.BC), +others) and -ιά (which see)}} = **campaign.**

4753:
** στράτ-ευμα** [ᾰ], ατος, τό,
**expedition, campaign**, ἐφ᾽ Ἑλλάδα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 758 (troch.); τὸ σ. τὸ ἐπὶ Σάμον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.49}}; διέφυγον τὸ σ. escaped the threatened **invasion**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 8.112, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1133.}}
**II** **armament, army, host**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.48}}; ὑγιαίνω.. μετὰ τοῦ σ. {{LXX+1st c.BC+|“OGI” 453.10 (Epist. Antonii, 1st c.BC),cf. LXX.1Mac.9.34, +others}}; ὑπὲρ τιμῆς ἐλαίου τῶν ἐνταῦθα σ. {{NT+2nd c.AD+|“Ostr.” 1595 (3rd c.AD),cf. NT.Luke.23.11, “BGU” 1564.5 (2nd c.AD)}}; πεζὸν σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 469}}; διαπόντιον σ., i.e. composed of Asiatic mercenaries, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hermippus Comicus 58; ἱππικόν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.26}}; πολιτικόν {{Refs|Id “HG” 5.4.41}}; ἱερὰ σ. {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“SIG” 880.7 (Pizus, 3rd c.AD)}}: also, a **naval armament**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.74; τὸ ναυτικὸν σ. Ἀχαιῶν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 59.}}
**II.2** ={στρατός} {{Refs|2}}**the people**, σ. Παλλάδος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 601 (Lyric poetry); φῦλα τρία τριῶν στρατευμάτων uncertain reading in 653.}}

4754:
** στρατ-εύω**,
(στρατός) **advance with an army** or **fleet, wage war**, or rulers, offcers, or men, Κροῖσος ἐνένωτο -εύειν ἐπὶ τοὺς Πέρσας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.77; Θηβαῖοι.. ἐστράτευον ἐπὶ τοὺς Πλαταιέας [prev. author] 6.108, compare 7 (variant), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.7, “OGI” 327.2 (Pergam., 2nd c.BC), etc.}}; οἱ Ἀθηναῖοι -εύσαντες ἐς Πλάταιαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.6; Καρχηδόνιοι -εύσαντες ἐπὶ Σικελίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.1.37; εἰς Σικελίαν -εύσαντες [prev. work] 1.5.21}}; ἐστράτευσαν πρὸς Ἄβυδον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1.2.16; σ. ὅποι Κῦρος ἐπαγγέλλοι [prev. author] “Cyr.” 7.4.9: with _accusative_ _cognate_, οἶσθ᾽ ἣν στρατείαν ἐστράτευσ᾽ ὀλεθρίαν (i.e. ἐγὼ Ἄδραστος) 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 116; Λακεδαιμόνιοι.. τὸν ἱερὸν καλούμενον πόλεμον ἐστράτευσαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.112}}; _metaphorically_, ἑνὸς δ᾽ ἐπ᾽ ἀνδρὸς δώματα στρατεύομεν (Iris et Lyssa loq.) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 825 (nisi to be read σῶμα συστρατεύομεν)}}:—so in _middle_, στρατεύομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.61, etc.:}}_future_ -εύσομαι{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 11, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.23}}: _aorist_ ἐστρατευσάμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.204, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1111, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.144, etc.; also ἐστρατεύθην 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.51, 2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus 1.9.13}}: _perfect_ ἐστράτευμαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 4.29, etc.}}; _Boeotian dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ _perfect_ _middle_ ἐστροτεύαθη {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 7.3174.27 (Orchom. _Boeotian dialect_), +others: εἰ μὴ στρατεύοισθ᾽ ἐς τὸν Ἑλλήνων τόπον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 790}}; -εύσονται ἐπὶ τὴν ἡμετέρην [Ἀθηναῖοι] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.11; οἱ δὲ -ευόμενοι οἵδε ἦσαν, Πέρσαι μέν.. [prev. work] 61, compare 64, 66, +others}}; ἐστρατευμένοι γάρ εἰσι **they have been soldiers, have seen war-service**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1113, compare “IG” 12.1.3, 18.9, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 9.4; ψιλὸς αὖ στρατεύσομαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 232, compare 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 117.8}}; ὁπλίτης σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.4.1; ἐκ καταλόγου σ. [prev. passage]; ὅταν ἡλικίαν ἐκπέμπωσι προγράφουσιν ἀπὸ τίνος ἄρχοντος καὶ ἐπωνύμου μέχρι τίνων δεῖ στρατεύεσθαι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 53.7; σφι ἐδόκεε -εύεσθαι ἐπὶ τὰς Θήβας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.86; ἐπὶ τοῦ κρυστάλλου -εύονται.. πέρην ἐς τοὺς Σίνδους [prev. author] 4.28; σ. μετά τινων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 967; ὑπὲρ τῆς πόλεως 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 429b; τῆς σῆς οὕνεκ᾽.. γυναικός 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1111; ὑπό τινι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Camillus” 2; ἐπ᾽ Αἴγυπτον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.139; ἐς τὴν Ἀσίην [prev. author] 1.4, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.30, etc.; κατὰ Ἐφεσίων “OGI” 437.70 (Pergam., 1st c.BC)}}; πρὸς τὴν τῶν Ὀλυνθίων πόλιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.3.3}}; μισθοῦ σ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 3.2.7}}; πανδημεὶ ἔξω σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 814a}}; opposed to ἐπιδημεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 20.21}}; opposed to δημηγορεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.22}}; στρατευσάμενος,= **a militiis,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 14.716 (from Naples): with _accusative_ _cognate_, τὰς στρατείας -ευόμενος 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 10.25.}}
**2** _middle_, **serve in the army**, τυΐ πρᾶτον ἐστροτεύαθη the following **have joined the army** for the first time, {{Refs|“IG” 7 [same place]}}; μηδεὶς ἐαθῇ -εύσασθαι **to join the army**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 110.162 (2nd c.BC),compare “Sammelb.” 7354.6 (2nd c.AD), “BGU” 1680.9 (3rd c.AD)}}; οἱ -ευόμενοι Ἕλληνες the Greeks **who are in the army,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|PTeb. 5.168 (2nd c.BC).—In 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus codices vary between}}_active_ and _middle_, as in {{Refs|6.7, 108}}; in _Attic dialect_ and later Gr. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|(“PGrenf.” 1.21.3 (2nd c.BC), “PTeb.” 5.168 (2nd c.BC), etc.) the}}_middle_ is much the more frequently
**II** later, in _active_, **take** or **receive into the army, enroll, enlist**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 25.12, 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 1.42, 2.141, 5.137, 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 2.14.6}}:— _passive_, τῶν νεολέκτων τῶν -ευθέντων ὑφ᾽ ἡμῶν {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“POxy.” 1103.5 (4th c.AD)}}; ὁ νῦν -ευθεὶς τίρων {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PLond.” 2.237.31 (4th c.AD).}}
**III** see at {στραγγεύομαι}.

4755:
** στρᾰτηγ-ός**, ὁ
(the _feminine_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 491, 500 is merely comic), 4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus}} and _Doric dialect_ **στρᾰτᾱγός** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 5(2).6.9 (Tegea, 4th c.BC), “SIG” 597 B (Thermum, 3rd c.BC), etc.}}; _Aeolic dialect_ **στρότᾱγος** {{Refs|“IG” 12 (2).6.7 (Mytil.), 11(2).1064b27 (from Delos)}}:— **leader** or **commander of an army, general**, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 58.1, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 816, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 22.3, etc.}}; ἀνὴρ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1627, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Ion” 540d}}; opposed to ναύαρχος (admiral), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1232 (see. below 11.1).}}
**2** generally, **commander, governor**, πόλει κήρυγμα θεῖναι τὸν σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ant.” 8, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 398a29.}}
**3** with _genitive_, στρατηγοὶ τοῦ πεζοῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.83; τῶν παραθαλασσίων [prev. author] 5.25, etc.; Ἀχαιῶν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Aj.” [same place]; στρατεύματος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.7.12.}}
**4** _metaphorically_, παραλαβὼν.. οἶνον σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 18}}; στρατηγοὶ κυνηγεσίων **masters** of hounds, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 398a24}}; so **strategum te facio huic convivio**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Plautus Comicus Latinus “Stich.” 702.}}
**II** at Athens, the title of 10 **officers elected by yearly vote to command the army and navy, and conduct the war-department at home, commanders in chief and ministers of war**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.109, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.61, 4.2, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 26.1, 44.4, 61.1, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.25; οἱ σ. οἱ εἰς Σικελίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.11, compare “IG” 12.302.46, +others; σ. εἵλοντο δέκα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.5.16, compare 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 117.4, _plural_ Comedy texts 185, etc.; τῷ σ. τῷ ἐπὶ τὰς συμμορίας ᾑρημένῳ “IG” 22.1629.209}}; when distinguished from ναύαρχος and ἵππαρχος, the στρατηγός is **commander of the infantry**, Decrees cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.184, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 4.2}}; χειροτονηθεὶς σ. ἐπὶ τὸ ναυτικόν, ἐπὶ τὰ ὅπλα, {{Refs|“IG” 22.682.5,31; ἐπὶ τὴν παρασκευήν [prev. work]22; ἐπὶ τὴν χώραν [prev. work]24.}}
**II.2** also of **chief magistrates** of the cities of Asia Minor, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.38}}; of many other Greek states, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 5(2) [prev. cited] (Tegea, 4th c.BC), 12(9).191 A 44 (Eretria, 4th c.BC), “OGI” 329.42 (Aegina, 2nd c.BC), 4th-3rd c.BC: Timaeus Historicus 114, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.43.1, etc.}}
**II.3** in Ptolemaic and Roman Egypt, **military and civil governor of a nome,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|PEnteux. 1.12, +others (3rd c.BC), “PCair.Zen.” 351.4 (3rd c.BC), “BGU” 1730.11 (1st c.BC), “OGI” 184.3 (Philae, 1st c.BC), Wilcken “Chr.” 41 ii 6 (3rd c.AD), 43.1 (4th c.AD)}}; also in other parts of the Ptolemaic empire, e.g. at Calynda in Caria, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 341 (a). 20 (3rd c.BC)}}; in Cyprus, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“OGI” 84 (3rd c.BC); ὁ σ. τῆς Ἰνδικῆς καὶ Ἐρυθρᾶς θαλάσσης [prev. work] 186 (Philae, 1st c.BC); in the Attalid empire, [prev. work]267.13 (Pergam., 3rd c.BC), +others}}; σ. τῆς πόλεως at Alexandria, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 729.1 (2nd c.AD)}}; at Ptolemais, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“OGI” 743 = “Raccolta Lumbroso” 299 (1st c.BC), “Sammelb.” 7027 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II.4** σ. ὕπατος **consul,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 5(1).1165 (Gythium, 2nd c.BC), 9(2).338 (Cyretiae, 2nd c.BC), 42(1).306 D (Epid., 2nd c.BC), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.52.5}}; also ς. alone, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.7.12, +others, “SIG” 685.20 (Crete, 2nd c.BC), and ὕπατος}} alone, see at {ὕπατο; σ. ἀνθύπατος} **proconsul**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. work]826 I 1 (Delph., 2nd c.BC), 745.2 (Rhodes, 1st c.BC)}}; ἑξαπέλεκυς σ. **praetor**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.106.6}}; used of the **praetor urbanus**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] 33.1.5; called σ. κατὰ πόλιν “IG” 14.951 (1st c.BC), etc.}}; ς. alone, = **praetor**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 2.6, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.1.26}}: also of the **duumviri** or **chief magistrates of Roman colonies**, as of Philippi, {{NT|NT.Act.16.20}}: later of the **Comes Orientis**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 56.21.}}
**II.5** **an officer who had the custody of the Temple at Jerusalem**, ὁ σ. τοῦ ἱεροῦ {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Luke.22.52, NT.Act.4.1, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 6.5.3.}}
**II.6** νυκτερινὸς σ. **superintendent of police** at Alexandria, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 17.1.12.}}
**II.7** ={φαλαγγάρχης} (which see), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Tactica” 10.7, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Tactica - Griechische Kriegsschriftsteller” 9.8.}}

4756:
** στρᾰτιά**,
_Ionic dialect_ **στρατ-ιή**, ἡ,= στρατός, **army**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.16, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 534 (_anapaest meter_), “Ag.” 799 (_anapaest meter_), etc.}}; σ. ναυτική, πεζή, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.33, 7.15}}; ναύφρακτος σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.296.30; ἔς τε τὴν σ. καὶ τὴν πόλιν τὴν Ἀθηναίων [prev. work] 108.40; τῆς σ. κάκιστος ἦν ἀνήρ 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 31}}: _absolutely_, **a land force**, as distinguished from seamen, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.12.}}
**2** generally, **host, company, band**, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 11.50, “N.” 11.35; ἡ σ. τοῦ οὐρανοῦ LXX.2Ch.33.3}}; αἱ σ. τῶν οὐρανῶν{{LXX|LXX.Neh.9.6}}; = **cohors ministrorum**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 54.7.}}
**II** sometimes ={στρατεία}, **expedition**, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.77 (variant{-είην}), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 587 (Lyric poetry), “Th.” 828, 1169, “Lys.” 592, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.108, “IG” 22.351.31; ἐπὶ στρατιᾶς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 354, 557, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 260b, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.14}}]; ἴτε.. ἐπὶ στρατιάν go.. on **service**, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1143; κατὰ στρατιήν 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἰητροῦ” 14; πολιτικαὶ στρατιαί, ξενικαὶ σ., [prev. passage]; see at {στρατεία}.}}]

4757:
** στρᾰτῐώτ-ης**, ου, ὁ:
_vocative_ στρατιῶτα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 155}}: (στρατιά):— **soldier**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.134, +others, 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 143, “IG” 12.60.12, etc.; στρατιώτας καταλέγειν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1065}}; σ. μισθωσάμενος, of Pisistratus, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 15.2}}; ἄνδρες σ., in a speech, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.61}}; collectively, in _singular_, ὁ πολὺς ὅμιλος καὶ σ. {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.24}}; also of soldiers serving on ship-board, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.88.}}
**2** later, **professional soldier**,= μισθοφόρος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1116b15, compare 4th c.BC: Archestratus Epicus “fragment” 61}}; **soldier** in Ptolemaic and Roman Egypt, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PEnteux.” 54.8 (3rd c.BC), “OGI” 86.12 (3rd c.BC), “PLond.” 1.142.4 (1st c.AD), etc.}}; Κάσσανδρος τῶν Ἀπολλωνίου στρατιωτῶν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 301.1 (3rd c.BC).}}
**II** **water-lettuce** (σ. ἔνυδρος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.131)}}, **Pistia Stratiotes**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Meno Medicus “Iatrika” 6.22, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.101}}; σ. χιλιόφυλλος, **Achillea Millefolium, yarrow** or **milfoil**, {{Refs|[prev. work]102.}}

4760:
** στρᾰτόπεδ-ον**, τό,
**camp, encampment**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author = 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus] 5.63, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 79 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 10, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.709}}; Στρατόπεδα, name of a part of Egypt, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.154, compare 112}}: hence, **encamped army**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.114, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.119, etc.; in both senses, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.81.}}
**2** at Rome, the **Castra Praetoriana**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 60.1, +others}}
**II** generally, **army**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.77, 9.51, 53}}; also, **squadron** of ships, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 8.94, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.117, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 21.6, “IG” 12.105.29; σ. ναυτικὰ καὶ πεζικά 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.3.18.}}
**II.2** the Roman **legion**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.16.2, “BGU” 362 xi 15 (3rd c.AD), 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 55.23, etc.}}
**III** **the court** or **suite** of the emperor or his representative, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Epistulae” 46.}}

4761:
** στρεβλ-όω**,
_future_ **-ώσω**{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Phocion” 35}}: _aorist_ ἐστρέβλωσα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.63}}:—**twist** or **strain tight**, ὄνοισι ξυλίνοισι τὰ ὅπλα **drawing** the cables **taut** with windlasses {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (στρέβλη 1), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.36}}; **screw up** the strings of an instrument, τὰς χορδὰς ἐπὶ τῶν κολλόπων στρεβλοῦν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 531b.}}
**II** **twist** or **wrench** a dislocated limb, with a view to setting it, σ. τὸν πόδα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.129}}; also of wrestlers, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 2.6, compare 1.6}} (_passive_):—_middle_, ἐς τοὐπίσω τὰς χεῖρας σ. {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.43}}:—_passive_, στρεβλωθῆναι **acquire a squint**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Herodotus Medicus in “Rh.Mus.” 58.78; ἐσχηκότες στρεβλουμένους τοὺς ὀφθαλμούς [prev. passage]}}
**II.2** **stretch on the wheel** or **rack, to rack, torture**, applied to slaves for the purpose of extracting evidence, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 620, “Ra.” 620, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.32, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 2.89; στρεβλοῦν.. τοῦτον ὡς κατάσκοπον 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 277}}:—_passive_, ἐπὶ τροχοῦ στρεβλούμενος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 846, “Pl.” 875; ἀπέθανε στρεβλωθείς 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.54, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.44, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.312:}}_future_ _middle_ στρεβλώσομαι in _passive_ sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 361e.}}
**II.3** _metaphorically_, **pervert** or **distort** words, {{NT|NT.2Pet.3.16}}:—_passive_, {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.22.27.}}

4762:
** στρέφω**,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.323, etc.}}; _Doric dialect_ **στράφω** [ᾰ] {{Refs|“IG” 12(3).92.6 (Nisyrus, uncertain)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ **στροφῶ** (to be read στρόφω) {{Refs|“Etymologicum Magnum” 728.44}}: _Epic dialect_ _imperfect_
**A** στρέψασκον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.546}}: _future_ στρέψω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1152, etc.}}: _aorist 1_ ἔστρεψα {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tr.” 1243, etc.}}, _Epic dialect_ στρέψα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.520:}}_perfect_ ἔστροφα (ἀν-) [{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Cercidas Iambographus 17.30, (ἀντ-, variant{ἀν-}) Theognetus Comicus 1.8, (ἐπ-) 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.110.6, (μετ-) 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 1.435 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}]; also ἔστρᾰφα (κατ-) {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 23.11.2}} codices:—_middle_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.488, etc.}}: _future_ στρέψομαι {{Refs|6.516, etc.}}: _aorist_ ἐστρεψάμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1416, (κατ-) 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.94, etc.:}}_perfect_ _passive_ (in _middle_ sense) ἔστραμμαι (κατ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.21}}:—_passive_, _future_ στρᾰφήσομαι {{LXX+5th c.BC+|LXX.1Ki.10.6, (ἀνα-) 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.64, (δια-) 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 175, “Av.” 177, (μετα-) 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 518d}}; _future_ _middle_ (in _passive_ sense) στρέψομαι (ἀπο-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.5.36:}}_aorist 1_ ἐστρέφθην frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.40, +others}}, rare in _Attic dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 1128, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 273e}}; _Doric dialect_ ἐστράφθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 88, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.132, also variant (for κατεστράφησαν) in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.130 (but στραφῆναι [prev. author] 3.129):}}_aorist 2_ ἐστράφην [ᾰ] {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 37.6, always in Trag., 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 315, etc.}}, frequently in _Attic dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 537 (μετα-), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.97 (κατα-), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 77b}}: _perfect_ ἔστραμμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Mercurium” 411, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 5, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.7.15, etc.; ἔστρεμμαι 4th c.BC: Eudoxus Astronomus “Ars astronomica” 12.10 (Papyrus), compare ἀποστρέφω, καταστρέφω}}:—**turn about** or **aside**, ἂψ δὲ θεοὶ οὖρον στρέψαν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.520}}; ἵππους σ. **turn** horses, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.168, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.205, etc.; σ. πηδάλιον 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 40; τὸν οἴακα 4th c.BC: Anaxandrides Comicus 4.5, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 482.4; σάκος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 575}}; of persons, ἡλίου πρὸς ἀντολὰς στρέψασα σαυτήν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 708; πρόσωπον πρὸς κασίγνητον στρέφε 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 457, compare “Hec.” 344; πάλιν στρέψεις κάρα [prev. author] “Med.” 1152; ὄμμα πανταχῇ στρέφων [prev. author] “IT” 68; σ. ἀνταυγεῖς κόρας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 902; σεαυτὸν εἰς πονηρὰ πράγματα [prev. author] “Nu.” 1455; πόλιν πρὸς κέρδος ἴδιον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 413; στρατὸν πρὸς ἀλκήν [prev. author] “Andr.” 1149}}; **wheel** soldiers **round**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 11.9}}; see below {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}
**A.2** **cause to rotate** as on an axis, κεραμικὴν γαῖαν σ., i.e. on the potter's wheel, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sannyrio Comicus 4; τὸν ἄτρακτον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.12; τὸν κόσμον μήτε αὐτὸν στρέφειν ἑαυτόν, μήτε.. ὑπὸ θεοῦ στρέφεσθαι διττὰς περιαγωγάς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 269e, compare “Epin.” 977b.}}
**A.II** πάντ᾽ ἄνω τε καὶ κάτω σ. **turn** upside down, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 651}}; κάτω σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 717, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 733; σ. λόγους ἄνω καὶ κάτω 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 511a, compare “Euthd.” 276d}}; ἄνω κάτω τοὺς νόμους σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.91; so δίκα καὶ πάντα πάλιν στρέφεται 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 411 (Lyric poetry)}}; στρέφειν alone, **overturn, upset**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “IT” 1166, “fragment” 536 (troch.)}}; γῆν σ. **turn** it **over** by digging or ploughing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 16.15: with _accusative_ _cognate_, πάσας σ. στροφάς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 43e}}; γράμματα πανταχῇ σ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cra.” 414c}}: with _infinitive_, **change** a thing **so as to.. **, εὔκλειαν ἔχειν βιοτὰν στρέψουσι φᾶμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 416 (Lyric poetry).}}
**A.III** σ. σφυρόν **sprain** or **dislocate** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Epictetus Philosophus “Enchiridion” 29.2, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.15.4 (so στραφῆναι τὸν πόδα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.129, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 789e).}}
**A.III.2** _metaphorically_ of pain, **twist, torture**, κακὸν στρέφει με περὶ τὴν γαστέρα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 177, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1131, “fragment” 462, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 2.44 (_passive_), 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 19.141; βρέμει ἡ κοιλίη καὶ στρέφει καὶ βορβορύζει 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἐντὸς παθῶν” 6: so σ. τὴν ψυχήν}} **torment**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 330e.}}
**A.III.3** of corruptions in Music, κάμπτων καὶ στρέφων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 145.15.}}
**A.IV** **twist, plait**, σπάρτα ἐστραμμένα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.7.15; ἐμβολάδην ἐστραμμέναι ἀλλήλῃσι “hymnus ad Mercurium” 411}}; **spin**, ὑπὸ μακρῷ λίνῳ στρεφομένη {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “JConf.” 7, compare 1; ἔστρεψεν Μοιρῶν μία νήματα “IG” 14.607i (from Caralis)}}; κρόκην σ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Fug.” 12: _metaphorically_, μεγάλας σ. περιόδους 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.235e.}}
**A.V** technical term of wrestlers, **twist** the adversary **back**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 3.155: _metaphorically_, ἔριδα σ. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 4.93.}}
**A.VI** _metaphorically_, **turn** a thing **over** in one's mind, τί στρέφω τάδ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 750; πρὸς ἀλλήλους 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 8; βουλὴν ἐν ἑαυτῷ 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 10.48; τὸ πρᾶγμα πανταχῇ στρέφων ἀγαγεῖν ἐπ᾽ ἐμέ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.116.}}
**A.VII** **return, give back**, ἀργύριά τισι {{NT|NT.Matt.27.3.}}
**A.VIII** **convert**, τὴν πέτραν εἰς λίμνας ὑδάτων {{LXX|LXX.Psa.113(114).8, compare 29(30).12, LXX.Exo.4.17}}; στραφήσῃ εἰς ἄνδρα ἄλλον{{LXX|LXX.1Ki.10.6}}; **transmute** metals,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Zos.Alch. p.195 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}
**A.IX** _falsa lectio_ for{τρέπω} in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 32.20.}}
**B** _passive_ and _middle_, **twist** or **turn oneself**, στρεφθείς **having turned face upward**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.435}}; **turn round** or **about, turn to and fro**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.40, 575, etc.; ἔν τε κύνεσσι κάπριος ἠὲ λέων στρέφεται 12.42}}; ἐστρέφετ᾽ ἔνθα καὶ ἔνθα, of one **tossing** in bed, {{Refs|24.5}}; τί δυσκολαίνεις καὶ στρέφει τὴν νύχθ᾽ ὅλη; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 36, compare 4th c.BC: Amphis Comicus 20.4}}; of patients, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 7.664.}}
**B.2** **turn to** or **from** an object, ἔμελλε στρέψεσθ᾽ ἐκ χώρης {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.516, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.352}}; στρεφθεὶς μετόπισθεν **turning** back, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.645}}; **return**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1648, “Ant.” 315, etc.; στραφέντες ἔφευγον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.63, “An.” 3.5.1}}; ποῖ στρέφε; **whither away?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 230, 610.}}
**B.3** of the heavenly bodies, **revolve, circle**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.274, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 40b}}; of the distaff, {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 617a}}; of a joint, ἐν ἄρθροις σ. κοτυληδών {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1495.}}
**B.II** **turn** or **twist about**, like a wrestler trying to elude his adversary: hence, in argument, **twist and turn, shuffle**, τί ταῦτα στρέφε; {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ach.” 385}}; τί δῆτα ἔχων στρέφε; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 236e, etc.}}; πάσας στροφὰς στρέφεσθαι **twist** every way, {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 405c, compare “Euthd.” 302b.}}
**B.II.2** **turn and change**, κἂν σοῦ στραφείη θυμός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1134}}; στρεφόμενα λέγων **things that tell both ways**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Ars Rhetorica” 8.15: with _genitive_ causae, τοῦ δὲ σοῦ ψόφου οὐκ ἂν στραφείην}} **I would** not **turn for** any noise of thine, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1117.}}
**B.III** **to be always engaged in** or **about**, ἐν τούτοις στρέφεται καὶ ἑλίττεται ἡ δόξα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 194b; περὶ τὸ αὐτὸ γένος στρέφεται ἡ σοφιστική 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1004b22, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2p.124S.}}
**B.III.2** generally, **to be** **at large, go about**, ἀνειμένη στρέφει {{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 516; ἐν κυσὶν.. ἐστράφην λύκος 6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 37.6; στρέφεσθαι περὶ τὰ δικαστήρια 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.139 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}; of things, **to be rife**, ταῦτα μὲν ἐν δήμῳ στρέφεται κακά {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 4.23.}}
**B.III.3** of places, τόποι ἐπὶ.. τὰς ἄρκτους ἐστραμμένοι **turned, lying** towards.., {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.15.8, etc.}}
**C** in strict _middle_ sense, **turn about with oneself, take back**, στράτευμ᾽ ἐς Ἄργος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1416.}}
**D** _intransitive_ in _active_, like _passive_, **turn about**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.544, 546, where, however, ζεύγεα may be supplied from 543, as may ὄϊς in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.528, and ἵππους in 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 7.18}}; of soldiers, **wheel about**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 4.3.26 and 32; στρέψαντες ἀπεχώρουν [prev. author] “Ages.” 2.3}}; ποῖ στροφαὶ.. μανιῶν στρέφουσ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ichn.” 224}}; τὸν στρέφοντα κύκλον ἡλίου **revolving**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “fragment” 738, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1154}}; στρέψαι δεῦρ᾽, of the Comic Chorus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 92}}; στρέψον τι, δούλη **withdraw** a little, {{NT+3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 1.8; ἔστρεψεν ὁ θεός NT.Act.7.42.}}

4764:
** στρῆνος**, ὁ,
{{NT+3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 438; but in “Anthologia Graeca” 7.686 (6th c.AD: Palladas Epigrammaticus), LXX and “NT” (see. below)}} εος, τό:— **insolence, arrogance**, {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.19.28.}}
**2** **wantonness,** {{NT|NT.Rev. 18.3, “Anthologia Graeca” [prev. cited]}}
**II** with _genitive_, **eager desire**, μόρου Lyc.[same place]

4765:
** στρουθ-ίον**, τό,
_diminutive_ of στρουθός {{NT+4th c.BC+|1, 4th c.BC: Anaxandrides Comicus 7, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 539b33, 613a29, LXX.Tob.2.10, LXX.Psa.10(11).1, +others, NT.Matt.10.29, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.435; τὸ σ. ἡ συκαλίς [prev. author] 15.882}}; **στρουθίν**, {{Refs|“Corpus Glossariorum Latinorum”}}
**II** στρούθιον, see at {στρούθειος} 111.

4766:
** στόρνῡμι**,
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 702 (_anapaest meter_)}}; _imperative_ στόρνυ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 844}}; _participle_ στορνύντες, στορνύντα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.54, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 902}}; _compound_ καστορνῦσα (={καταστ-}) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.32}}; also **στορνύω** (variant{στρωννύω}), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 295.4}}; **στρώννῡμι**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 909, “Comica Adespota” 1211}} (written with one ν in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 589.44 (5th c.BC: Magnes Comicus Mae., 2nd c.BC), but with two, [prev. work] 9)}}; also **στρωννύω**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 1.216 J., (ὑπο-) 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 2.48d}}: _imperfect_ ἐστρώννυον {{NT|NT.Matt.21.8}}: later **στορεννύω**, **στορέννυμι**, {{Refs|12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 748.31, 32}}; _present_ _participle_ στορεννύς (variant{στρωννύς}) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 877:}}_future_ στορῶ (παρα-) {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 481, (ὑπο-) 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 90.1; also στρώσω LXX.Isa.14.11, (ὑπο-) 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 59, 4th c.BC: Amphis Comicus 46}}; and στρωννύσω (ἐπι-) Ps.-{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Philopatr.” 24}}; _Doric dialect_ _infinitive_ στορεσεῖν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 6.33:}}_aorist_ ἐστόρεσα, _Epic dialect_ and Lyric poetry στόρεσα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.621, 660, +others, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 12.129, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 192 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.99; also ἔστρωσα [prev. author] 6.139, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 921}}: _perfect_ ἔστρωκα {{LXX|LXX.Pro.7.16}}: _pluperfect_ ἐστρώκειν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 4.16, (ὑπ-) 2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 34.2:}} —_middle_, στόρνῠμαι (ὑπο-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.8.16}}: _imperfect_ ἐστόρνυντο {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.33, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Aetia” 3.1.16}}: _future_ στρώσομαι {{LXX|LXX.Eze.27.30:}}_aorist_ ἐστορεσάμην, _Epic dialect_ στ-, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 13.33, 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.375, (ὑπ-) 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 1030; also ἐστρωσάμην 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 21.7:}} —_passive_, στρώννῠμαι (variant{στορέννυμαι}) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|Scholia 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.57d}}; ὑποστορένυσθαι is _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 22.5}}: _aorist_ ἐστορέσθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.787e, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 67.14, (κατ-) 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 19; ἐστορήθην 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}; ἐστρώθην (κατ-) {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 14.114:}}_perfect_ ἐστόρεσμαι (ὑπ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 6.10; ἔστρωμαι “hymnus ad Venerem” 158, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 380, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.34, etc.}}: _pluperfect_ ἐστόρεστο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 74.13, 4th c.AD: Himerius Sophista “Eclogae” 13.2; also ἔστρωτο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.155, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.193}}:—**spread** the clothes over a bed, λέχος στορέσαι **spread** or **make up** a bed, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.621, 660}}; so δέμνια, ῥῆγος σ., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.301, 13.73; δέμνια 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 902; κλίνην στρώσαντες 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.139; σ. τισὶ λέχος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 844; λέκτρα σοι ἀντὶ γάμων ἐπιτύμβια “Anthologia Graeca” 7.604 (Paul. Sil.)}} (also in _middle_, ἐστόρνυντο τὰ κλισμία {{Refs|Callias Epigrammaticus [same place])}}: _absolutely_, **make a bed**, χαμάδις στορέσας {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.599; στρῶσον ἡμῖν ἔνδον 3rd c.BC: Macho Comicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 13.581b, cf. NT.Act.9.34.}}
**b** generally, **spread, strew**, ἀνθρακιὴν σ. {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.213; φιτροὺς σ. καθύπερθεν ἐλαίης 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.405; [στιβάδας] εἰς ὁδόν NT.Mark.11.8}}: also in _middle_, frequently in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus, as 13.33, +others}}
**2** **spread smooth, level**, πόντον σ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.158, compare “Hymni Homerici” 33.15, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.57, etc.; τὸ κῦμα ἔστρωτο 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.193; στόρεσεν πόντον οὐρία 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 12.129; αἰθὴρ νήνεμος ἐστόρεσεν δίνας 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.1155}}; χρηστὴν ἡμῖν ἡ θάλαττα τὴν γαλήνην ἐστ. {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 1.1}}; _metaphorically_, **calm, soothe**, ἀτέραμνον στορέσας ὀργήν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 192 (_anapaest meter_); [φθόνου] στορεσθέντος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.787e.}}
**2.b** **level, lay low**, πλάτανον δαπέδοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.247 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus): _metaphorically_, Μήδων δύναμιν 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 90; λῆμα μὲν οὔπω στόρνυσι χρόνος τὸ σόν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 702 (_anapaest meter_); ἵνα Πελοποννησίων στορέσωμεν τὸ φρόνημα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.18.}}
**3** ὁδὸν σ. **pave** a road, {{Refs|“IGRom.” 4.1431.5, +others (from Smyrna), uncertain in “IG” 12(5).229.7 (from Paros)}}:—_passive_, ἐστρωμένη ὁδός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.138}}; ἔδαφος λίθων πλαξὶ λείαις ἐστρ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 12, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 67.14.}}
**II** **strew** or **spread with** a thing, μυρσίνῃσι τὴν ὁδόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.54, compare 8.99; πέδον πετάσμασιν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 909, compare 921}}; **saddle a horse, provide a mount**, τινι {{Refs 7th c.AD+|“POxy.” 138.22 (7th c.AD)}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 372b}}; of a room, **to be furnished with** στρώματα, {{NT|NT.Mark.14.15}}; πλοῖον.. ἐστρωμένον καὶ σεσανιδωμένον uncertain meaning in {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PLond.” 3.1164 (h) 7 (3rd c.AD). (Cf. Sanskrit}}**stṛṇómi, stṛṇā´mi** 'strew', Latin _sterno_, Engl. **strew.**)

4769:
** στῦλος**, ὁ
(_feminine_ at Epidaurus, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 42(1).102.66, +others(4th c.BC), but also _masculine_ there, [prev. work]109iii92 (3rd c.BC))}}, **pillar** (={κίων} _accusative_ to {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.544)}}, especially as a **support** or **bearing**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.169, “IG” [same places]; στέγης 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 898; δόμων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 50; σ. μονόλιθοι “BGU” 1713 (2nd-3rd c.AD)}}: _metaphorically_, σ... οἴκων εἰσὶ παῖδες ἄρσενες {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 57, cf. NT.Gal.2.9, NT.1Tim.3.15.}}
**2** σ. πυρός {{LXX+NT|LXX.Exo.13.21, NT.Rev.10.1.}}
**3** **wooden pole**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 203, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.22.4}}; [σκηνῆς] tent-**poles, uprights**, opposed to διατόναια, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 353.9 (3rd c.BC)}}; **plank**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 47.}}
**4** **stile** for writing on waxed tablets; wrongly used in this sense by Greek speakers at Alexandria and in the East _accusative_ to {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herophilus Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “Anatomy texts Ad” xiv (Arabic version, ii p.183 ed. M. Simon, Leipzig 1906)}}; compare στυλοειδής.

4771:
** σύ** [ῠ],
**thou**: _pronoun_ of the second _person_:—_Epic dialect_ _nominative_ **τύνη** [ῡ] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.485, +others}} (_Laconian dialect_ **τούνη** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ **σύ** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 16.6, 21.9}}; _Doric dialect_ **τύ** [ῠ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.85, 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 34, +others, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 3.33, etc.}}; _Boeotian dialect_ **τού** [short syllable] {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica “Supp.” 2.83, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 55.6}} (also τούν {{Refs|[prev. work] 50.27, 55.6): Nom.}}**σύ**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.31, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1035, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 29, etc.; _vocative_, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.193, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 165, “Pl.” 1069.}}—Gen. **σοῦ**, {{Refs|“Hymni Homerici” 29.4}}, elsewhere only _Attic dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 302, etc.}}; _enclitic_ σου, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 761, “OT” 538, etc.; never in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, who uses σεῦ, lines 3.206, +others, σέο [prev. work] 446, +others}} (also in Lyric poetry, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus (?)in “PLit.Lond.” 54, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 3.65), σεῖο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.137, +others; also σέθεν 1.180, +others}} (which also occurs in Lyric poetry, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 33, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 10.9, and Trag., 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 264, +others)}}, and as _enclitic_ σευ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.811, +others, σεο 1.396: 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus has only σέο 1.124}}, σεο (_enclitic_) {{Refs|[prev. work] 9, σεῦ [prev. passage], 3.42, 85, 7.38}} σευ (_enclitic_) {{Refs|3.36, 134, 7.49}}:—_Doric dialect_ τεῦ, τευ, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.19, 10.36, etc.}}; rarely τέο, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 17; lengthened τεοῦ 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 145, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 84, and τεοῖο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.37, 468}}; _Boeotian dialect_ τεῦς {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 24}}; _Doric dialect_ τεοῦς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 59; also τιοῦς 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 74.27; τεῦς 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.126}}; τοι variant in {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.25}}; _enclitic_ τεος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 83; Cretan dialect τέορ 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}; other _Doric dialect_ forms are τίω, τίως, both {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Rhinthon Comicus 13, τίος 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 75.24.}}—Dat. **σοί**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.158, 167, 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 88, 6th c.BC: Mimnermus Lyricus 8, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 7,99, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 3, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.42, 6.86.ά, 7.52}}, etc.; _Doric dialect_ τοί {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 86 (oxytone)}}; _Doric dialect_, Lesb., and _Ionic dialect_ _enclitic_ τοι{{Refs 8th c.BC+|Alem. 33, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Frr.” 2.2,8, 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 79, 6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 20, 6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 44, 75.3, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.76, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 10.104, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.115, 3.35, 63, 85; in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, Lesbian Lyric poetry, and _Ionic dialect_ Lyric poetry and Prose τοι is always _enclitic_, σοί never _enclitic_ (τοί and σοι are not found except σοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.359, 11.381, ς (οι) 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.170}}, and in codices of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.270, 9.55}}; rarer than τοι in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, 3.69, +others)}}; in _Attic dialect_ both σοί and σοι (_enclitic_) are used (σοί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 361, etc., σοι [prev. work] 87, etc.)}}, τοί and τοι are not used; σοι is never elided except in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.170, τοι is elided in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.60, 347, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 55, [prev. author] “Oxy.” 1788 “fragment” 15 ii 9, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 28.2}}; _Epic dialect_ and Lyric poetry also **τεΐν**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.201, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.619, epigram cited in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.60, 61, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 930}}; also **τίν** [ῐ], {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 16, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.29, 8.68 (uncertain reading)}}; **τίν** [ῑ], {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] “I.” 6(5).4, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.20 (τίν}} before a consonant, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).93); also τεΐ 7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 53; τίνη 3rd c.BC: Rhinthon Comicus 13.}}—Acc. **σέ**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.256, +others}}; _enclitic_ σε,{{Refs 7th c.BC+|1.26, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 1.2, “Supp.” 23.9, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.42, etc.}}; in late Gr. σέν, {{Refs|“Anatolian Studies” p.76}}; _Doric dialect_ τέ {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 52, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.48, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.5, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 114}}; τ᾽ variant (codex R) in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 779 (on the accent see 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 54.14, 83.4)}}; τρέ (to be read τϝέ) {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}; or (_enclitic_) τυ {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“IG” 42(1).121.69 (Epid., 4th c.BC), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1225, “Ach.” 730 (uncertain in “Ach.” 779), 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.56, 78, etc.:—also τίν 6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 4, 3rd c.BC: Cercidas Iambographus 7.6, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 11.39, 55, 68.}}
**2** in combination with γε, **σύ γε,** **σέ γε**, etc. (compare ἔγωγε), **thou at least, for thy part**, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} and _Attic dialect_; _Doric dialect_ τύγε [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus [272], 5th c.BC: Timocreon Lyricus 1 (variant{τύ γα}); τύγᾰ 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.69, 71}}]; _Boeotian dialect_ **τούγα** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 55.6}}: _dative_ **σοί γε** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.557}}: _accusative_ **σέ γε** {{Refs|10.96}}, etc.:—also **σύ περ** {{Refs|24.398.}}
**3** σύ with _infinitive_ (as _imperative_), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.134, 4.163.}}
**II** Dual _nominative_ and _accusative_ **σφῶϊ**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.336, 4.286, +others}}, **you two, both of you**; **σφώ** (not σφῴ,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|compare 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 85.17), 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.574, 11.782, 13.47, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 344, 1543, etc.}}—Gen. and Dat. **σφῶϊν**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.341, +others}}; _contraction_ σφῷν once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.62, and always in _Attic dialect_ (Hdn.Gr. 1.475) and Trag., e.g. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 12, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 342, “OT” 1495, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 892e (codices 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus have σφῶϊν in “Tht.” 193c, +others, σφῶν “Euthd.” 273e, +others)}}. None of these forms are _enclitic_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 38.9, 85.12; codex A of 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 658c, 673e, 689a wrongly makes σφῳν}} _enclitic_; Ζεὺς σφὼ is prescribed in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.146 by Hdn.Gr. 2.93.}} —σφῶϊ is never _dative_; in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.286}} it is the _accusative_ depending on κελεύ; σφῶϊν is never _accusative_; in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.52 it is dative commodi.}}
**III** Plur. _nominative_ **ὑμεῖς**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.75, +others (before a vowel, 4.246, 7.194, +others), 6th c.BC: Pherecydes Syrius Philosophus 11, 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 29a, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.72, etc.}}, **ye, you**; _Aeolic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ ὔμμες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.274, +others, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 24, 25}}; _Doric dialect_ ὑμές {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 60, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 760, 761, 862; ὑμέν “SIG” 685.118 (Crete, 2nd c.BC), “GDI” 5155.6 ([prev. passage], 2nd c.BC)}}; _Boeotian dialect_ οὐμές {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 6}}; a resolved form ὑμέες, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Parthenius Mythographus “fragment” 14, is a poeticlicence (so 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 93.3)}} rather than genuine Ionic {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(variant in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.22).}}— Gen. **ὑμῶν**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 143, etc.}}; ὑμέων (_disyllable_) [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.159, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.7, +others, 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 74.6 (ὑμῶν codices), 6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 11.5 (variant{ὑμῶν})}}]; ὑμέων also {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.73, 6.130, +others; as _trisyllable_, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 2.27; ὑμείων 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.348, 7.195, +others}}; _Doric dialect_ ὑμέων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 46}}; also ὑμῶν, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 95.23}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ὑμμέων {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 96}}; _Boeotian dialect_ οὐμίων {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Corinna Lyrica 22.}}—Dat. **ὑμῖν**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.46, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.126, etc.}}; _Ionic dialect_ _enclitic_ ὗμῐν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 97.28}} also _Doric dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 91}}; _Doric dialect_ (not _enclitic_) ὑμίν [ῐ] {{Refs|[prev. author] 92}}; ὑμίν [ῐ] also in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 864, 1242, “OT” 991, 1402, “Ant.” 308, “El.” 804, +others (but ὗμιν}} should perhaps be _restored_ where the sense needs an enclitic on the principle stated by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 35.6, 36.5, “Synt.” 130.23)}}; ὕμιν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|(as _enclitic_) is prescribed by Hdn.Gr. (2.124) in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.33, by “Etymologicum Magnum” 432.34 in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.376, 2.141, etc.:—_Aeolic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ ὔμμῐ, ὔμμῐν, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.316, 11.336, +others, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 328, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 14, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 26.9, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 11(10).17.}}—Acc. **ὑμᾶς**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 325}}, etc. (**-υ** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Argonautica” 820, variant in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 222}}; ὗμας or (more probably) ὕμας is required by the metre in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 9.9, 47.11)}}; _Ionic dialect_ ὑμέας (_disyllable_) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.198, +others}}; _enclitic_ ὕμεας (_disyllable_) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 2.60 (Papyrus)}}; ὑμέας also {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.126, +others}}; _Aeolic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ ὔμμε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.412, +others, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 6(5).19; also in 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 620 (trimeter), and 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 846 (Lyric poetry)}}; _Doric dialect_ ὑμέ {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 3, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 52, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 737, “Lys.” 1076, “SIG” 528.3 (Cretan dialogue, 3rd c.BC), 622 B 8 (Cretan, 2nd c.BC).}}—The _plural_ is sometimes used in addressing **one** person, when others are included in the speaker's thought, as {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.81, 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 89. (With σύ}} cf. Latin _tu_, Gothic _pu_; with τοι Sanskrit _genitive_ and _dative_ **te**; the origin of σφῶϊ is doubtful; with ὑμεῖς cf. Sanskrit _accusative_ _plural_ **yusmān.**)

4772:
** συγγέν-εια**, ἡ,
**kinship**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 492, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.65, etc.; πρὸς συγγενείας καὶ κηδεστίας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.21}}; ἡ πρώτη σ. primary **kinship**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Sent.V at.” 61}}: with _genitive_, **kin, relationship, with** or **to** another, ἡ τοῦ θεοῦ σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 322a}}; διὰ τὴν τοῦ Ἡρακλέους σ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ly.” 205c: with _dative_, κατὰ τὴν αὑτῶν ἑκατέροις σ}}. **with** either of them, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Plt.” 307d; σ. ἔχειν τινί [prev. work] 257d}}; ἡ πρὸς τοὺς παῖδας σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.18}}; ἡ πρὸς ἀλλήλους σ. {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.43, etc.}}: not properly applied to the relation of parents and children (see. συγγενής {{Refs 4th c.BC+|11.1 b), γένος γάρ, ἀλλ᾽ οὐχὶ συγγένεια, τοῦτ᾽ ἔστιν 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 8.33 (but compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.20).}}
**2** **ties of kindred, family connexion, family influence**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 491c, “Smp.” 178c}}; of the relation between Athens and Thebes, Decrees cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.186.}}
**3** _metaphorically_, κατὰ σ. τῆς μορφῆς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 539a22}}; ἡ πρὸς τὸ.. ἱερὸν πῦρ σ. [παντὸς πυρός] {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.702f; ὤτων καὶ γλώττης 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herc.” 5}}; of metals, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd-4th c.AD: Zosimus Alchemista p.197B.}}
**4** **congenital character** of disease, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 2.125.}}
**II** **kinsfolk, family**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 733 (troch.), 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 923.1}}; of a single **kinsman**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1233}}: collectively, **kinsfolk, kinsmen**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tr.” 754}}; ἡ Περικλέους ὅλη οἰκία ἢ ἄλλη σ. {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 472b, compare “Lg.” 627c, NT.Luke.1.61}}: _plural_, **families**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.87}}; ἡ σ. ἡ Ἀγανιτέων **clan**, {{Refs|“BCH” 46.397 (from Mylasa), compare “Michel” 476.9 (from Olymus).}}
**II.2** of animals, **kind, species**, παρὰ τὰς σ. συνδυάζεσθαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 566a26}}: generally, **kind, class**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] “APo.” 76a9, 3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.39.}}

4773:
** συγγεν-ής**, ές,
**congenital, inborn**, ἦθος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.13; εὐδοξία [prev. author] “N.” 3.40}}; σ. εἶδος,= φύσις, **character**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ χυμῶν” 1; νόσημα σ. ἐστί τινι [prev. author] “Prorrh.” 2.2; φόβος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 691}}; παύροις.. ἐστι συγγενὲς τόδε **natural** to them, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ag.” 832; ἡ τύχη προσγίγνεθ᾽ ἡμῖν σ. τῷ σώματι 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 10}}; πότμος σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 5.40}}; προϊδεῖν σ. οἷς ἕπεται who have the **natural** gift to foresee, {{Refs|[prev. work] 1.28}}; συγγενεῖς μῆνες my **connate** months, the months **of my natural life**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1082}}; σ. τρίχες the hair **born with one**, i.e. the hair of the head as opposed to to the beard, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 518a18, 584a24}}; σημεῖα σ. **birth**-marks, {{Refs|[prev. work] 585b31}}; δυνάμεις αἱ σ., opposed to αἱ ἔθει and αἱ μαθήσει, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Metaphorical” 1047b31}}; αὔξει τὸ σ. increases **its natural force**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “EN” 1119b9.}} _adverb_, -νῶς δύστηνος miserable **from his birth**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1293}}; see at {σύμφυτος}.
**II** **of the same kin, descent**, or **family, akin to**, τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.109, 3.2, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 229}}: _absolutely_, **akin, cognate**, θεός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 14; γυνή 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 887; χείρ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1387}}; συγγενέστατον φύσει πάντων **most nearly akin**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 11.17; σ. γάμος ἀνεψιῶν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 855}}; of animals, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 539a23, “GA” 747a31, +others: hence}}
**II.b** _substantive_, **kinsman, relative**, οὖσα σ. ἐκείνου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 618 (troch.)}}; τῆς ἐμῆς γυναικὸς ξυγγενεῖ (dual) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Av.” 368 (troch.); πρὸς σ. τε καὶ οἰκείους αὐτῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 378c; ἔργον εὑρεῖν συγγενῆ πένητός ἐστιν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 4; γάμει τὴν συγγενῆ [prev. author] 929: frequently in _plural_, οἱ σ}}. **kinsfolk, kinsmen**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.133, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.91, etc.}}; not properly applied to children (ἔκγονοι) in relation to their parents, and so opposed to ἔκγονοι in [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 8.30, see at {συγγένεια} 1 (but compare 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.17); τοῖς συγγενέσι τὰ τοῦ συγγενοῦς ψηφίζεσθαι 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 4.23.}}]
**II.c** τὸ σ.,= συγγένεια, **kindred, relationship**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 291 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1469, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.82, etc.}}; also, **the spirit of one's race**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 10.12, “N.” 6.8}}; εἰ τούτῳ προσήκει Λαΐῳ τι σ. if he had any **connexion** with him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 814}}; of tribes, κατὰ τὸ ξ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.95.}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_, **akin, cognate, of like kind**, τοὺς τρόπους οὐ συγγενής {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1280 (troch.), compare “Th.” 574}}; ξυγγενὴς ὁ κύσθος αὐτῆς θητέρᾳ (for τῷ τῆς ἑτέρας) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ach.” 789; frequently in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus, [ἡ ψυχὴ] σ. οὖσα τῷ θείῳ “R.” 611e; τῇ πολεμικῇ σ. ἡ πάλη “Lg.” 814d; τοῖς.. λόγοις τὴν αἰτίαν συγγενῆ δεῖ νομίζειν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 788b9, compare “Rh.” 1398a21 (_comparative_): rarely with _genitive_, νοῦς αἰτίας σ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 31a, compare “Phd.” 79d, “R.” 403a, 487a: _absolutely_, σ. τιμωρίαι}} **fitting, proper** punishments, [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 122 (but probably _falsa lectio_ for{εὐγ-})}}]; συγγενῆ **things of the same kind, homogeneous**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Posteriora” 76a1; τὰ σ. καὶ τὰ ὁμοειδῆ [prev. author] “Rh.” 1405a35; σ. τέχναι “Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 2.30}}; ἐν γαίῃ μὲν σῶμα τὸ σ. its **congener,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 9(1).882.7}} (Corcyra). _adverb_, συγγενῶς ἔρχεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 897c; σ. τρέχων Πλάτωνι 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 1 (codices 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius)}}; τὰ σ. εἰρημένα **to similar effect**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.92K.}}
**III** συγγενής represented a title bestowed at the Persian court by the king as a mark of honour, 'cousin', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.27, 2.2.31, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 16.50}}; also at the Ptolemaic and Seleucid courts, {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|“OGI” 104.2 (Delos, 2nd c.BC), +others, “BGU” 1741.12 (1st c.BC), LXX.1Mac.10.89}}; οἱ σ. τῶν κατοίκων ἱππέων probably a category of **nobles** among the κάτοικοι, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 61 (b). 79 (2nd c.BC)}}; συγγενεῖς κάτοικοι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 14.8 (2nd c.BC).}}
** συγγεν-εύς**, έως, ὁ,
= συγγενής, only in _dative_ _plural_, {{LXX+NT|“JHS” 22.358 (from Pisidia), LXX.1Mac.10.89 codex A, NT.Mark.6.4, NT.Luke.2.44}}.

4774:
** συγγνώμη**,
**fellow-feeling, forbearance, lenient judgement, allowance**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 997, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 107a, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1143a23, NT.1Cor.7.6. Phrases: a. συγγνώμην ἔχειν}} **judge kindly, excuse, pardon**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 661, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 668, etc.}}; τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.116, 155, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1319, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.29, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 88c, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.2.13, etc.; ἑαυτῷ κακῷ ὄντι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 391e}}; τινος for a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.86. γ, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 400, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 368, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 10.2, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 233c, etc.; περί τι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1143a22}}; followed by ὅτι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.13, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 472a}}; by εἰ.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 117, etc.}}; by _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1322; with _genitive_ _absolutely_, σ. ἔχε ἐμοῦ παρανοήσαντος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1479; so σ. ποιήσασθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.110; διδόναι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.35.2; νέμειν 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 2.27.4, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 2.50c; ἀπονέμειν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nigr.” 14: opposed to}}
**b** συγγνώμης τυγχάνειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.7.4, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.141, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.3}}; παρά τινος from a person, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 24.17, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.38, etc.}}; συγγνώμης τινός, ὑπέρ τινος τυχεῖν, for a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1326, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.271}}; ξυγγνώμην ἁμαρτεῖν.. λήψονται will be **pardoned for** offending, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.40; συγγνώμην αἰτεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 106b}}; σ. ἔχει calls for **for-bearance**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 328; ἔχοντάς τι ξυγγνώμης 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.44}}; ἐχέτω σ. **let it pass**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1118e.}}
**c** πολλὴ ἔκ γε ἐμεῦ ἐγίνετο σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.58}}; συγγνώμη τοι I **excuse** you, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.39, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.50 (both with _infinitive_): συγγνώμη [ἐστί]}}, with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, it is **excusable** that.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.61, 5.88, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.238}}; τὸ πεπεῖσθαι.. σ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Prooem.” 34}}: also with a _participle_, σ. [ἐστί τινι] πλοῦτον ἀγειρομένῳ {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.389 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus)}}; σ. [ἐστὶ] εἰ.., ἐὰν.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.32, 4.114, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Mi.” 372a.}}
**2** Rhet., **confession and avoidance**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ τῶν στάσεων” 2, +others}}

4775:
** συγκάθ-ημαι**,
_Ionic dialect_ **συγ-κάτημαι**, used as _perfect_ of συγκαθέζομαι, **sit as assessor with**, [τῷ Καίσαρι] Wilcken {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Chr.” 14 ii 5 (1st c.AD)}}; **live in the same quarters**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.68}}; of a number of persons, **sit together**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 811, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.7.21}}; especially of persons sitting to deliberate, **sit in conclave, meet in assembly**, ἐν τῇ Πυκνὶ.. πρόβατα -ήμενα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 32; ἐν συνεδρίῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.23; περὶ εἰρήνης 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.55: _absolutely_, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.115.}}
**II** **sink** or **subside together, settle down**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 16.4. 16; ἐς γόνυ συγκαθήμενος 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Pseudol.” 20.}}

4776:
** συγκαθ-ίζω**,
**make to sit together** or **in a body**, τὰ συνέδρια {{LXX|“Hellenica Oxyrhynchia” 11.4; τὸν λαόν LXX.Exo.18.13}}:—_middle_ or _passive_, **sit in conclave, meet for deliberation**, σ. τὸ δικαστήριον [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.35, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Prooemia” 23 (variant{-καθεζ-}).}}]
**II** _intransitive_ in _active_, **sit together**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 11.216.}}
**II.2** **settle down** in a boiling pot, {{Refs|“PHolm.” 19.8.}}
**III** **sit** or **settle down**, of quadrupeds that lie down by doubling their legs under them, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 498a9}}; σ. ἐπὶ τὰ ὄπισθεν{{LXX|[prev. work] 578a21, cf. LXX.Num.22.27}}; σῶμα συγκεκαθικός a **bent, stooping** figure, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physiognomonica” 807b5}}; of men, **crouch down**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aratus” 21}}; of women, Thessalus in {{Refs|“Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 8(3).147}}; also τὰ νέφη εἰς τὰ κοῖλα σ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Signis Tempestatum” 3.}}

4779:
** συγκᾰλέω**,
_future_ **-καλέω**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus “Epigrammata” 14.8}}, _Attic dialect_ **-καλῶ**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.46:}}
**1** **call to council, convoke, convene**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.55, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 517, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.206, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 201, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus [same place], etc.; τὰς γυναῖκας ἐπί τι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 22; σ. τινὰς παρεῖναι 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Vit.Auct.” 1}}:—_middle_, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.160, NT.Luke.9.1, etc.}}
**2** **invite with others to** a feast, σ. αὐτοῖς καὶ Ἀρτάβαζον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.4.1.}}

4780:
** συγ-κᾰλύπτω**,
**cover** or **veil completely**, σὺν δὲ νεφέεσσι κάλυψε γαῖαν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.293; σ. τι χρόνῳ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 872, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 452d; τὴν ἀλήθειαν 4th c.AD(?): Olympiodorus Alchemista p.70 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus; συγκαλύψαντές μου τὴν κεφαλήν “BGU” 1816.19 (1st c.BC)}}; ὁ συγκεκαλυμμένος πατήρ, with reference to a well-known fallacy {{Refs 4th c.BC+|compare (ἐγκαλύπτω 1), 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 9}}; ἐξάγει συγκεκαλυμμένην **muffled up**, {{LXX+1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Numa” 10, cf. LXX.Sus.39:—}}_middle_, _aorist_ συγκαλύψασθαι, **wrap oneself up, cover one's face**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.7.28, “Smp.” 1.14; -ψασθαι τὴν κεφαλήν “IG” 42(1).126.6 (Epid., 2nd c.AD).}}
**2** _intransitive_ in _active_, λόγος συγκαλύψας ἀχλύϊ {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 4.59b.}}

4782:
** συγκατα-βαίνω**,
_future_ -βήσομαι: _aorist_ -έβην:— **go** or **come down with**, σᾷ πτέρυγι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 505 (Lyric poetry); ἅμα τοῖς ᾠοῖς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 756a25}}; of curls, σ. ταῖς παρειαῖς {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Epistulae” 58.}}
**2** **go down together**, opposed to ἀνέρχομαι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 358b32}}; especially **to the sea-side**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.30; εἰς ὁμαλοὺς τόπους 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.39.12; ἀπὸ τοῦ λόφου 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Crassus” 31: _metaphorically_, σ. ταῖς ἡλικίαις ἐπὶ τὸν αὐτὸν καιρόν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1334b34, compare 1335a31.}}
**3** **come down to one's aid**, Ζεὺς.. Μοῖρά τε συγκατέβα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 1046 (Lyric poetry), compare “Ch.” 727 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**4** like Latin _descendere in arenam_, σ. εἰς κίνδυνον, εἰς πόλεμον, etc., {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.89.8, 5.66.7, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 12.30, etc.; εἰς παράταξιν [prev. author] 17.98.}}
**5** **come down to, agree to**, εἰς κρίσιν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.90.5.}}
**6** _metaphorically_, **let oneself down, submit to**, εἰς φόρους καὶ συνθήκας {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.45.4}}; σ. εἰς πᾶν **agree to** all conditions, {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.10.1}}: generally, **stoop, condescend**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 26.1.3; εἰς λοιδορίαν 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.383 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**7** **come down in one's price** or **demands**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.26.12. Cf. συγκαθίημι.}}

4783:
** συγκατά-θεσις**, εως, ἡ,
**approval, assent**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.58.11, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.210 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, Andronic.Rhod.p.577 M., “OGI” 484.32 (Pergam., 2nd c.AD), etc.}}; opposed to ἀντίφασις, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 18 (_plural_)}}; **agreement, concord**, {{NT|NT.2Cor.6.16}}; in legal sense, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 194.11 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}; **flattering assent**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 24.}}
**2** in Stoic philosophical, **assent given by the mind to its perceptions**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Zeno Citieus Stoicus 1.39, +others, compare 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 1.8.14, etc.}}; a term introduced into Latin by Cicero, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 40: compare συγκατατίθημι.}}
**3** Grammars, **affirmative**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Conjunctionibus;” 226.17, 2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 642.5}}; αἱ δύο ἀρνήσεις μίαν σ. ποιοῦσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1053.}}

4784:
** συγκατα-τίθημι**,
**deposit together** or **at the same time**, ἐμαυτὴν συγκατέθηκα τάφῳ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“epigram Gr.” 367 (from Cotiaeum), compare 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 8.157}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 6.32.}}
**2** _middle_, σ. τινὶ τὴν αὐτὴν δόξαν περί τινος (where δόξαν represents ψῆφον) **put down** the same vote or opinion **with** another, **agree entirely with** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 501c}}: with _dative_ only, **agree with, assent** or **conform to**, ταῖς δόξαις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Sent. Vat.” 29}}; τοῖς παρακαλουμένοις Philipp. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.166; ταῖς ἐπιβολαῖς σ. τινί 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.71.5, etc.: σ. ὅτι..}}**agree** that.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 116a11}}: _absolutely_, **assent**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Sphaerus Stoicus 1.141, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 38, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 49.12, “BSA” 18.140 (Beroea, 2nd c.AD), etc.: later in}}_active_, -θεῖναι εἴς τι **agree to**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “Vita Pythagorae - Porphyrii Opuscula” 61, 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de vita Pythagorica” 33.236: compare συγκατάθεσις.}}

4785:
** συγκατα-ψηφίζομαι**,
**condemn with** or **together**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Themistocles” 21.}}
**II** _passive_, **to be reckoned along with**, μετά τινων {{NT|NT.Act.1.26.}}

4786:
** συγκερ-άννῡμι**
or **συγκερ-νύω**, _poetry_ **συγκεράω** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 321}}:—_passive_, _future_ συγκρᾱθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 406:}}_aorist 1_ συνεκράθην [ᾱ], _Ionic dialect_ **-εκρήθην**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 1.32}}; also **-εκεράσθην** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 889c:}}_perfect_ συγκέκρᾱμαι (see. below): —**mix, blend with**, πολλὰ [ἑνί] or εἰς ἕν, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cra.” 424d, “Ti.” 68d}}; λύπῃ τὴν ἡδονὴν σ. **temper** pleasure **by a mixture** of pain, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] “Phlb.” 50a; τὸ πικρὸν μέλιτι “Anthologia Graeca” 12.154 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus).}}
**2** **mix together, commingle**, πολλά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 424e}}; τὸν πέμπτον [κύαθον] {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 12.168 (3rd c.BC: Posidippus Comicus); μέλος συγκεράσας τις ἐγχέοι “Anacreontea” 20.4}}; ἐξ ἀμφοτέρων σ. **make a mixture** of both, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 397c.}}
**3** **attemper, compose**, ὁ θεὸς -κέρασε τὸ σῶμα {{NT|NT.1Cor.12.24.}}
**II** more frequently in _passive_, **to be mixed** or **blended with, coalesce**, τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 68c; πρὸς ἄλληλα [prev. author] “R.” 618d.}}
**II.2** **to be commingled, blended**, τὰ παλαιὰ συγκεκρ. ἄλγη the old **commingled** woes, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 744: with _dative_, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Aetia” 3.1.75}}; παίδων ὅπως νῷν σπέρμα σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus [same place]; ὁμοῦ τό τε φαῦλον καὶ τὸ μέσον καὶ τὸ πάνυ ἀκριβῶς.. ξυγκραθέν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.18}}; τῇ τῶν ἐναντίων κράσει σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 889c; ἔκ τινων [prev. author] “Ti.” 37a; ἀπό τινων [prev. author] “Phd.” 59a; παιδεία εὐκαίρως συγκεκραμένη 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 61.43}}; συγκέκραται αὐτῶν ἡ φύσις, of the dog and fox, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 3.1.}}
**II.3** of friendships, **to be formed by close union**, φιλίαι μεγάλαι συνεκρήθησαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.152:}} —_middle_, πρός τινα φιλίην συγκεράσασθαι **form a close** friendship with any one, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] 7.151, compare 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.7}}; so τὸ ἔχθος τὸ ἐς Λακεδαιμονίους συγκεκρημένον (conjecture Reiske for συγκεκυρημένον) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.37.}}
**II.4** of persons, **to be closely attached to, be close friends with**, τοῖς ἡλικιώταις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.1.}}
**II.4.b** **to become closely acquainted with, become deeply involved in**, συγκέκραμαι δύᾳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1311 (Lyric poetry); πολυφόρῳ συγκέκραμαι δαίμονι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 853; πενία δὲ συγκραθεῖσα δυσσεβεῖ τρόπῳ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 944}}; οἴκτῳ τῷδε συγκεκραμένη **deeply affected** by.., [{{Refs|[prev. author] “Aj.” 895; for “Tr.” 662 (Lyric poetry), see at {πάγχριστος}.}}]
**II.5** of a wife, ἀξίοις γάμοις -κερασθεῖσα {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“IG” 5(2).268.32 (Mantinea, 1st c.BC),compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.768b.}}
**III** _middle_, **mix with** or **for oneself**, εἰς μίαν πάντα ἰδέαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 35a, compare 69d; σ. αἰσθήσεις νῷ [prev. author] “Lg.” 961e.}}

4787:
** συγκῑν-έω**,
**stir up** or **excite**, {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 15.17.1, NT.Act.6.12}}; **stir up** a mixture, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.1041}}:—_passive_, **move along with** or **together**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 113a30, “Pr.” 921b28, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.520, etc.; σ. κινήσεις ἀνελευθέρους 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.704d}}; τὸ συγκεκινημένον **sympathetic emotion**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 15.2; συγκεκ. λόγοι [prev. author] 29.2.}}
**II** apparently _intransitive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 949a19 (uncertain reading).}}

4788:
** συγ-κλείω**,
_future_ -κλείσω: _Ionic dialect_ **συγ-κληΐω**, fut -κληΐσω: old _Attic dialect_ **ξυγκλήω**, _future_ -κλῄσω: _Epic dialect_ _aorist_ συνεκλήϊσσα {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 48.309:}} —_passive_, _aorist_ συνεκλείσθην, old _Attic dialect_ ξυνεκλῄσθην: _perfect_ συγκέκλειμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.68}}, but **-εισμαι** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 670, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 15.63, variant in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 487}}; old _Attic dialect_ ξυνκέκλῃμαι, _Ionic dialect_ συγκεκλήιμαι (see. below):—**shut** or **coop up, hem in, enclose**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.157, 7.41; ξ. τὴν ἐκκλησίαν ἐς τὸν Κολωνόν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.67}}; πρὶν συγκλεῖσαι (i.e. τοὺς ἰχθῦς τοῖς δικτύοις) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 533b26; αἱ συγκλείουσαι πλευραὶ τὸ στῆθος [prev. author] “PA” 654b35; σ. τινὰς ἐντὸς τειχῶν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.17.8; εἰς πολιορκίαν [prev. author] 1.8.2 (_passive_)}}; σ. [θεοὺς] τῇ ὕλῃ **include** them in matter, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.426b}}; [ἡ πολεμία] δυνέκλῃε διὰ μέσου **shut off and intercepted** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.64}}:—_passive_, λίμνη συγκεκληιμένη πάντοθεν ὄρεσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.129; τὸ στόμα τῶν μητρέων ὑπὸ πιμελῆς -είεται 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 21; σ. εἰς στενὴν ἐντομήν 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.32}}; ξυγκεκλῃμένη πέπλοις **close muffled**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 487.}}
**2** generally, of straits or difficulties, τινὰ εἰς ἀγῶνα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.63.3; εἰς τὸν ἔσχατον καιρόν [prev. author] 11.2.10:}} —_passive_, συγκλείεσθαι ὑπὸ τῶν καιρῶν, τῶν πραγμάτων, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2. 60.4, 11.20.7}}; εἰς χαλεπὸν.. συγκεκλεισμένος βίον '**cabin'd, cribb'd, confined**', {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus [prev. cited]}}
**3** **pit against one another, set to fight as in the lists**, οἳ σὲ καὶ Ἑρμιόναν ἔριδι.. ξυνέκλῃσαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 122 (Lyric poetry).}}
**4** ὁ συγκλείων,= **smith**, {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.24.14:—}}_passive_, χρυσίον συγκεκλεισμένον{{LXX|LXX.3Ki.6.20.}}
**II** **shut close, close**, στόμα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 498; ὄμμα [prev. author] “Hec.” 430, “Ion” 241}}; [τὰ βλέφαρα] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.4.6 (_passive_)}}; ξ. τὰς πύλας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.67; τὰς θύρας 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.74; τὰς θυρίδας 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.578: _absolutely_, σύγκλῃε}} **shut the doors**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1096}}; σ. τὰ δικαστήρια **close** the courts, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Eq.” 1317; τὰ καπηλεῖα 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator “Fragmenta” 1.3}}; σ. τοὺς ὀφθαλμούς **close** them **up** by blows, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.8}}:—_passive_, τὸ δεσμωτήριον συνεκέκλειστο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.48 codices (συνεκέκλῃτο Sauppe)}}; of bivalve fish, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 528a16}}; of eyebrows, **come together**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τόπων τῶν κατὰ ἄνθρωπον” 3}}; of wounds, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus “Ther.” 2.}}
**II.2** _intransitive_ in _active_, ὥρας ἤδη συγκλειούσης as the season **was** now **closing in**, i.e. the days becoming shorter, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.7.3, compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 10.4; τοῦ καιροῦ συγκλείοντος εἰς χειμῶνα “GDI” 3087.19 (from Chersonesus).}}
**III** **close jointly**, συνανοιγόντων καὶ συγκλειόντων {{Refs|“IG” 12.91.17.}}
**IV** σ. τὰς ἀσπίδας **lock** their shields, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.33}}: hence, _absolutely_, **close up** the ranks, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.35}}; τὸ διάκενον καὶ οὐ ξυγκλῃσθέν **the part that was** not **closed up**, of a gap in the line, {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.72.}}
**IV.2** **connect closely together**, τὰ ἀνόμοια ἁρμονίᾳ συγκεκλεῖς θαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Philolaus Philosophus 6}}; ἐν ἄρθροις συγκεκλῃμένον καλῶς well **linked** or **compacted**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1300}}; ς. (i.e. τὴν πόλιν) εἰς ταὐτόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 117e, compare “Ti.” 76a, etc.; σ. τὴν ἀρχὴν τῶν ῥηθήσεσθαι μελλόντων τῇ τελευτῇ τῶν προειρημένων 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.24, compare 15.68 (_passive_)}}:—_passive_, συγκλεισθήσονται ταῖς τε ἐπιγαμίαις καὶ ἐγκτήσεσι παρ᾽ ἀλλήλοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.19.}}
**V** **conclude, complete**, λόγον, διάνοιαν, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 121.1, “Synt.” 66.8:}} —_passive_ {{Refs|[prev. work] 11.9.}}

4790:
** συγκοινων-έω**,
**have a joint share of**, τῆς δόξης ταύτης {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.2}}; **to be connected with**, τῆς κνήμης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 85}}; σ. τινί τινος **go shares with** one **in** a thing, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 162.5.}}
**2** in “NT” with _dative_, **take part in, have fellowship with**, ταῖς ἁμαρτίαις {{NT|NT.Rev.18.4; τοῖς ἔργοις NT.Eph.5.11; σ. μου τῇ θλίψει NT.Phil.4.14.}}

4792:
** συγκομ-ίζω**,
**carry** or **bring together, collect**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.21, 2.121.δ᾽, 9.80, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.85}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.94}}; **bring together to oneself, collect round one**, ἰατροὺς ἀρίστους πρὸς αὑτόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.2.24}}; συγκεκόμισθε κάλλιστον κτῆμα εἰς τὰς ψυχάς **you have stored up** in your souls,{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 1.5.12; ὀλίγα τῇ μνήμῃ 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nigr.” 10}}; σ. πρὸς ἐμαυτόν **concentrate** in myself, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.3.17:}} —_passive_, ἐκέατο ἁλέες συγκεκομισμένοι **heaped together**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.25: _metaphorically_, ἐνταῦθα γάρ μοι κεῖνα συγκομίζεται}} **are gained both at once**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 585.}}
**2** of the harvest, **gather in**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.8.3, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 5.68, etc.}}: frequently in _middle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.6.37, etc.:}} —_passive_, of the harvest, ὀργᾷ συγκομίζεσθαι it is ripe **for carrying**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.199, compare “PCair.Zen.” 225.9 (3rd c.BC), “PRev.Laws” 43.5 (3rd c.BC)}}; ἐλαχίστοις πόνοις συγκομίζεται **is got in.. **, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.36.}}
**II** **help in burying** or **cremating**, τόνδε τὸν νεκρὸν.. μὴ συγκομίζειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1048}}; ἔφθη τὸ σῶμα συγκομισθέν the body was already **cremated**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sulla” 38, compare “Ages.” 19.}}

4793:
** συγκρίνω** [ῑ],
**bring into combination** or **aggregation**, opposed to διακρίνω, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus cited in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 985a24, compare 984a10, 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus [245]}}; σ. [τὴν ὄψιν] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 67d, compare 5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 101c}}; τὰ συγκρινόμενα **bodies which are formed by combination**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Anaxagoras Philosophus 4, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 72c, “Prm.” 157a; συνεκρίθη, συνέστη 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 6.2.25}}; συγκρίνεσθαι εἰς ὕδωρ, of vapour, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 370a30, compare 350a13}}; ἐξ οὗ συνεκρίθη of which it **was formed,** {{Refs|Placit. 5.3.1(nisi to be read ἀπεκρίθη).}}
**2** **combine**, συγκρινόμενος τούτοις καὶ ὁ Ἑρμῆς {{Refs|“Cat.Codex Astr.” 2.164.}}
**II** **compare**, τι πρός τι {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1368a21, “Pol.” 1295a27, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 1.8.2, 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 109; κάλυκας βάτῳ “Anthologia Graeca” 12.204 (2nd c.AD: Strato Epigrammaticus); ἑαυτόν τινι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “C. Gracchus” 4, cf. NT.2Cor.10.12; σ. τι ἐκ παραθέσεως 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 12.9.1}}; σ. τὰ λεγόμενα **compare and examine** them, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 14.3.7, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1165a32}}; μή με τάφῳ σύγκρινε do not **measure, estimate** me by my tomb, {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 7.137}}:—_passive_, ὁ Ἐπικούρου βίος τοῖς τῶν ἄλλων -όμενος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Gnomologium Vaticanum - Kleine Schriften” 36}}:—_middle_, **measure oneself with another, strive** or **contend**, τινι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.14; εἰς ἅμιλλαν [prev. author] 1.58}}; a usage censured by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Sol.” 5, 13th-14th c.AD: Thomas Magister Grammaticus p.345 R.}}
**III** **interpret**, τὰ λεγόμενα {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 14.3.7, cf. NT.1Cor.2.13}}; especially σ. ἐνύπνια **interpret** dreams, {{LXX|LXX.Gen.40.8, +others}}
**IV** **decree**, ζημίας, ἧς ἂν ὁ στρατηγὸς συγκρίνῃ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 3p.69 (3rd c.BC),compare “PCair.Zen.” 355.102, +others (3rd c.BC)}}; **decide**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work] 371.14 (3rd c.BC)}}; τί ποιήσωσιν {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|LXX.Num.15.34; ἐν τόπῳ ὃν ἂν -κρίνῃ ὁ ἀρχιτέκτων “Inscription Prien.” 119.25 (1st c.BC); also εἰς ὃν ἂν σ. τ. ὁ ἀ. [prev. work]107.44 (2nd c.BC)}}; **give judgement**, with _infinitive_, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PEnteux.” 62.11 (3rd c.BC), “PFay.” 12.30 (2nd c.BC)}}:—_passive_, ἐπιτελέσαι καθότι συγκέκριται {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BGU” 1827.13 (1st c.BC)}}; τῶν συγκεκριμένων ἀπαιτεῖν α (ἔτους) ἐκφόριον **the lands for which it has been decided** to demand one year's rent, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 61 (b). 1 (2nd c.BC)}}; ἡ συγκριθησομένη τροφή **which shall be adjudged**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“OGI” 56.71 (Canopus, 3rd c.BC)}}; ὅσον ἂν συγκριθῇ ἱκανὸν εἶναι δίδοσθαι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Sammelb.” 7450.11 (3rd c.BC).}}

4794:
** συγκύπτω**,
**bend forwards, stoop and lay heads together**, παιδάρια συγκύψανθ᾽ ἅμα βληχᾶται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 570}}; σ. πρὸς ἀλλήλας, of mares, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 572a23: _metaphorically_, οἱ κακοῦντες τὰ κοινὰ συγκύψαντες ποιεῦσι}} they do it **in concert**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.82, compare 7.145; καὶ συγκύψαντες ἅπαντες γελῶσιν 5th c.BC: Phrynichus Comicus 3.6; τοῦτο δ᾽ εἰς ἕν ἐστι συγκεκυφός 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 854.}}
**2** **draw together**, ἢν συγκύπτῃ τὰ κέρατα τοῦ πλαισίου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.4.19, compare 21.}}
**II** **to be bowed down, bent double**, as under a burden, {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Luke.13.11, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 2.20; συγκεκῡφώς 4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 7.90b; σ. τῷ προσώπῳ LXX.Job.9.27}}; μελανίᾳ{{LXX|LXX.Sir.19.26.}}

4796:
** συγχαίρω**,
_aorist_ -εχάρην [ᾰ] {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 15.5.13, 30.18.1}}, _imperative_ **-χάρηθι**{{Refs|“Anacreontea” 31.30}}:—**rejoice with**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 793 (_anapaest meter_), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1317 (_anapaest meter_); χαῖρε.. καί σοι ξυγχαίρομεν ἡμεῖς [prev. author] “Eq.” 1333 (_anapaest meter_); σ. ἐπὶ τοῖς σοῖς ἀγαθοῖς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 11.12; σ. ἀγαθῷ γενομένῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epinomis” 988b: with _dative_ _person_, τὸν συναλγοῦντα καὶ σ. τῷ φίλῳ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1166a8; οὐ σ. οὐδὲ συναλγεῖν ἑαυτοῖς [prev. work] 1166b18}}: later in _middle_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“IG” 14.966.5 (Rome, 2nd c.AD).}}
**II** **wish** one **joy, congratulate**, σ. τῶν γεγενημένων **wish** one **joy of.. **, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 15.15; σ. τῇ συγκλήτῳ ἐπὶ τοῖς γεγονόσιν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 30.18.1, compare “SIG” 700.41 (Lete, 2nd c.BC)}}; σ. τῇ πόλει ὅτι.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.45.}}

4797:
** συγχέω**:
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} uses _present_ and _imperfect_ _active_ and _2nd pers._ _singular_ _aorist_ σύγχεᾰς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.366, but more frequently _Epic dialect_}}_aorist_ συνέχευα, _infinitive_ συγχεῦαι, _aorist_ _passive_ σύγχῠτο:—_aorist_ _passive_ -εχύθην [ῠ], for which -εχέθην is _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus 1.7.2, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMar.” 9.2}}:—**pour together, commingle, confound**, συνέχευε ποσὶν καὶ χερσὶν [τὰ ἀθύρματα] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.364; τὰ διακεκριμένα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 46e; ἄνω κάτω τὰ πάντα σ. ὁμοῦ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 349}}; σ. τὰς ψήφους **mix** them **up**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 5.18; τὰ σύμβολα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.173; τὰς τάξεις 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.40.13}}; τὰς ὄψεις, of lightning, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 1.118}}:—_passive_, ἡνία δέ σφι σύγχυτο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.471}}; μεταλλεῖα συγκεχυμένα **in confusion**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 678d; τὴν κρόκην καὶ τοὺς στήμονας συγκεχυμένους διακρίνομεν [prev. author] “Cra.” 388b.}}
**2** **obliterate, demolish**, σ. τοὺς τάφους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.127; τὴν ὁδόν [prev. author] 7.115; δῶμα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 615.}}
**3** **confuse, blur**, τὰ γράμματα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “IA” 37 (_anapaest meter_), compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 721b34 (_passive_), “Aud.” 801b18 (_passive_)}}; συγκεχυμένον μέλαν an **indistinct** black mark, {{Refs|[prev. author] “HA” 585b34}}; φωνὴ σ. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.8}}; πλαδαρὰ καὶ σ. σάρξ flabby and **ill-defined** flesh, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD(?): Theon Rhetor cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.96}}; συνεκέχυτο δ᾽ ἔτι τοῦτο **was** still **confused**, not yet distinguished, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.30, compare 713.}}
**II** of the mind, **confound, trouble**, μή μοι σύγχει θυμόν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.612, compare 13.808; σὺν δὲ γέροντι νόος χύτο 24.358; συνεχέοντο αἱ γνῶμαι τῶν φαμένων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.142; ὁ βίος δι᾽ ἀπιστίαν συγχυθήσεται 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Gnomologium Vaticanum - Kleine Schriften” 57}}: with the person as object, ἄνδρα γε συγχεῦαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.139, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.99:}} —_passive_, τί συγχυθεῖσ᾽ ἕστηκα; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1005; μὴ ἀθυμείτω τις, ἐὰν συγχέηται 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.584.}}
**II.2** **confound, make of none effect**, πολὺν κάματον καὶ ὸϊζὺν σύγχεας Ἀργείων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.366, compare 473; τὴν πάρος σ. χάριν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1229}}; especially of contracts, engagements, etc., **make of none effect, frustrate, violate**, ἐπεὶ σύν γ᾽ ὅρκι᾽ ἔχευαν Τρῶες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.269, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 379e, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Jusj.”, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1063; τὰ πάντων ἀνθρώπων νόμιμα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.136, compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.1.2, “SIG” 45.33 (Halic., 5th c.BC)}}; τὴν πολιτείαν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.91; ἁλίαν “Schwyzer” 323 D 28 (Delph., 4th c.BC)}}; συγκέχυκε νῦν τὴν πίστιν ὁ καθ᾽ ἡμᾶς βίος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 781, compare “OGI” 669.18 (Egypt, 1st c.AD)}}; ξυνουσίαν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Bis Acc.” 17:}} —_passive_, λέλυται πάντα, συγκέχυται {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.25.}}
**III** πόλεμον σ. **stir up** a war, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.10.3, 15.2.4, 28.17.6.}}

4798:
** συγχράομαι**,
**make use of, avail oneself of**, τῇ συμμαχίᾳ τοῖς καιροῖς, etc., {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.8.1, 18.51.6, etc.; πρὸς τὴν ἀδικίαν ταῖς ναυσί [prev. author] 4.6.2}}; συναγωνιστῇ τῷ ποταμῷ as a coadjutor, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.14.5; τῇ ἀπὸ τῶν φίλων εὐνοίᾳ “SIG” 685.45 (Crete, 2nd c.BC)}}; πόρῳ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“OGI” 544.23 (Ancyra, 2nd c.AD)}}; of commercial dealings, σ. τῇ νήσῳ {{Refs|“Periplus Maris Rubri [1st c.AD]” 31}}: generally, **have dealings, associate with**, Σαμαρείταις {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.John.4.9, compare 2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus “fragment” 64.}}
**II** **borrow jointly**, τινῶν τριήρεις {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.20.14}}:— _passive_, σιτικὰ τὰ συγχρησθέντα {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 2p.64 (3rd c.BC,compare “Arch.Papyrus” 3.518).}}

4799:
** σύγ-χῠσις**, εως, ἡ,
(συγχέω) **mixture, confusion, confounding**, ἡ τῶν ἄλλων (variant{ὅλων}) σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 6.3.1}}; of Babel, {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|LXX.Gen.11.9; σ. ποιήσασθαι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 30.22.7}}; σ. λαβεῖν **to be commingled**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.990a; σ. ὅρων [prev. work]122c}} ς. **litterularum**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 6.9.1}}; **political confusion**, σ. τῆς πολιτείας{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. work] 7.8.4, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 14.5.8.}}
**b** **formation of a compound**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.153, +others}}
**2** **confusion, ruin**, βίου, δόμων, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 291 (Lyric poetry), 959; σ. τοῦ κατὰ φύσιν ἡ νόσος 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 5.8.1}}; σ. θανάτου μεγάλη 'indiscriminate mortality' {{LXX+4th c.BC+|LXX.1Ki.5.6; σ. λήψεται 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 300.}}
**3** Grammars, of composition, **confusion, indistinctness**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 12.15, “Synt.” 24.18}}; opposed to εὐκρίνεια, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ ἰδεῶν” 1.4.}}
**4** **an injury to the eye, syncopated hysis**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.12, “Eup.” 1.33, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 14.776, 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 7.58.}}
**II** of persons, **confusion**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nigr.” 35}}; σ. ἔχοντες **confounded**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1128; σ. ὀμματίων “Anthologia Graeca” 5.129 (1st c.AD(?): Maecius Epigrammaticus).}}
**III** of contracts and the like, **violation**, τῶν σπονδῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.146, 5.26; νόμων 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.114 (_plural_); σ. ὁρκίων 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alcibiades” 14}}; τὴν τῶν ὅρκων καὶ σπονδῶν σ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 379e.}}
**III.2** **confusion**, {{NT+2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 684.7 (Dyme, 2nd c.BC), NT.Act.19.29.}}
